{"parentUuid":"0251e684-7949-44f4-a682-c44410e14d11","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:startup","toolUseID":"f82bf94f-d170-4143-99b8-d51ce176ee5c","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"","stdout":"{\"hookSpecificOutput\": {\"hookEventName\": \"SessionStart\", \"additionalContext\": \"## Today\\nSunday, September 13, 2026\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Knowledge Base\\n\\n276 compiled articles (205 concepts, 70 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here \\u2014 read it only when a question actually needs it:\\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \\\"<query>\\\" --top-k 5`\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Recent Daily Log\\n\\n- Re-run the review gate after amending a plan \\u2014 the prior approval is stale. Codex found 5 new bugs in the amendment itself.\\n- `dcg` blocks `mv` with variable paths; Python with explicit containment checks is the workaround.\\n- Directory counts can mismatch because `ls` omits hidden files (209 vs 208 was a hidden `.gitignore`).\\n- A second reviewer with a different provider catches different classes of error \\u2014 the provider switch wasn't just a fallback, it found the plan's biggest error.\\n\\n**Action Items:**\\n- Fix the three round-10 findings: make the backup report a failed commit, have the sync unstage third-party links, correct `.gitignore` \\u2192 `.git/info/exclude` wording, handle the `staff`-group inheritance in the Pi boundary step.\\n- Re-run the review gate, then start Tasks 3\\u20138.\\n- Windows: separately install the vendored package that provides `claudex-loop`, `claudex-route`, `codex-build`, `codex-review` (currently dead symlinks there).\\n- Task 2 result to record: global skills pool pruned 306 \\u2192 208; 61 skills still resolve; ~150 third-party installs untouched.\\n\\n### Session (23:30)\\n\\n**Context:** Ran `/backup` (manual catch-up path for the KeystoneOS daily backup).\\n\\n**Key Exchanges:**\\n- First run failed; second run succeeded after a fix. Final state: `status: OK`, 0 errors, `okCount: 80` (full count \\u2014 SSD capture included).\\n\\n**Decisions Made:**\\n- Renamed rather than deleted the stale SSD artifact: `/Volumes/CRASHBOX/AssistantBackup/DEV-nonrepo/LibraryProject/ZoteroInjestion/.git.stale-dir-2026-09-12` (139 MB). Reversible; safe to delete later since GitHub has the repo.\\n- Archived a copy of `.errors.log`, then emptied it so the session-start HOOK FAILURE warning stops.\\n\\n**Lessons Learned:**\\n- **Submodule conversion breaks rsync backups.** On 9/11 `ZoteroInjestion` became a submodule inside `LibraryProject`, so its `.git` changed from a directory to a file. rsync cannot overwrite an existing destination directory with a file, so every backup failed while CRASHBOX was mounted. Fix = remove/rename the stale `.git` directory on the SSD.\\n- A backup failure of this class only appears when the SSD is plugged in \\u2014 it is invisible on SSD-absent runs.\\n\\n**Action Items:**\\n- Backup manifest still classifies `LibraryProject` as non-repo (`DEV-nonrepo/`); it now is a git repo with a GitHub remote. Backups work either way \\u2014 Eric was asked whether to correct the entry; answer pending.\\n- Optional cleanup: delete `.git.stale-dir-2026-09-12` on CRASHBOX whenever convenient.\\n\"}}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"Loading knowledge base...","durationMs":183},"type":"attachment","uuid":"ab35a94c-e6b3-486a-8578-ad67b214ba0b","timestamp":"2026-09-13T07:32:11.824Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/global-memory","sessionId":"c922703c-e012-455a-8f07-d26099b07dc4","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"ab35a94c-e6b3-486a-8578-ad67b214ba0b","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:startup","toolUseID":"f82bf94f-d170-4143-99b8-d51ce176ee5c","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"","stdout":"{\n  \"hookSpecificOutput\": {\n    \"hookEventName\": \"SessionStart\",\n    \"additionalContext\": \"<EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>\\nYou have superpowers.\\n\\n**Below is the full content of your 'superpowers:using-superpowers' skill - your introduction to using skills. For all other skills, use the 'Skill' tool:**\\n\\n---\\nname: using-superpowers\\ndescription: Use when starting any conversation - establishes how to find and use skills, requiring skill invocation before ANY response including clarifying questions\\n---\\n\\n<SUBAGENT-STOP>\\nIf you were dispatched as a subagent to execute a specific task, ignore this skill.\\n</SUBAGENT-STOP>\\n\\n<EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\\nIf you think there is even a 1% chance a skill might apply to what you are doing, you ABSOLUTELY MUST invoke the skill.\\n\\nIF A SKILL APPLIES TO YOUR TASK, YOU DO NOT HAVE A CHOICE. YOU MUST USE IT.\\n\\nThis is not negotiable. You cannot rationalize your way out of this.\\n</EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\\n\\n## The Rule\\n\\n**Invoke relevant or requested skills BEFORE any response or action** — including clarifying questions, exploring the codebase, or checking files. If it turns out wrong for the situation, you don't have to use it.\\n\\n**Before entering plan mode:** if you haven't already brainstormed, invoke the brainstorming skill first.\\n\\nThen announce \\\"Using [skill] to [purpose]\\\" and follow the skill exactly. If it has a checklist, create a todo per item.\\n\\n## Skill Priority\\n\\nWhen multiple skills apply, process skills come first — they set the approach, then implementation skills (frontend-design, etc.) carry it out. Brainstorming and systematic-debugging are Superpowers' most common process skills, but the rule holds for any of them.\\n\\n- \\\"Let's build X\\\" → superpowers:brainstorming first, then implementation skills.\\n- \\\"Fix this bug\\\" → superpowers:systematic-debugging first, then domain skills.\\n\\n## Red Flags\\n\\nThese thoughts mean STOP—you're rationalizing:\\n\\n| Thought | Reality |\\n|---------|---------|\\n| \\\"This is just a simple question\\\" | Questions are tasks. Check for skills. |\\n| \\\"I need more context first\\\" | Skill check comes BEFORE clarifying questions. |\\n| \\\"Let me explore the codebase first\\\" | Skills tell you HOW to explore. Check first. |\\n| \\\"I can check git/files quickly\\\" | Files lack conversation context. Check for skills. |\\n| \\\"Let me gather information first\\\" | Skills tell you HOW to gather information. |\\n| \\\"This doesn't need a formal skill\\\" | If a skill exists, use it. |\\n| \\\"I remember this skill\\\" | Skills evolve. Read current version. |\\n| \\\"This doesn't count as a task\\\" | Action = task. Check for skills. |\\n| \\\"The skill is overkill\\\" | Simple things become complex. Use it. |\\n| \\\"I'll just do this one thing first\\\" | Check BEFORE doing anything. |\\n| \\\"This feels productive\\\" | Undisciplined action wastes time. Skills prevent this. |\\n| \\\"I know what that means\\\" | Knowing the concept ≠ using the skill. Invoke it. |\\n\\n## Platform Adaptation\\n\\nIf your harness appears here, read its reference file for special instructions:\\n\\n- Codex: `references/codex-tools.md`\\n- Pi: `references/pi-tools.md`\\n- Antigravity: `references/antigravity-tools.md`\\n- Hermes Agent: `references/hermes-tools.md`\\n\\n## User Instructions\\n\\nUser instructions (CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, GEMINI.md, etc, direct requests) take precedence over skills, which in turn override default behavior. Only skip skill workflows or instructions when your human partner has explicitly told you to.\\n</EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>\"\n  }\n}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"\"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/hooks/run-hook.cmd\" session-start","durationMs":229},"type":"attachment","uuid":"d6168c86-7156-4106-b3bc-489d24e56a93","timestamp":"2026-09-13T07:32:11.866Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/global-memory","sessionId":"c922703c-e012-455a-8f07-d26099b07dc4","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"d6168c86-7156-4106-b3bc-489d24e56a93","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:startup","toolUseID":"f82bf94f-d170-4143-99b8-d51ce176ee5c","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"","stdout":"{\"hookSpecificOutput\":{\"hookEventName\":\"SessionStart\",\"additionalContext\":\"## User profile (global-memory/USER.md)\\n\\n<!-- Cap: 1,375 chars. Updated when the agent learns durable user preferences. -->\\n# User Profile\\n\\n## About\\nEric Rash — Director of Applied Performance, Baylor Athletics. Builds athlete performance data pipelines (Football GPS, force plate). Family: wife Kristina; kids Ames, Saylor, Rowan (5, 4, 10mo as of 2026-09-01). Career target: analytics-forward player-coach near Iowa, ~$90K+ floor, ~mid-2027. Deeper context: `~/DEV/ContextVault` — read before career/personal advice.\\n\\n## Preferences\\n- Python + R. **macOS since 2026-08-05**; Windows 11 box still ACTIVE (synced 9/9). Tool paths: see CLAUDE.md.\\n- Local SQLite/DuckDB; no cloud/servers/schedulers without Baylor IT approval. OneDrive project folders — beware DuckDB locks.\\n- Tangible working examples over documentation.\\n\\n## Working Style\\n- Direct; says \\\"stop\\\" when done. Wants to be challenged, not affirmed — push on vague or rehearsed reasoning (\\\"sharpened, not soothed\\\").\\n- Defers technical micro-decisions when convention exists — decide, state it, move on.\\n- Builds incrementally. Nothing that breaks mid-season.\\n- Authors own direction when leadership doesn't; OKR-driven change agent in a status-quo org.\\n- Right question over downstream craft; Christian faith is his organizing frame.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Working memory (global-memory/MEMORY.md)\\n\\n<!-- Cap: 2,500 chars. Working scratchpad; writes take effect next session. -->\\n# Working Memory\\n\\n## Active Threads\\n- **Personal site (9/2)**: `DEV/Website/scrollcraft/builds/eric-rash/`, pushed 9/9. NEXT: real-phone check; host or keep local. →Tier A personal-site-build-routes.\\n- **Baseball profiles (9/2)**: `DEV/Baseball_PerformanceProfiles`, Excel-only coach. 3 .xlsx mock-data prototypes. NEXT: coach picks one + real tests.\\n- **RTP CLA course (9/10)**: ingest + PDF rebuild DONE. NEXT: import .ris → Zotero; lesson 0002. →Tier A rtp-cla-course.\\n- **ATH Tech framework (8/14)**: shipped. NEXT: Micah mtg (need ITS intake path); inventory refresh due 9/29. →Tier A ath-tech-framework, tech-inventory-refresh.\\n- **Kinexon volleyball (8/1)**: API email→CSM, awaiting docs. Plan: sync→SQLite→dashboards; CSV interim.\\n- **Status report (9/7)**: #2 drafted, UNSENT — `SecondBrain/Inbox/status-reports/2026-09-07-email.txt`. NEXT: read-check, send, rename `-email-sent.txt`, flip `sent: true`. #3 = 9/21. →Tier A supervisor-log-pipeline.\\n- **Acro & Tumbling (9/8)**: owe an Excel \\\"pass builder\\\" — routines → cumulative impact load. 5-athlete pilot. Awaiting skill inventory + assignments.\\n- **Open PRs (9/7)**: AgentAssist #1 (draft), CFB_Analytics_Lab #1. Deferred: FA26 camp regex.\\n- **OKR/JD 2026 (7/17)**: Blake LEFT, AJ owns FB. →vault `Projects/Applied-Performance-JD-2026/`.\\n- Parked in Tier A: Fireflies key rotation; child dental.\\n\\n- **Skill audit (9/12)**: DONE. 160→61 global skills, 19.2k→6.4k tok/session. Plugins beat local copies. →Tier A skill-bloat-and-promotion\\n\\n## Environment Notes\\n- **Portfolio dashboard** LIVE; `/project` re-renders+republishes. →Tier A command-centre, portfolio-registry-write-provenance.\\n- **Terminal (9/4)**: tmux = default VS Code shell; `cc`/`cx` aliases. →vault `Knowledge/tooling/tmux-cheat-sheet.md`.\\n- Hooks exist in TWO copies (`.claude/hooks/`, `.codex/hooks/`) — patch both. →Tier A hook-two-copies-trap.\\n- `~/DEV` = THIN repo (`erash11/DEV`), 8 router `.md` only; `.gitignore` starts `/*` — never remove. `_ARCHIVE/` = parked. →Tier A github-consolidation.\\n- Schedules/gws limits →automation-cadence. CFB capture (Mon, 8/24–1/23) →cfb-forecast-capture.\\n\\n## Pending Decisions\\n- **DUE (was 8/31)**: OKR Q1 gate: AI integration (buy-in) + data governance (Anthony's proposal).\\n- **Kitman vs MyLink**: MyLink intros (~half Kitman's cost) + buy Kitman API access. Undecided; blocks priority 3.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## HOOK FAILURES — surface these to the user on the first response\\n\\n[2026-09-13T04:30:44.946298+00:00] compiler-stale-lock: reclaimed compile lock held 1550min by pid 47899 (compile.py --auto)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Cross-harness activity (KeystoneOS unified snapshot)\\n\\nNot loaded here — read it only if a question needs it.\\nPath: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS/outputs/dashboard/unified-snapshot.md` (5670 chars, updated 2026-09-12 13:35)\"}}","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"Loading unified cross-harness snapshot...","durationMs":262},"type":"attachment","uuid":"8f1da268-8a77-4e06-b691-05771508cbcf","timestamp":"2026-09-13T07:32:11.899Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/global-memory","sessionId":"c922703c-e012-455a-8f07-d26099b07dc4","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"8f1da268-8a77-4e06-b691-05771508cbcf","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:startup","toolUseID":"f82bf94f-d170-4143-99b8-d51ce176ee5c","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"/last30days: Ready to use. Run /last30days to get started — setup takes 30 seconds.\n  Research any topic across Reddit, HN, X, YouTube, Polymarket (last 30 days).\n\nReddit, Hacker News, Polymarket, and YouTube (yt-dlp detected) work out of the box.\nThe setup wizard can unlock X/Twitter and more.\n  Detected: yt-dlp is installed (YouTube transcripts ready, no setup needed).","stdout":"/last30days: Ready to use. Run /last30days to get started — setup takes 30 seconds.\n  Research any topic across Reddit, HN, X, YouTube, Polymarket (last 30 days).\n\nReddit, Hacker News, Polymarket, and YouTube (yt-dlp detected) work out of the box.\nThe setup wizard can unlock X/Twitter and more.\n  Detected: yt-dlp is installed (YouTube transcripts ready, no setup needed).\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"bash \"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/hooks/scripts/check-config.sh\"","durationMs":333},"type":"attachment","uuid":"735a8dd3-5bc4-4456-a6bd-6b2b91d1f297","timestamp":"2026-09-13T07:32:11.970Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/global-memory","sessionId":"c922703c-e012-455a-8f07-d26099b07dc4","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"735a8dd3-5bc4-4456-a6bd-6b2b91d1f297","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:startup","toolUseID":"f82bf94f-d170-4143-99b8-d51ce176ee5c","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"","stdout":"{\"hookSpecificOutput\":{\"hookEventName\":\"SessionStart\",\"additionalContext\":\"\\n<context_window_protection>\\n  <priority_instructions>\\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\\n  </priority_instructions>\\n\\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \\\"timeline\\\")\\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\\n       - Each command: {label: \\\"section header\\\", command: \\\"shell command\\\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\\\"q1\\\", \\\"q2\\\", ...])\\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\\n\\n  <when_not_to_use>\\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\\n  </when_not_to_use>\\n\\n  <file_writing_policy>\\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\\n  </file_writing_policy>\\n\\n  <output_constraints>\\n    <artifact_policy>\\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\\n    </artifact_policy>\\n  </output_constraints>\\n  <session_continuity>\\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\\n  </session_continuity>\\n\\n  <ctx_commands>\\n    \\\"ctx stats\\\" | \\\"ctx-stats\\\" | \\\"/ctx-stats\\\" | context savings question\\n    → Call stats MCP tool, display full output verbatim.\\n\\n    \\\"ctx doctor\\\" | \\\"ctx-doctor\\\" | \\\"/ctx-doctor\\\" | diagnose context-mode\\n    → Call doctor MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\\n\\n    \\\"ctx upgrade\\\" | \\\"ctx-upgrade\\\" | \\\"/ctx-upgrade\\\" | update context-mode\\n    → Call upgrade MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\\n\\n    \\\"ctx purge\\\" | \\\"ctx-purge\\\" | \\\"/ctx-purge\\\" | wipe/reset knowledge base\\n    → Call purge MCP tool with confirm: true. Warn: irreversible.\\n\\n    After /clear or /compact: knowledge base preserved. Tell user: \\\"context-mode knowledge base preserved. Use `ctx purge` to start fresh.\\\"\\n  </ctx_commands>\\n\\n</context_window_protection>\"}}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"node \"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/hooks/sessionstart.mjs\"","durationMs":365},"type":"attachment","uuid":"d55ebfb2-8f06-4a72-934f-5fc00671557e","timestamp":"2026-09-13T07:32:12.003Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/global-memory","sessionId":"c922703c-e012-455a-8f07-d26099b07dc4","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"d55ebfb2-8f06-4a72-934f-5fc00671557e","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_additional_context","content":["## Today\nSunday, September 13, 2026\n\n---\n\n## Knowledge Base\n\n276 compiled articles (205 concepts, 70 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here — read it only when a question actually needs it:\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \"<query>\" --top-k 5`\n\n---\n\n## Recent Daily Log\n\n- Re-run the review gate after amending a plan — the prior approval is stale. Codex found 5 new bugs in the amendment itself.\n- `dcg` blocks `mv` with variable paths; Python with explicit containment checks is the workaround.\n- Directory counts can mismatch because `ls` omits hidden files (209 vs 208 was a hidden `.gitignore`).\n- A second reviewer with a different provider catches different classes of error — the provider switch wasn't just a fallback, it found the plan's biggest error.\n\n**Action Items:**\n- Fix the three round-10 findings: make the backup report a failed commit, have the sync unstage third-party links, correct `.gitignore` → `.git/info/exclude` wording, handle the `staff`-group inheritance in the Pi boundary step.\n- Re-run the review gate, then start Tasks 3–8.\n- Windows: separately install the vendored package that provides `claudex-loop`, `claudex-route`, `codex-build`, `codex-review` (currently dead symlinks there).\n- Task 2 result to record: global skills pool pruned 306 → 208; 61 skills still resolve; ~150 third-party installs untouched.\n\n### Session (23:30)\n\n**Context:** Ran `/backup` (manual catch-up path for the KeystoneOS daily backup).\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- First run failed; second run succeeded after a fix. Final state: `status: OK`, 0 errors, `okCount: 80` (full count — SSD capture included).\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- Renamed rather than deleted the stale SSD artifact: `/Volumes/CRASHBOX/AssistantBackup/DEV-nonrepo/LibraryProject/ZoteroInjestion/.git.stale-dir-2026-09-12` (139 MB). Reversible; safe to delete later since GitHub has the repo.\n- Archived a copy of `.errors.log`, then emptied it so the session-start HOOK FAILURE warning stops.\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- **Submodule conversion breaks rsync backups.** On 9/11 `ZoteroInjestion` became a submodule inside `LibraryProject`, so its `.git` changed from a directory to a file. rsync cannot overwrite an existing destination directory with a file, so every backup failed while CRASHBOX was mounted. Fix = remove/rename the stale `.git` directory on the SSD.\n- A backup failure of this class only appears when the SSD is plugged in — it is invisible on SSD-absent runs.\n\n**Action Items:**\n- Backup manifest still classifies `LibraryProject` as non-repo (`DEV-nonrepo/`); it now is a git repo with a GitHub remote. Backups work either way — Eric was asked whether to correct the entry; answer pending.\n- Optional cleanup: delete `.git.stale-dir-2026-09-12` on CRASHBOX whenever convenient.\n","<EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>\nYou have superpowers.\n\n**Below is the full content of your 'superpowers:using-superpowers' skill - your introduction to using skills. For all other skills, use the 'Skill' tool:**\n\n---\nname: using-superpowers\ndescription: Use when starting any conversation - establishes how to find and use skills, requiring skill invocation before ANY response including clarifying questions\n---\n\n<SUBAGENT-STOP>\nIf you were dispatched as a subagent to execute a specific task, ignore this skill.\n</SUBAGENT-STOP>\n\n<EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\nIf you think there is even a 1% chance a skill might apply to what you are doing, you ABSOLUTELY MUST invoke the skill.\n\nIF A SKILL APPLIES TO YOUR TASK, YOU DO NOT HAVE A CHOICE. YOU MUST USE IT.\n\nThis is not negotiable. You cannot rationalize your way out of this.\n</EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\n\n## The Rule\n\n**Invoke relevant or requested skills BEFORE any response or action** — including clarifying questions, exploring the codebase, or checking files. If it turns out wrong for the situation, you don't have to use it.\n\n**Before entering plan mode:** if you haven't already brainstormed, invoke the brainstorming skill first.\n\nThen announce \"Using [skill] to [purpose]\" and follow the skill exactly. If it has a checklist, create a todo per item.\n\n## Skill Priority\n\nWhen multiple skills apply, process skills come first — they set the approach, then implementation skills (frontend-design, etc.) carry it out. Brainstorming and systematic-debugging are Superpowers' most common process skills, but the rule holds for any of them.\n\n- \"Let's build X\" → superpowers:brainstorming first, then implementation skills.\n- \"Fix this bug\" → superpowers:systematic-debugging first, then domain skills.\n\n## Red Flags\n\nThese thoughts mean STOP—you're rationalizing:\n\n| Thought | Reality |\n|---------|---------|\n| \"This is just a simple question\" | Questions are tasks. Check for skills. |\n| \"I need more context first\" | Skill check comes BEFORE clarifying questions. |\n| \"Let me explore the codebase first\" | Skills tell you HOW to explore. Check first. |\n| \"I can check git/files quickly\" | Files lack conversation context. Check for skills. |\n| \"Let me gather information first\" | Skills tell you HOW to gather information. |\n| \"This doesn't need a formal skill\" | If a skill exists, use it. |\n| \"I remember this skill\" | Skills evolve. Read current version. |\n| \"This doesn't count as a task\" | Action = task. Check for skills. |\n| \"The skill is overkill\" | Simple things become complex. Use it. |\n| \"I'll just do this one thing first\" | Check BEFORE doing anything. |\n| \"This feels productive\" | Undisciplined action wastes time. Skills prevent this. |\n| \"I know what that means\" | Knowing the concept ≠ using the skill. Invoke it. |\n\n## Platform Adaptation\n\nIf your harness appears here, read its reference file for special instructions:\n\n- Codex: `references/codex-tools.md`\n- Pi: `references/pi-tools.md`\n- Antigravity: `references/antigravity-tools.md`\n- Hermes Agent: `references/hermes-tools.md`\n\n## User Instructions\n\nUser instructions (CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, GEMINI.md, etc, direct requests) take precedence over skills, which in turn override default behavior. Only skip skill workflows or instructions when your human partner has explicitly told you to.\n</EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>","## User profile (global-memory/USER.md)\n\n<!-- Cap: 1,375 chars. Updated when the agent learns durable user preferences. -->\n# User Profile\n\n## About\nEric Rash — Director of Applied Performance, Baylor Athletics. Builds athlete performance data pipelines (Football GPS, force plate). Family: wife Kristina; kids Ames, Saylor, Rowan (5, 4, 10mo as of 2026-09-01). Career target: analytics-forward player-coach near Iowa, ~$90K+ floor, ~mid-2027. Deeper context: `~/DEV/ContextVault` — read before career/personal advice.\n\n## Preferences\n- Python + R. **macOS since 2026-08-05**; Windows 11 box still ACTIVE (synced 9/9). Tool paths: see CLAUDE.md.\n- Local SQLite/DuckDB; no cloud/servers/schedulers without Baylor IT approval. OneDrive project folders — beware DuckDB locks.\n- Tangible working examples over documentation.\n\n## Working Style\n- Direct; says \"stop\" when done. Wants to be challenged, not affirmed — push on vague or rehearsed reasoning (\"sharpened, not soothed\").\n- Defers technical micro-decisions when convention exists — decide, state it, move on.\n- Builds incrementally. Nothing that breaks mid-season.\n- Authors own direction when leadership doesn't; OKR-driven change agent in a status-quo org.\n- Right question over downstream craft; Christian faith is his organizing frame.\n\n---\n\n## Working memory (global-memory/MEMORY.md)\n\n<!-- Cap: 2,500 chars. Working scratchpad; writes take effect next session. -->\n# Working Memory\n\n## Active Threads\n- **Personal site (9/2)**: `DEV/Website/scrollcraft/builds/eric-rash/`, pushed 9/9. NEXT: real-phone check; host or keep local. →Tier A personal-site-build-routes.\n- **Baseball profiles (9/2)**: `DEV/Baseball_PerformanceProfiles`, Excel-only coach. 3 .xlsx mock-data prototypes. NEXT: coach picks one + real tests.\n- **RTP CLA course (9/10)**: ingest + PDF rebuild DONE. NEXT: import .ris → Zotero; lesson 0002. →Tier A rtp-cla-course.\n- **ATH Tech framework (8/14)**: shipped. NEXT: Micah mtg (need ITS intake path); inventory refresh due 9/29. →Tier A ath-tech-framework, tech-inventory-refresh.\n- **Kinexon volleyball (8/1)**: API email→CSM, awaiting docs. Plan: sync→SQLite→dashboards; CSV interim.\n- **Status report (9/7)**: #2 drafted, UNSENT — `SecondBrain/Inbox/status-reports/2026-09-07-email.txt`. NEXT: read-check, send, rename `-email-sent.txt`, flip `sent: true`. #3 = 9/21. →Tier A supervisor-log-pipeline.\n- **Acro & Tumbling (9/8)**: owe an Excel \"pass builder\" — routines → cumulative impact load. 5-athlete pilot. Awaiting skill inventory + assignments.\n- **Open PRs (9/7)**: AgentAssist #1 (draft), CFB_Analytics_Lab #1. Deferred: FA26 camp regex.\n- **OKR/JD 2026 (7/17)**: Blake LEFT, AJ owns FB. →vault `Projects/Applied-Performance-JD-2026/`.\n- Parked in Tier A: Fireflies key rotation; child dental.\n\n- **Skill audit (9/12)**: DONE. 160→61 global skills, 19.2k→6.4k tok/session. Plugins beat local copies. →Tier A skill-bloat-and-promotion\n\n## Environment Notes\n- **Portfolio dashboard** LIVE; `/project` re-renders+republishes. →Tier A command-centre, portfolio-registry-write-provenance.\n- **Terminal (9/4)**: tmux = default VS Code shell; `cc`/`cx` aliases. →vault `Knowledge/tooling/tmux-cheat-sheet.md`.\n- Hooks exist in TWO copies (`.claude/hooks/`, `.codex/hooks/`) — patch both. →Tier A hook-two-copies-trap.\n- `~/DEV` = THIN repo (`erash11/DEV`), 8 router `.md` only; `.gitignore` starts `/*` — never remove. `_ARCHIVE/` = parked. →Tier A github-consolidation.\n- Schedules/gws limits →automation-cadence. CFB capture (Mon, 8/24–1/23) →cfb-forecast-capture.\n\n## Pending Decisions\n- **DUE (was 8/31)**: OKR Q1 gate: AI integration (buy-in) + data governance (Anthony's proposal).\n- **Kitman vs MyLink**: MyLink intros (~half Kitman's cost) + buy Kitman API access. Undecided; blocks priority 3.\n\n---\n\n## HOOK FAILURES — surface these to the user on the first response\n\n[2026-09-13T04:30:44.946298+00:00] compiler-stale-lock: reclaimed compile lock held 1550min by pid 47899 (compile.py --auto)\n\n---\n\n## Cross-harness activity (KeystoneOS unified snapshot)\n\nNot loaded here — read it only if a question needs it.\nPath: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS/outputs/dashboard/unified-snapshot.md` (5670 chars, updated 2026-09-12 13:35)","\n<context_window_protection>\n  <priority_instructions>\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\n  </priority_instructions>\n\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \"timeline\")\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\n       - Each command: {label: \"section header\", command: \"shell command\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\"q1\", \"q2\", ...])\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\n\n  <when_not_to_use>\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\n  </when_not_to_use>\n\n  <file_writing_policy>\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\n  </file_writing_policy>\n\n  <output_constraints>\n    <artifact_policy>\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\n    </artifact_policy>\n  </output_constraints>\n  <session_continuity>\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\n  </session_continuity>\n\n  <ctx_commands>\n    \"ctx stats\" | \"ctx-stats\" | \"/ctx-stats\" | context savings question\n    → Call stats MCP tool, display full output verbatim.\n\n    \"ctx doctor\" | \"ctx-doctor\" | \"/ctx-doctor\" | diagnose context-mode\n    → Call doctor MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\n\n    \"ctx upgrade\" | \"ctx-upgrade\" | \"/ctx-upgrade\" | update context-mode\n    → Call upgrade MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\n\n    \"ctx purge\" | \"ctx-purge\" | \"/ctx-purge\" | wipe/reset knowledge base\n    → Call purge MCP tool with confirm: true. Warn: irreversible.\n\n    After /clear or /compact: knowledge base preserved. Tell user: \"context-mode knowledge base preserved. Use `ctx purge` to start fresh.\"\n  </ctx_commands>\n\n</context_window_protection>"],"hookName":"SessionStart","toolUseID":"SessionStart","hookEvent":"SessionStart"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"47b581f3-937a-49a4-873a-28b60a14c28c","timestamp":"2026-09-13T07:32:12.003Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/global-memory","sessionId":"c922703c-e012-455a-8f07-d26099b07dc4","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"47b581f3-937a-49a4-873a-28b60a14c28c","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"63e66813-b7dc-4003-8225-fbe2858e2717","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"Review this personal memory system for contradictions and redundancy.\n\nThese files are injected into an AI assistant at the start of every session, so a\nstale or conflicting statement here is acted on silently and repeatedly.\n\n## Memory files\n\n### global-memory/MEMORY.md\n\n<!-- Cap: 2,500 chars. Working scratchpad; writes take effect next session. -->\n# Working Memory\n\n## Active Threads\n- **Personal site (9/2)**: `DEV/Website/scrollcraft/builds/eric-rash/`, pushed 9/9. NEXT: real-phone check; host or keep local. →Tier A personal-site-build-routes.\n- **Baseball profiles (9/2)**: `DEV/Baseball_PerformanceProfiles`, Excel-only coach. 3 .xlsx mock-data prototypes. NEXT: coach picks one + real tests.\n- **RTP CLA course (9/10)**: ingest + PDF rebuild DONE. NEXT: import .ris → Zotero; lesson 0002. →Tier A rtp-cla-course.\n- **ATH Tech framework (8/14)**: shipped. NEXT: Micah mtg (need ITS intake path); inventory refresh due 9/29. →Tier A ath-tech-framework, tech-inventory-refresh.\n- **Kinexon volleyball (8/1)**: API email→CSM, awaiting docs. Plan: sync→SQLite→dashboards; CSV interim.\n- **Status report (9/7)**: #2 drafted, UNSENT — `SecondBrain/Inbox/status-reports/2026-09-07-email.txt`. NEXT: read-check, send, rename `-email-sent.txt`, flip `sent: true`. #3 = 9/21. →Tier A supervisor-log-pipeline.\n- **Acro & Tumbling (9/8)**: owe an Excel \"pass builder\" — routines → cumulative impact load. 5-athlete pilot. Awaiting skill inventory + assignments.\n- **Open PRs (9/7)**: AgentAssist #1 (draft), CFB_Analytics_Lab #1. Deferred: FA26 camp regex.\n- **OKR/JD 2026 (7/17)**: Blake LEFT, AJ owns FB. →vault `Projects/Applied-Performance-JD-2026/`.\n- Parked in Tier A: Fireflies key rotation; child dental.\n\n- **Skill audit (9/12)**: DONE. 160→61 global skills, 19.2k→6.4k tok/session. Plugins beat local copies. →Tier A skill-bloat-and-promotion\n\n## Environment Notes\n- **Portfolio dashboard** LIVE; `/project` re-renders+republishes. →Tier A command-centre, portfolio-registry-write-provenance.\n- **Terminal (9/4)**: tmux = default VS Code shell; `cc`/`cx` aliases. →vault `Knowledge/tooling/tmux-cheat-sheet.md`.\n- Hooks exist in TWO copies (`.claude/hooks/`, `.codex/hooks/`) — patch both. →Tier A hook-two-copies-trap.\n- `~/DEV` = THIN repo (`erash11/DEV`), 8 router `.md` only; `.gitignore` starts `/*` — never remove. `_ARCHIVE/` = parked. →Tier A github-consolidation.\n- Schedules/gws limits →automation-cadence. CFB capture (Mon, 8/24–1/23) →cfb-forecast-capture.\n\n## Pending Decisions\n- **DUE (was 8/31)**: OKR Q1 gate: AI integration (buy-in) + data governance (Anthony's proposal).\n- **Kitman vs MyLink**: MyLink intros (~half Kitman's cost) + buy Kitman API access. Undecided; blocks priority 3.\n\n\n---\n\n### global-memory/USER.md\n\n<!-- Cap: 1,375 chars. Updated when the agent learns durable user preferences. -->\n# User Profile\n\n## About\nEric Rash — Director of Applied Performance, Baylor Athletics. Builds athlete performance data pipelines (Football GPS, force plate). Family: wife Kristina; kids Ames, Saylor, Rowan (5, 4, 10mo as of 2026-09-01). Career target: analytics-forward player-coach near Iowa, ~$90K+ floor, ~mid-2027. Deeper context: `~/DEV/ContextVault` — read before career/personal advice.\n\n## Preferences\n- Python + R. **macOS since 2026-08-05**; Windows 11 box still ACTIVE (synced 9/9). Tool paths: see CLAUDE.md.\n- Local SQLite/DuckDB; no cloud/servers/schedulers without Baylor IT approval. OneDrive project folders — beware DuckDB locks.\n- Tangible working examples over documentation.\n\n## Working Style\n- Direct; says \"stop\" when done. Wants to be challenged, not affirmed — push on vague or rehearsed reasoning (\"sharpened, not soothed\").\n- Defers technical micro-decisions when convention exists — decide, state it, move on.\n- Builds incrementally. Nothing that breaks mid-season.\n- Authors own direction when leadership doesn't; OKR-driven change agent in a status-quo org.\n- Right question over downstream craft; Christian faith is his organizing frame.\n\n\n---\n\n### ~/feedback_daily-brief-and-execution-partner.md\n\n---\nname: daily-brief-and-execution-partner\ndescription: Eric wants Claude used as thought-partner + execution layer, and wants a daily morning on-ramp brief\nmetadata:\n  type: feedback\n---\n\nEric wants to lean on Claude more as both a **thought partner and an execution layer** — his self-identified bottleneck is distilling his many ideas into a few actionable steps and holding daily momentum (things fall through the cracks). He wants a **daily morning on-ramp brief**: yesterday's incompletes → today's must-dos → the week's big rocks.\n\n**Why:** 2026-06-02 end-of-day interview — frustrated by low progress on a Tuesday; named focus, organization, and clarity-of-outcomes as the gap. See vault notes [[the-daily-on-ramp]] and [[self-authored-direction]].\n\n**How to apply:**\n- When he idea-dumps, don't just discuss — distill into a short, ordered, actionable list and offer to drive execution.\n- Push for outcome clarity early (\"what does done look like?\") rather than activity.\n- Tie daily work up to weekly big rocks / the Applied Performance OKR cadence (weekly Thursday check-in). The daily brief is the daily tier beneath the weekly OKR check-in.\n- **The on-ramp mechanism is gone.** `morning-briefings/onramp.sh`, the Outlook\n  inbox triage, and the `morning-briefing` system were retired 2026-08-14: the\n  directory was deleted, the launchd job disabled, and Outlook auth destroyed.\n  Do not try to run them. The *preference* above still stands — the daily\n  distillation is now done in-session, not by a script.\n- His original constraint still applies: he chose \"Claude assembles it\" over a\n  full script but was explicit about token burn. Keep the daily pass cheap and\n  do not re-parse raw calendar/email JSON in-session.\n- The weekly tie-up is the Applied Performance OKR cadence. Use the v3 OKR doc\n  as the source, not the older cadence doc — see the DEV memory\n  `status-report-sourcing`.\n\n\n---\n\n### ~/feedback_defer_to_judgment.md\n\n---\nname: defer-to-judgment\ndescription: \"User delegates technical micro-decisions to me; don't bikeshed on tooling/version/path questions when an established convention exists in the project\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: feedback\n  originSessionId: 27df54a5-abaf-449b-8ec7-f36ba16bb6b5\n---\n\nWhen I asked Eric to choose between two equally-reasonable technical micro-decisions (e.g. module path style, Go toolchain version) on the Zotero CLI scaffolding, he said \"I defer to whatever you recommend. You're the expert here.\"\n\n**Why:** Eric is a sport scientist, not a Go/CLI ecosystem maintainer. Asking him to arbitrate decisions like \"bare module path vs github.com/user/repo\" or \"Go 1.26.3 vs whatever's installed\" wastes his time on choices he has no strong opinion about — and where I have all the context to choose well (e.g. matching the existing [[catapult-cli]] convention).\n\n**How to apply:**\n- For decisions that have a clear \"match existing convention\" answer, just make it and move on. Don't surface the micro-question.\n- Save the question form for genuine forks: scope (what to build), data sources, UX behavior, stack choices when no precedent exists.\n- The litmus test: would Eric have a domain-rooted opinion? If no, decide and ship. If yes (sport science domain, his research workflow, his existing tooling), ask.\n- After deciding silently, state the decision in one line (\"Matching catapult: bare module path, Go 1.26.3\") so he can override if he wants — don't bury it.\n\n\n---\n\n### ~/feedback_project_idea_capture.md\n\n---\nname: project-idea-capture\ndescription: Offer (never silently write) to file new project ideas/milestones to the portfolio inbox\nmetadata:\n  type: feedback\n---\n\nWhen a conversation clearly spawns a new project idea, or completes a milestone\non a tracked portfolio project, offer once: \"Want me to file that to your\nproject inbox / log it on <project>?\" On yes, use the /project skill verbs.\n\n**Why:** Eric's side-project ideas historically died on paper scraps; capture\nmust be frictionless but never silent.\n**How to apply:** Offer only when the signal is clear (an \"I should build…\" /\n\"new idea…\" statement or a completed deliverable on a tracked project). One\noffer, no nagging. Registry: `~/DEV/project-triage/portfolio.json` (the Windows path this file\ncarried until 2026-09-01 no longer exists). That file is also written outside\nsessions — see the DEV memory `portfolio-registry-write-provenance` before\nediting it by hand.\n\n\n---\n\n### ~/project_ai-integration-initiative.md\n\n---\nname: ai-integration-initiative\ndescription: Baylor Athletics AI adoption + data-platform initiative — Claude AI workflow automation (Hoff/Soto) and Databricks/Player360 evaluation\nmetadata:\n  type: project\n---\n\nBaylor Athletics **AI integration initiative** — two parallel threads Eric is driving to bring AI + a unified data platform into a lean, change-cautious department.\n\n**Thread A — Claude AI workflow automation** (`Hoff_Soto_AI_Discovery`, 2026-05-28):\n- Goal: automate content/workflow work (marketing, social, website, branding, agendas, email, slide decks). Claude preferred for content; ~4x output with style-trained models.\n- Eric's actions: send docs + prompt templates to the team; build reusable AI **\"skills\"** (agenda creation, branded slide-deck generation); run an internal **AI usage survey**; centralize brand guidelines/templates so AI has structured inputs; explore Baylor funding **enterprise AI licenses**; facilitate the next AI meeting to review progress.\n- Cody Hall = designated AI \"guinea pig\" (experiment + feedback, marketing/brand use cases).\n- Idea raised: internal **fan-profile data project** leveraging campus + athletics data for marketing/engagement.\n- Friction: institutional IT policies restrict AI tools; generational/cultural resistance — frame AI as a productivity tool, lean staffing makes it necessary.\n\n**Thread B — Databricks / Player360 data platform** (2026-05-29, 10am discovery call):\n- Player360 demo aggregates wearables, force plate, manual DEXA, PDFs/medical records, sports-perf DBs into a HIPAA-compliant Lakehouse; readiness scoring; AI video analytics for biomechanics (object/joint detection on broadcast footage); medical-doc extraction/sentiment.\n- Pain points: limited staff, siloed data, underused Kitman Labs AMS (storage limits).\n- Pricing: consumption-based (cost-control emphasized).\n- Next steps: Eric to quietly gauge internal colleague interest before going broad; Databricks to send customer stories/slides + run a **mid-June virtual PD session** for staff; discovery/workshop with department heads to prioritize POC use cases; follow-up virtual meeting ~Wed 3–4pm.\n\nRelated: [[athlete-data-governance]] (vendor/AI risk + compliance gating these tools), [[practice-load-framework]].\n\n\n---\n\n### ~/project_athlete-data-governance.md\n\n---\nname: athlete-data-governance\ndescription: Baylor Athletics athlete-data governance + AI/vendor-risk/compliance framework (external firm engagement, Anthony/Jonathan)\nmetadata:\n  type: project\n---\n\nBaylor Athletics **athlete-data governance** initiative — building a governance/compliance framework for athlete data + AI/vendor risk. The gating layer for adopting new tools (ties to [[ai-integration-initiative]]).\n\n**Source:** `Athlete_Data_Legal_Discussion` (2026-05-26) with an external advisory firm.\n\n**Scope / approach:**\n- Phased model: **discovery** (map Baylor's data flows, vendors, existing agreements) → draft **policies + SOPs** → **vendor risk assessments** → **training**.\n- >50 vendors to standardize contracts/data protocols. Compliance surface: FERPA, HIPAA, NCAA best practices, state + international AI laws.\n- **AI Risk Navigator** tool (Jonathan) evaluates AI-powered products specifically; non-AI tools assessed separately.\n- Policy must address data ownership + portability when athletes transfer institutions; consent management to streamline tech approvals.\n\n**Players:**\n- **Anthony** — to deliver a detailed **formal proposal** with phased engagement options + pricing; kicks off initial vendor/data-flow data gathering.\n- **Jonathan** — vendor risk assessments, AI Risk Navigator updates, policy/SOP/training drafting.\n- **Lisa** — coordinate campus partners (chief privacy/legal officer, athletics leadership) for review.\n- **John Allen** — key reviewer / decision-maker on tech deployments + data policy.\n- **Kenny** — operational + vendor-management input.\n- **Eric Rash** — follow up on proposal reception; facilitate communication across stakeholder groups.\n\n**Next step:** await Anthony's formal proposal, then route stakeholder review.\n\n\n---\n\n### ~/project_catapult_cli.md\n\n---\nname: catapult-cli\ndescription: \"Catapult Connect CLI built with Printing Press — key API discoveries, CLI location, and what works\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: 9ad4b586-a29e-48c0-be31-455d712183c5\n---\n\nCLI built and working at `~/printing-press/library/catapult` (binary: `catapult-pp-cli.exe`).\n\n**Why:** Sport scientist at Baylor Football needed programmatic access to GPS wearable data without R scripts or manual OpenField exports.\n\n## Critical API Discoveries (do not re-investigate)\n\n- **Correct base URL:** `https://connect-us.catapultsports.com/api/v6` (NOT v4 — v4 gives 404/405 on stats)\n- **Stats endpoint:** `POST /stats` (NOT `/statistics` — that path doesn't work)\n- **Auth:** Pre-generated JWT Bearer token from OpenField → Settings → API Tokens; set `CATAPULT_TOKEN` env var\n- **Token scope matters:** Token with scopes `[\"connect\"]` works. `[\"connect\",\"catapultr\",\"sensor-read-only\"]` may not access stats.\n- **Stats body:** `{\"filters\":[{\"name\":\"activity_id\",\"comparison\":\"=\",\"values\":[\"<uuid>\"]}],\"parameters\":[\"average_player_load\",...],\"group_by\":[\"athlete\"]}`\n- **Key slug:** `average_player_load` (NOT `total_player_load`) — v6 returns per-athlete session average\n- **Date format from API:** `MM/DD/YYYY` — must normalize to `YYYY-MM-DD` before SQLite date comparisons\n- **Statistics NOT bulk-syncable:** Must query per-activity; CLI uses Python bulk sync for historical data\n\n## What Works\n\n- `athletes`, `activities`, `parameters` — standard GET endpoints, work with any token\n- `stats --activity <uuid> --metric <slugs> --group-by athlete` — live data, caches to local store\n- `acwr --squad --metric average_player_load --date YYYY-MM-DD` — ACWR dashboard\n- `pb --metric max_vel` — personal bests with trend\n- `diff <activity1> <activity2> --metrics ...` — session comparison\n- `report --from ... --to ... --squad` — weekly load summary\n- `params search <keyword>` — FTS across 1,829 parameter slugs\n\n## Data Available (Baylor Football account)\n\n- 520 athletes, 2,522 activities, 1,829 parameters\n- Last 30 days synced: 15 activities, 869 statistics rows (Spring 2026)\n- Historical DB at `~/printing-press/.runstate/.../working/catapult-pp-cli/.env` (token file)\n\n## Known Nuances (from prior working system)\n\n- `max_vel` is in **mph** from API (multiply × 0.44704 to store as m/s)\n- `velocity_band3_total_distance` = HSD (75–90% max vel), bands 5–8 return 0 for Baylor\n- `average_player_load` slug = session average per athlete (misnamed; acts like total)\n- Walk-throughs have near-zero velocity — filter by activity name when computing speed metrics\n- ACWR values >3 at spring camp start are expected artifact (low chronic denominator)\n\nSuperseded by the `pp-catapult-openfield-connect` skill for day-to-day use, but this file's API discoveries below remain the ground truth for the endpoints — don't re-investigate them.\n\n\n---\n\n### ~/project_facecomps-practice-simulator.md\n\n---\nname: facecomps-practice-simulator\ndescription: FaceComps repo (Baylor Football Practice Load) + the practice load simulator Next.js app — Eric's \"face comps\" and \"practice simulator\" are this one project\nmetadata:\n  type: project\n---\n\n**FaceComps** = Eric's repo `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Football\\FaceComps` (\"Baylor Football Practice Load\"). His shorthand **\"face comps\"** = this folder; **\"practice simulator project\"** = the `simulator/` app inside it. Same effort — it's the *implementation* of the practice-load decisions from the meetings (see [[practice-load-framework]]). \"Faces\" = practice-script Excel files (periods + load) in `SourceFaces/`.\n\n**Layout:**\n- `simulator/` — **production Next.js app, deployed https://baylor-load-sim.vercel.app** (live tool coaches use). Classifies periods by **PlayerLoad (PL/player) → GREEN/YELLOW/RED**. Domains: `'2026 Spring'`, `'2026 Fall Camp'` (18 DEF faces seeded to prod). Data model (`simulator/lib/types.ts`): `Practice` (season, date, dress, tempo, emphasis, duration) + `Period` (minutes, pl_team_projected/actual). Stack: Next.js + Vercel + db/lib, vitest tests.\n- `SourceFaces/` — raw practice-face Excel (2025 Spring, 2026 Spring, 2026 Fall Camp, Fast Friday/MorphoCycle). `legacy/html/` — archived pre-rebuild artifacts (read-only). `SPEC_SHEET.md` — original load-math algorithm. `CLAUDE.md`/`CONTEXT.md` — project conventions.\n\n**Active work (ACTIVE as of 2026-06-16, resumed from the 5/29 pause):** planning & prescription layer (MorphoCycle + color/rep model) on top of the simulator. State tracked in `docs/practice-planning-grill-state.md`. Major decisions now LOCKED (resolves most of the old B1–B7 branches):\n- **Plan of record (6/15):** 14-day install structure (3/3/4/4) at the **Moderate** ramp — the recommended flavor of the v2 comparison matrix. Other 5 candidates (15-day; Conservative/Aggressive) kept as decision record only; coach-facing artifact is now the pruned **v3 deck** (`scripts/render_camp_models_v3.py`).\n- **Mock Game = Aug 29, controlled rehearsal** (6/15, Aranda staff-confirmed): game-week *operations* cadence, NOT game-week *collision* — anchors the taper, holds even if pushed to be \"competitive.\" Resolves B6 (MorphoCycle vs camp): mock/game week is morphocycle-shaped (day-of-week aligned); camp itself stays block-shaped, MC qualities don't tile across blocks.\n- **NCAA 5-day acclimatization locked as a hard constraint** (6/15): Aug 5 = practice day 1 (no pre-camp acclimatization). Days 1–2 helmets/spider pads only (GREEN ceiling); Days 3–5 shoulder pads max, never RED; full pads/live contact only from Day 6 (Aug 11).\n- **Mock-week sequencing refined 6/16** (AJ feedback): Tue→full pads (red, mimics in-season hardest day); Tue/Wed emphases flipped (Tue=red zone/short yardage, Wed=3rd down+tempo) so stimuli split not stack; \"coming out\" installed early (Mon); walk-through relabeled MD-2.\n- **Next deliverable:** day-by-day color/quality templates for the locked 14-day build (Aranda's late-June blue/green/yellow/red ask) — not the full candidate matrix anymore, since that's been pruned to one plan.\n\n**Glossary (use these terms):** MorphoCycle (game-week day-quality template: activation→collision→volume→speed→recovery), Block (3–4 day stretch between off-days; rep target unit), Rep Ramp (across-camp per-block rep progression ~88→124→152→180→taper, built backward from ~180 in-season weekly stimulus), Undulation (day-color variation within a block), Macro (camp calendar) vs Micro (per-day period template).\n\n**Baylor constraints:** indoor / no clear-sky → no yardage or max-velocity; S7 hardware (no auto-participation); reps counted manually (like K-State).\n\n**Feeder meetings:** PreSummer_Planning (5/22, Aranda/Viloria/Klanderman/Orloff), KSU_Planning_Brief_LYancy (5/26, K-State methodology), 2026-05-14 Applied_Planning (Viloria — plan-vs-actual, practice builder adopted), 2026-05-21 AJ & Coach Red (green 80–90 / yellow 90–105 / red 105–120 min; \"4 on, 1 off\"; mock game = green).\n\n\n---\n\n### ~/project_practice-load-framework.md\n\n---\nname: practice-load-framework\ndescription: Baylor football practice-load / \"practice simulator\" framework — color-coded day system + rep-count load model for fall camp\nmetadata:\n  type: project\n---\n\nBaylor football **practice-load framework** (likely what Eric calls the **\"practice simulator\" project**) — a system to standardize and simulate practice load to manage athlete stress/injury and maintain availability into fall camp.\n\n**Source meetings:** `PreSummer_Planning` (2026-05-22, with Dave Aranda + Vic Viloria) and `KSU_Planning_Brief_LYancy` (2026-05-26, K-State model via Lance Yancy).\n\n**Core design:**\n- Color-coded practice days: **blue / green / yellow / red** (red = max intensity, full pads, high physicality, ≤2 hrs). Eric + Vic Viloria define the template characteristics per color.\n- **Rep-count progression** simulating game-week workload: start ~88 reps → ramp to ~180 over four days, then taper for games.\n- **Speed-maintenance standard:** players must hit ~90% of top speed at least once every 10 min; Catapult-tracked (top speed + 14 mph efforts shared with players for accountability); failure → extra speed drills after practice.\n- **Manual rep counting** by student staff post-practice → workload data within 20–30 min.\n- **Lifting integration:** heavy lifts scheduled on high-volume (red/yellow) days to align stress, lighter days for recovery.\n- Recovery-mandate options under consideration (staffed facility monitoring, scheduled access windows, or trainer-led sessions); Vic + Eric to decide which modalities (massage, cold tub, foam rolling, Normatec, soft tissue) are mandatory.\n\n**Owners:** Eric Rash + Vic Viloria (templates, recovery); Dave Aranda (final sign-off / Baylor-specific adaptation of the K-State model); Lance Yancy + Coach Klanderman (K-State source / scripting guardrails).\n\n**Status (updated from FaceComps repo activity through 2026-06-16):** framework finalized ahead of the \"last week of June\" deadline — the 2nd planning meeting recs converged into a **locked plan of record (6/15): 14-day install structure (3/3/4/4) at Moderate ramp**, plus locked mock-game rules and NCAA acclimatization constraints. See [[facecomps-practice-simulator]] for the full decision log. No FaceComps commits since 6/16 as of this check — next deliverable is the day-by-day color/quality templates; worth a status check-in before fall camp (Aug 5 start) if it's been quiet.\n\n**Implementation:** this framework is built in the **FaceComps repo / load simulator** — see [[facecomps-practice-simulator]] (the meeting decisions above are the spec; the simulator + planning-layer grill is the build).\n\nRelated: [[ai-integration-initiative]] (Catapult/data tooling).\n\n\n---\n\n### ~/project_zotero_cli.md\n\n---\nname: zotero-cli\ndescription: \"Zotero research-library CLI built in Go at ~/printing-press/library/zotero-research-library/ — mirrors catapult conventions, Web API v3 only, full library mgmt\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: 27df54a5-abaf-449b-8ec7-f36ba16bb6b5\n---\n\nEric is building a Zotero CLI (`zotero-research-library-pp-cli`) so both he and Claude have scriptable access to his research-paper library. Plan approved 2026-05-18, lives at `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\.claude\\plans\\i-want-to-dig-peppy-hoare.md`.\n\n**Locked architecture decisions:**\n- Mirrors [[catapult-cli]] conventions exactly: Go, Cobra, `cmd/<binary>/`, `internal/{cli,client,config,store,cache,cliutil,types}`, TOML config in `~/.config/`, SQLite store via `modernc.org/sqlite`, file-based HTTP cache\n- **Zotero Web API v3 only** — no local SQLite reads of `~/Zotero/zotero.sqlite` (avoids fighting desktop app for lock; trades offline for sync consistency)\n- **No MCP companion in v1** — CLI only; Claude shells out via Bash\n- Full library mgmt scope: read, add (DOI/arXiv/ISBN via Crossref/arXiv/OpenLibrary lookups, NOT Zotero translation-server), edit, delete (PATCH `deleted: 1` for trash, hard DELETE for `--permanent`), notes, annotations (read/export only)\n- Better BibTeX citekey bridge via JSON-RPC at `127.0.0.1:23119/better-bibtex/json-rpc` — opportunistic, graceful-degrades when desktop app is closed\n- Group libraries supported via `--library user|group:<id>` global flag\n\n**Why:** Today Eric pastes paper details manually when he wants Claude to scaffold a project from a method or recreate an analysis from a paper. The CLI removes that friction.\n\n**How to apply:**\n- When Eric mentions a paper/citation in any project, check if the CLI exists yet and use it (`search`, `item get`, `pdf text`) instead of asking him to paste.\n- Out-of-scope for v1 (don't quietly add): MCP server, local-SQLite read mode, Zotero translation-server, group discovery UX, annotation writes, BBT citekey writes.\n- Build order: skeleton (go.mod, main, root, config) → reusable catapult utilities (ratelimit, cache, store skeleton) → client → auth/doctor/sync → commands.\n\n\n---\n\n### ~/reference_okr-staff-meeting-prep.md\n\n---\nname: okr-staff-meeting-prep\ndescription: OKR unit check-in meeting-prep artifacts (deck/run-of-show/KR deep-dive/1pg agenda) — reusable template set for Thursday check-ins & the Aug gate\nmetadata:\n  type: reference\n---\n\n**OKR staff-meeting prep artifacts** — built 2026-06-04 for the first Applied Performance unit OKR check-in + rollout (Eric DRI; AJ + Blake co-own). Live in OneDrive (NOT the vault):\n\n`C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\OneDrive - Baylor University\\00_Primary_Storage_Drive\\06_MEETINGS_ADMIN\\Meet_Prep\\Staff_Meetings\\`\n\n- **OKR-Rollout-2026-06-04.html** — 11-slide present-from-browser deck (why → 3 pillars → how-it-works → scoreboard → standing → ownership → the one rule → Thu check-in → summer arc → seed week one). Design: dark #0a0a0a, accent #DA7756, arrow-key nav, F=fullscreen.\n- **OKR-Rollout-RunOfShow-2026-06-04.md** — timed facilitation script (rollout block → cadence → summer → seed → close), anticipated Qs, \"three phrases to land.\"\n- **OKR-KR-DeepDive-Discussion-Guide.md** — per-KR discussion frame (\"4 questions we ask every KR\": Target/Reality/Blocker/Next action) with **Eric's lean** on all 9 KRs + cross-KR sequencing notes. Leans are Claude-inferred from blockers — Eric to verify 1.1 VB-relationship owner & 3.2 Putz-vs-Boyd warmth.\n- **OKR-OnePage-Agenda-2026-06-04.html** — printable Baylor-green one-pager (flow + 4 questions + 9-KR table + summer arc + 3 phrases + blank notes column). \"Print/Save PDF\" button, fits 1 Letter page.\n\nReusable as a template for future weekly Thu check-ins and the **end-of-Aug gate** meeting. Source of truth for content stays the vault: [[secondbrain-backup]] → `Applied-Performance-OKRs-2026-27.md`, `Applied-Performance-Operating-Cadence.md`, `okr-tracker/SCOREBOARD.md`.\n\n\n---\n\n### ~/reference_secondbrain-backup.md\n\n---\nname: secondbrain-backup\ndescription: SecondBrain vault is git-backed to private GitHub erash11/SecondBrain; canonical project hub location\nmetadata:\n  type: reference\n---\n\n**SecondBrain vault** (`~/Desktop/SecondBrain`) is a **git repo backed up to private GitHub `erash11/SecondBrain`** (set up 2026-06-01). Desktop is **NOT** OneDrive-synced — git push is the backup path. **To back up: run `./backup.sh` from the vault** (idempotent: stages all, commits with timestamp, pushes; no-op if clean). Optional message: `./backup.sh \"msg\"`. No scheduler/cron (per user constraint). `.gitignore` excludes Obsidian volatile workspace + OS cruft.\n\n**Canonical project hub:** `Projects/Applied-Performance-Hub.md` — single source of truth linking the unit OKRs (`Applied-Performance-OKRs-2026-27.md`), the operating cadence (`Applied-Performance-Operating-Cadence.md`), readable project briefs (`Project_FaceComps-Practice-Simulator.md`, `Project_AI-Integration.md`, `Project_Athlete-Data-Governance.md`), and the symposium reference.\n\n**OKR tracking:** `Projects/okr-tracker/` — hand-edit `okrs.yaml`, run `python okr.py` (or `okr.py check <KR> <conf 0-1> <green|yellow|red|met> \"note\" --next \"next action\" [--blocker ..]`) → regenerates `SCOREBOARD.md` (status/confidence/progress bar/trend/**next action**, grouped by objective). PyYAML-only. Every KR carries exactly one next action.\n\n**OKR operating playbook:** `Projects/Applied-Performance-Operating-Cadence.md` — rollout script (folded into the first check-in, no separate kickoff), Thursday check-in facilitation, outcome-based assignment, progression/escalation, and the summer red→green arc (June stand-up / July build / Aug adopt → gate). **Eric DRI on all 9 KRs; AJ/Blake co-own. Weekly check-in Thursdays** (→ biweekly in-season); first check-in + rollout = 2026-06-04. Unit = Eric/AJ/Blake only. Strategy/notes are canonical in the vault; executable tools stay in their own `~/Desktop/DEV/` repos and are linked from the hub. Don't duplicate these into DEV — link instead.\n\nRelated: [[facecomps-practice-simulator]], [[ai-integration-initiative]], [[athlete-data-governance]].\n\n\n---\n\n### ~/reference_sportsci-symposium-2026.md\n\n---\nname: sportsci-symposium-2026\ndescription: Sport Science Symposium 2026-05-30 — 8 talks Eric recorded; mineable themes + speakers for essays/OKR R&D\nmetadata:\n  type: reference\n---\n\n**Sport Science Symposium** — 8 talks Eric attended/recorded solo on **2026-05-30** (Fireflies, `*_SportSciSympo`). Reference material for R&D, essays, and the [[practice-load-framework]] / OKR work.\n\n**Talks & themes:**\n- **Patrick Ward** — research/publishing in applied sport science.\n- **Panel: Data Architecture** (John Wagle, Cam, JW — `JN-CM-JW_PanelDataArchitecture`) — internal \"listening tours\" to understand existing data measures + their rationale; eliminate unreliable/inefficient collection; strict data-communication + viz standards; choose tech by **industry norms, not individual staff preference** (scalability).\n- **Kate Weiss** — **ADI metrics** (mechanical work subcategories, mechanical power, acceleration profiles); push toward a **drill-level ADI metric** + time-based mechanical-power intensity zones; report mechanical work/power to coaches across comparable training days.\n- **Adam Parr** — (sport-science application).\n- **Sam Contorno** — training volume + **periodization** (Stanford & Texas models); **backward planning from game-week volume**; protect player availability across the season.\n- **Duncan French** — sport science as methodology; bioenergetics; advanced + sport-specific testing (horizontal sled, boxing tests); **NIRS** + respiratory/cardiac/metabolic athlete profiling; role of AI in conditioning.\n- **Cam Josse** — data validity + tool confidence; **graded explanations / clear coding systems**; manage **cognitive load** when presenting data to coaches/players (concise, actionable).\n- **Nick Winkelman** — Irish Rugby's leadership + data practices; **co-authorship culture**; national dosing standards for sprint/speed exposure; centralized health/performance dashboards with provincial data anonymity; instrumented mouthguards.\n- Also surfaced (**Dan Joss**): purposeful evidence-based data systems; standardized biomech-interpretation frameworks; **multivariate convergence + mixed-effects models** to flag individual deviation; **Bayesian** comms over p-values.\n\nRecurring threads: data validity/communication, periodization from game-week backward, mechanical-load (ADI) granularity, AI in performance, governance/standards.\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/feedback_ingest-review-gate.md\n\n---\nname: ingest-review-gate\ndescription: \"Before any expensive, hard-to-inspect ingest (ingest-book atomization): a verification step plus an independent model review must pass first\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: feedback\n  originSessionId: 4597514e-1de9-4f80-87b7-4c8742ddec83\n  modified: 2026-09-05T14:57:33.666Z\n---\n\n**Stated 2026-09-05**, finishing the Myszka book scan, when Eric was asked how much of the\npipeline could run on a cheaper model.\n\n**Do not run `/ingest-book` (or any comparable atomization) straight off a freshly built\nartifact. Gate it on two things: an automated verification step, and a review by an\nindependent model — Codex, or a fresh adversarial agent if Codex is unavailable.**\n\n**Why:** the ingest is the expensive step *and* the one whose output is hardest to eyeball.\nA page-order error costs almost nothing to make, is baked into the PDF, and is then\ninherited silently by every note built from it. Eric's own framing: \"cheap step, very\nexpensive mistake.\" He explicitly declined to cheapen the ingest itself, because that is\nwhere judgement about what matters in the text happens.\n\n**How to apply:**\n\n- Build the artifact, then **verify by a route that shares no code path with how it was\n  built.** For the book scan: the order was set by a *visual* read of cropped folio bands,\n  so verification read the folio out of the *full-page OCR text* of the finished PDF, and a\n  third pass rendered the PDF and read the folios off the rendered pages.\n- **A cheap arithmetic identity is worth having, but do not oversell it.** `files −\n  duplicates == last − first + 1` costs nothing; make it a hard gate that refuses to\n  proceed, and test it with a deliberately corrupted input. **But it is a map-consistency\n  check, not a proof of order.** On the default path — where folios are assigned as a\n  running counter — the two sides of the identity are equal *by construction*, so the gate\n  compares a number with itself. A transposition, a wrong start page, a missed duplicate,\n  and two cancelling errors all pass it.\n- **Audit the verification itself, not just the artifact.** The first version of\n  `verify_pdf.py` \"checked\" that folios increase by appending the *expected* value and\n  testing that those increased — true by construction, so it printed YES unconditionally.\n  A check that cannot fail is worse than no check. **Prove a check can fail**: feed it a\n  deliberately corrupted copy and confirm it says NO.\n- **State coverage and blind spots in the output.** Six of 240 pages printed no folio and\n  contributed no evidence; a uniform offset error is invisible to a folio check. Print\n  that, do not imply 100%.\n- Then hand it to the independent reviewer with an instruction to **falsify**, not confirm,\n  and with the specific claims spelled out.\n\n**The related cheap-model decision (same conversation):** a small model may take the first\npass at reading page numbers off contact sheets, because the sheet prints the *expected*\nnumber beside each crop — so the job is spotting disagreements, not reading every number.\nAnything it flags, and anything ambiguous, gets a careful second look. Eric chose this\nmainly for speed; he was explicit that the savings are small and **the gate is the part\nworth keeping.**\n\n**What the gate actually caught, 2026-09-05.** The independent review returned **GO** on the\nbook PDF and **NO-GO** on the reference file shipped alongside it — which is the whole\nargument for the gate in one result. It found: a verification check that was a tautology and\ncould never fail; an overstated claim about what the arithmetic gate proves; 7 chimeric\nbibliography records welding one entry's authors to another's title; and 67 of 113 records\ncarrying a phantom author literally named \"K\". All were fixed before ingest. **The page order\nwas sound, and the thing shipped beside it was not** — so gate every deliverable, not just\nthe expensive one.\n\nRelated: [[book-scan-page-ordering]] · [[keystoneos-verification-habits]]\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/project_ath-tech-framework.md\n\n---\nname: ath-tech-framework\ndescription: \"ATH software/tech management framework — where the shipped 08-14 set lives, which sections are still missing, and who unblocks them\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: 75960c5c-8bdf-4780-831f-f85391d38ed5\n  modified: 2026-09-05T12:10:04.886Z\n---\n\nBaylor Athletics software & technology management framework. Lives at\n`~/DEV/SoftwareTech_Mgmt_Framework/deliverables/`.\n\n**Shipped set — 2026-08-14** (four documents, `.docx` + `.pdf` of each):\n- `Software_and_Tech_Management_ER_2026-08-14` — the framework itself, including\n  Appendix A, the NCAA performance-technology crosswalk (`NCAA_PerformanceTech_v3.docx`).\n- `Software_and_Tech_Management_WORKFLOW_ER_2026-08-14` — the request/approval workflow.\n- `Software_and_Tech_Management_EXEC_SUMMARY_ER_2026-08-14` — the exec summary.\n- Intake forms: `Form_1_Technology_Idea_Form.docx` (requester),\n  `Form_2_Internal_ATH_Form.docx` (internal), `Request_Tracker.xlsx`.\n\nEarlier drafts in the same folder carry `DRAFT`, `SUPERSEDED_pre-edit`, or an older date\nin the filename. **The `2026-08-14` stamp is the live set** — there is no `_Archive/`\nsubfolder here, so read the date, not the folder.\n\n**Still missing from the framework** (known gaps as of 2026-08-14, none written yet):\nEducation section · Working Group definition · transfer student-athlete handling ·\nconsent template · annual survey.\n\n**Blocked on:** the ITS intake path. Micah is the contact who can supply it; the\nframework cannot state how a request actually reaches ITS until that meeting happens.\nThe separate inventory email to staff is the other open action.\n\n**Why:** the shipped/draft distinction is invisible from the filenames alone, and the\nfive gaps are not marked inside the documents — they only ever lived in working memory.\n\n**How to apply:** when asked to revise or extend the framework, start from the\n`2026-08-14` files, never a `DRAFT` or `_` variant. Do not treat the five gaps as\noversights to fix silently — Education and Working Group in particular need Micah's\nITS answer first. Related: [[status-report-sourcing]] (this is a recurring \"Shipped\"\nline item), [[kenny-boyd-doc-review-preferences]] (tone rules for the review pass).\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/project_automation-cadence.md\n\n---\nname: automation-cadence\ndescription: \"Standing automation schedule on the Mac — ritual nudges, memsearch reindex timing, OS audit cadence, gws/Gmail capability limits, reMarkable OCR entry point\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: 330c1f71-242a-47c7-ac7b-129bd6f5dd3c\n  modified: 2026-08-14T20:15:14.244Z\n---\n\nDurable schedule and capability facts for the Mac automation stack. These are stable\nsettings, not active threads — health-check them with `/claude-stack-doctor`.\n\n## Ritual nudges (launchd `com.keystone.rituals`)\n\n- **Weekdays 17:00** → `/interview-me`\n- **Fridays 15:30** → `/weekly-review`\n- `harvest` and `forecast-capture` also run through `ritual-check.ps1`; their\n  due/overdue state is computed from their own output, not from a fixed clock\n  (forecast-capture is Monday-anchored and season-bounded — see\n  [[cfb-forecast-capture]]). Four rituals total; `Name    = ` in\n  `KeystoneOS/scripts/ritual-check.ps1` is the authoritative list.\n\nThe nudge is only a prompt; **the output artifact is the record**, not the reminder firing.\n\n## memsearch reindex\n\nReindex **rides the daily backup task** rather than running on its own timer. The\nnightly moved 00:30 → **09:00 on 2026-08-29** (CRASHBOX was unplugged at 00:30), so\nany reasoning that assumes an overnight reindex is wrong — the backup's uncapped full reindex absorbs backlogs the\nshort SessionEnd hook can't finish. See [[backup-sync-system]].\n\n## OS audits\n\nFull OS audit last run **2026-07-24** (reports under `audits/`, guards auto-run);\n**next due 2026-10-24**.\n\n## Capability limits worth remembering\n\n- **gws** is authenticated. Re-auth requires explicit full scope URLs — the `-s drive`\n  shorthand silently grants no scopes (v0.22.5 bug), and `~/.config/gws/token_cache.json`\n  must be deleted after re-auth or the stale token is served. See [[gws-forms-api]].\n- **MCP Gmail is draft-only** — it can compose drafts but must not be treated as a send path.\n\n## reMarkable OCR\n\n`remarkable_extract.py` — USB-first with a `--cloud` fallback; Vision API key lives in\n`.env`.\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/project_backup-sync-system.md\n\n---\nname: backup-sync-system\ndescription: \"Nightly backup/sync architecture and its traps — two-writer root cause, manifest classification rules, Milvus is NOT in the memsearch path, dry-run can't verify writes\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: 3db720f8-cca5-4aed-96cd-e7ac4a95ab2d\n  modified: 2026-09-03T18:15:45.092Z\n---\n\n**Current state (2026-09-03).** Two backup runs happen on most days, and the 09-02 entry was\nright about that — but **not** because reconcile hard-codes the backup. The real mechanism,\ntraced 09-03:\n\n`keystone-reconcile.ps1` is a **generic make-up engine**. It has no mention of `daily-backup` at\nall; it loops the registry at `DEV/KeystoneOS/data/jobs.json` and calls\n`run-job.ps1 -Job <id> -Trigger reconcile -MadeUp` for any job with no success recorded today.\nThe `daily-backup` job is in that registry with `reconcile: true`, `graceMinutes: 60`,\n`maxAttemptsPerDay: 2`, and `makeupWindow: 07:00-20:00`. Its `successProbe` greps today's\n`daily-backup.log` for `daily-backup end: OK`. So:\n\n- **If no successful run is recorded by 07:00, reconcile replays it** → the ~07:00-07:10 run.\n- **launchd `com.keystone.dailybackup` fires at 09:00 regardless** → the second run.\n- **Run the backup by hand before 07:00 and reconcile skips it** (\"already succeeded today\").\n  Confirmed 09-03: a 06:15 manual run logged `OK` at 06:25, and no 07:00 run happened.\n\nRegistry inconsistency worth knowing: `jobs.json` records `schedule.at: \"00:30\"` while the live\nplist fires at 09:00. The make-up window is what actually drives the 07:00 replay, so the stale\n`at` has not bitten yet. Still true: `launchctl ... runs = 0` proves nothing — read the log.\nReceipts cluster only on days CRASHBOX is attached: a mount gap, not a schedule gap.\n\n**\"Scheduled runs cannot push\" is NOT yet established — and the two contexts differ.** On\n2026-09-03 the **09:00 launchd** run pushed `global-memory` cleanly and ended `OK (0 errors)`:\nno `PUSH FAILED`, no `PUSH ERR`. But the **reconcile/`run-job.ps1`** context — which is where the\n09-02 07:08 and 08-13 07:00 failures happened — **was not exercised that day**, because the manual\n06:15 run pre-empted the replay. Do not read 09-03 as clearing the gap; it only clears launchd.\n`daily-backup.ps1:74` no longer swallows the reason: the `Out-Null` was replaced 09-03 with\ncaptured output that logs `REPO <name> : PUSH ERR | <text>` on failure only. **The error text will\nonly appear on a day nobody runs the backup by hand before 07:00.** Diagnose from that line rather\nthan assuming a credential gap. `daily-backup.err` is stale (last written 2026-08-06); never read\nit as current. Manual remedy after a failed push is still `git -C <repo> push origin main` for\nSecondBrain, `.claude/global-memory`, `.claude`.\n\n**SSD layout:** non-repo DEV dirs land in `DEV-nonrepo/`, **not** `DEV/` — there is no `DEV/`\non CRASHBOX (roots are `DEV-nonrepo/`, `DEV-repo-data/`, `DEV-working-tree/`, `claude/`,\n`codex/`, `gemini/`, `SecondBrain/`, `_receipts/`). `._`-prefixed files beside every real file\nare normal AppleDouble xattr sidecars on exFAT, not corruption or duplication.\n\nThe nightly backup is `~/.claude/scripts/daily-backup.ps1` (pwsh, launchd\n`com.keystone.dailybackup`), driven by `~/.claude/scripts/backup-manifest.json`, logging to\n`~/.claude/scripts/logs/daily-backup.log`. `~/.claude` is itself the `dotclaude` repo.\nWorkflow docs live in `~/DEV/Bucket` (`docs/SYSTEM-HEALTH-FINDINGS.md`,\n`docs/AGENT-FIX-PROMPT.md`, `docs/FIX-STATUS-2026-08-14.md`).\n\n## The root cause behind most \"divergence\" symptoms — CLOSED 2026-08-14\n\nThe retired **Windows box was still running `daily-backup.ps1` and pushing to the shared\nrepos** (SecondBrain, dotclaude, claude-skills, claude-global-memory). Two writers on one\nset of repos manufactured `repo-diverged:SecondBrain` nightly. Identified by the\ncommit-message format `chore: daily backup sync YYYY-MM-DD [auto]`, generated at\n`daily-backup.ps1:62`.\n\n**Retired 2026-08-14**: all 9 KeystoneOS/MorningBriefing scheduled tasks disabled (not\nunregistered) on the Windows box; all four repos left at 0 ahead / 0 behind. It contributed\nno unique history since 08-05 — only two append-only Windows failure logs, preserved\nbyte-identical to `~/Desktop/windows-retirement-2026-08-14/` on that machine and\ndeliberately **not** pushed (origin had already untracked `.memsearch-index.log` in\n`a0e58c9`; committing would have undone a Mac-side decision).\n\nResidual, by design — the box is meant to stay usable:\n- **Session hooks still write.** `settings.json` `Stop`/`SessionEnd` hooks write\n  `global-memory/transcripts/`, so interactive Claude Code use there re-dirties that repo.\n  It cannot *diverge* it — nothing pushes on a schedule anymore. (That Stop hook is\n  currently broken there anyway: `MODULE_NOT_FOUND` on a mangled\n  `C:\\Program Files\\Git\\...\\transcript-capture.js` path.)\n- **`daily-backup.ps1` on Windows is still the macOS port** and still buggy. It is inert\n  because the task is disabled, not because it was repaired.\n- Three non-KeystoneOS tasks left enabled (`CFB-ForecastCaptureReminder`,\n  `DailyGmailDigest`, `Refresh_All_Data`) — all operate under the Windows `~/Desktop/DEV`\n  and touch none of the four repos.\n\n## Manifest classification — get this right, it routes athlete data\n\n`backup-manifest.json` is default-deny: any `~/DEV` dir absent from `paths[]` fails the\nhealth check. Adding an entry requires a human class call:\n\n| class | destinations |\n|---|---|\n| `institutional` | `ssd`, `baylor` (+`github` if a repo) |\n| `personal` | `ssd` (+`github` if a repo) |\n\n`isRepo: true` also sets `captureOrphans` and `captureWorkingTree` true and needs `remote`.\n**Athlete data must never be classed `personal`** — `personal` skips the Baylor OneDrive\ndestination. `baylor` is `deprecated: true` and must never be a sole destination.\n\n## Traps that cost time\n\n- **Milvus is NOT in the memsearch path**, despite what `RESTORE.md` and the Bucket docs\n  say. Indexing works with port 19530 closed and Docker not installed. Don't set it up.\n- **`-DryRun` cannot verify a write path.** It passes `rsync -n`, which writes nothing. A\n  snapshot that fails live will show `OK` in a dry run. Only a non-dry run is evidence.\n- **Never size up a DriveFS destination with `du`.** It reports local cache/allocated\n  blocks, not logical size — the `codex/sessions` mirror read as \"47 MB of 289 MB\" while\n  actually holding 255 of 256 files, with a 35 MB file verified byte-identical by\n  `stat -f%z` on both sides. Compare **file counts and per-file byte sizes**, never `du -sh`.\n- **`snapshot:sessions` is a genuine hang** — not a size problem, not a lock, not a\n  permission fault, and not slow enumeration. Three wrong theories died here in one\n  session; see the evidence section below before forming a fourth. **Bounded 2026-08-14**\n  by a watchdog in `Invoke-Rsync` (900s ceiling, hard SIGKILL, records `rsync-timeout`),\n  committed as `8863a37`. That caps the symptom so the run reaches the SSD capture. The\n  wedge itself is still unexplained.\n- **Tier A memory is NOT in git.** `~/.claude/.gitignore` line 1 is `/*` (default-deny\n  allowlist) and `projects/` is not allowlisted, so `projects/*/memory/*.md` has exactly\n  two backup layers: the Drive SNAP of `~/.claude/projects`, and the SSD. With CRASHBOX\n  unmounted that is a single copy. Nothing to \"commit\" there — it's by design.\n- **`daily-backup.ps1` refuses to merge diverged repos by design** — that's correct\n  behavior, not a bug. Reconcile by hand.\n- **stack-doctor's cloud heartbeat** watches the newest per-run receipt\n  (`_receipts/run-*.txt`). It used to watch `RESTORE.md`, which the job *copies*\n  (`Copy-Item -Force`, line 411) rather than rewrites, so its mtime never advanced and the\n  check cried `backup-cloud-stale (218h)` while DriveFS was healthy. Fixed 2026-08-14.\n- **Untracked files block a fast-forward**, and plain `git stash` does not pick them up —\n  needs `-u`, or rename them aside. Bit the documented SecondBrain fix.\n- Reading exit codes through a pipe returns the *last* pipeline element's status, not the\n  command's — see [[zsh-shell-traps]]. This produced two wrong readings in one\n  session (`rsync ... | tail` and a stack-check run).\n\n## Standing state as of 2026-08-14\n\nFixed: `Invoke-Rsync` false-OK guard, 6 manifest entries, SecondBrain reconciled (both\nmachines' briefings preserved, Mac copies renamed `*.mac-local.md`), stack-doctor cloud\nheartbeat. Morning brief **retired** at Eric's request — `com.keystone.morningbriefing`\nbooted out + disabled, plist and `~/DEV/morning-briefing` retained, dropped from\nstack-doctor's checked sets. `com.keystone.morning` is a *different* KeystoneOS cadence —\nleave it alone.\n\nCRASHBOX SSD **reattached 2026-08-14 ~11:50** (`/Volumes/CRASHBOX/AssistantBackup`, 447 GB\nfree, writable) — first orphan/working-tree capture since migration due in the next\nnightly. Bucket doc set committed + pushed (`ce94371`). Windows retired (above).\n\nTwo script fixes committed 2026-08-14 beyond the ones above: `ac2af1d` guards the script\nagainst running on a non-macOS host (it is the macOS port; `$IsMacOS` is PS6+, so\nWindows PowerShell 5.1's undefined `$null` aborts too — intended), and `8863a37` adds the\n`Invoke-Rsync` watchdog. Both are in dotclaude, so any machine pulling the repo inherits\nthem.\n\nOpen: the `codex/sessions` wedge itself (bounded, not explained — see evidence below).\n`.errors.log` retains 6 historical lines — clear the file once one clean nightly lands\nwith the Windows box off, not before.\n\n**Acceptance test — run 2026-08-14 15:48, mostly PASSED.** `repo-diverged:SecondBrain` and\n`manifest-unmanaged` are both GONE (Windows retirement + manifest work confirmed). The\nwatchdog fired at 900s exactly as designed, killed the wedge, and the run *continued* —\nso the **SSD layer captured for the first time since 2026-08-06**: 53,533 files newer than\nthe old receipt, `RECEIPT ssd : verified run=20260814-154848`, ~24 GB total including\nRisk_Stratification_Engine 2.0 GB and ForcePlate_DecisionSystem 1.6 GB.\n\nRemaining: `ERROR (2 errors)` = `rsync-timeout` + `snapshot:sessions`, which are the same\nsingle `codex/sessions` wedge counted twice (the watchdog error plus the snapshot failure\nit causes). Not two problems. Clear `.errors.log` only after the wedge itself is fixed.\n\nVerification trap met here: `find -newermt '-30 minutes'` silently matches nothing on BSD\nfind (macOS) — it wants an absolute date. Use `find -newer <reference-file>` instead. Also\nremember `rsync -a` preserves source mtimes, so freshly-copied files look old; compare\n*directory* mtimes or use a reference file to prove a capture ran.\n\nNote the run ordering: Drive SNAPs run *before* the SSD capture, so a long `codex/sessions`\nseed delays the SSD layer within a given run. Not a failure, just a wait.\n\n## `snapshot:sessions` — evidence from a live reproduction, 2026-08-14 15:20 (stack-doctor)\n\nCaught the hang in progress and narrowed it. **Root cause still open** — this section is\nevidence, not a verdict. Everything below is measured, not inferred:\n\n- **It is a hang, not a slow transfer.** Four rsync processes (client `-a ... codex/sessions/`,\n  its `--server` receiver, plus two older receivers) sat at **0.01–0.03 s of CPU across\n  34–85 minutes** of wall clock. Zero progress, not slow progress.\n- **Nothing is left to copy.** The mirror is **256/256 files** — the previously-missing\n  `2026/08/06/rollout-*.jsonl` is present. A complete mirror still hangs.\n- **The mount is healthy.** A recursive `find` over the destination returned 256 files\n  promptly, and `touch` inside the exact hung directory succeeded instantly. Reads *and*\n  writes both work, so this is not a wedged DriveFS/FUSE mount.\n- **The wedged processes ignore SIGTERM** (still alive minutes later in `S` state) — they\n  are blocked inside a call that neither returns nor delivers signals.\n- **Dry runs cannot see it**: `-n` completes clean, live runs hang. Another instance of the\n  \"`-DryRun` cannot verify a write path\" trap above.\n\n### Orphans are a symptom, not the cause\n\nTwo `rsync --server` receivers were **orphaned to PPID 1** (started 13:55 and 14:33). It is\ntempting to call them the blocker; the timeline refutes it — the **10:31:54 run hung at the\nidentical point** (last line `SNAP skip (missing): .codex/memories`, no end line) while the\noldest surviving orphan did not exist until 13:55. `kill` on both orphans did not unblock\nthe live pair either.\n\nWhy they accumulate: **`daily-backup.ps1` contains no `pkill` at all** (verified by grep —\ndo not go looking for one). The anchored `pkill -f \"^/usr/bin/rsync\"` belongs to an *ad-hoc\nwrapper used interactively in another session*. That pattern matches only the client, whose\n`argv[0]` is the full path; the `--server` child's `argv[0]` is bare `rsync`, so\n`pgrep -f \"^/usr/bin/rsync\"` returns the client alone. Every kill-and-retry therefore strands\none more receiver. If that wrapper is reused, match on `-x rsync` or the destination path.\n\n### ROOT CAUSE FOUND 2026-08-14 17:00 — it is not rsync\n\nRan the copy in the foreground with `-avv`, unpiped. It printed ~99 lines of\n`... is uptodate` and froze mid-filename. Then:\n\n- `sample <receiver-pid>` showed the process blocked in **`__mmap`** — one frame, not moving.\n- `lsof` on it showed FD 5 open on a specific **destination** file:\n  `codex/sessions/2026/05/08/rollout-2026-05-08T17-14-58-019e09a8-*.jsonl`.\n- **`head -c 512` on that destination file hangs by itself**, with zero rsync processes\n  running. A plain read. So the file is unreadable through DriveFS.\n- A 44-file sample elsewhere in the same mirror read instantly, so it is **specific files,\n  not the mount**.\n\nThe files are **DriveFS placeholders** — uploaded to Google, then evicted from the local\ncache. Census of the destination on 2026-08-14: 256 files, **122 materialized (48.5 MB on\ndisk), 134 placeholder-only (240.7 MB logical, 0 bytes local)**. Source is 256/256\nmaterialized. Detect with `stat -f '%b %z'`: blocks==0 with a nonzero logical size means\nthe content is not local. Reading one forces an on-demand fetch, and that fetch stalls —\nwhich is what blocks `__mmap`. rsync was the victim, not the cause. **Do not chase rsync\nflags**; the block happens at open, before any comparison strategy applies.\n\n**This inverts the problem: the backup is fine, the verification is what's broken.** All\n256 files are on Google's servers. Nothing is missing. What fails is the *next* run's\nattempt to re-read them to confirm they match. Never conclude \"the snapshot is broken\"\nfrom this failure mode.\n\nAlso: `du -sh` on the destination reports the *local cache* (48 MB), not the logical 289 MB.\nBoth numbers are true and mean different things — do not treat the gap as missing data.\n\nDiagnostic sequence that worked, reuse it: `sample <pid>` for the blocking syscall →\n`lsof -p <pid>` for the file it is on → bounded `head -c` on that path to prove the\nfilesystem, not the tool, is at fault.\n\n**Attempted fix that DID NOT WORK**: pinning `My Drive/Backups` \"Available offline\" via the\nFinder context menu (there is no CLI or xattr for it — the app ships no control surface).\nEric pinned it 2026-08-14 17:30. Result: **one** file materialized, then flat for 5 minutes\n(133 placeholders, 49.2 MB, unchanged). Restarting Drive did not help either.\n\n**DriveFS on this Mac is itself unhealthy** — this is the real blocker, not rsync and not\nthe pin:\n- It refused a graceful `osascript quit` for 20s and needed SIGKILL. Its log emitted\n  `crashpad ... ThreadsStuck` every 6s. Restarting cleared the stuck threads but nothing else.\n- The **download** queue fails, not just uploads: `merger.cc:969:ProcessDownloadEvent` +\n  `HandleFailedChange [DownloadMergeQueueItem]`. DriveFS cannot pull content *down*.\n- Signs of local DB damage: `item_query_handler.cc:1135:OnGetItemIds Ignoring item\n  local-376561 that's missing from ...`.\n- NOT quota: 671 GB used of 5.5 TB.\n\n**Do not clear the DriveFS content cache as the remedy.** It converts every materialized\nfile into a placeholder needing re-download, feeding the exact mechanism that is failing —\n122 readable files could become 0. If DriveFS is repaired, do it by signing out and back in\nto force a DB rebuild, deliberately, with time to watch it.\n\n### RESOLVED 2026-08-14 — the backup was never broken, the verification was\n\n**Final mechanism**: files Drive has evicted from local cache are placeholders. Fetching a\nplaceholder through DriveFS is *unreliable* — usually fast, sometimes stalling for tens of\nminutes. rsync blocking on such a fetch is the entire \"wedge\". Proof it is a slow fetch and\nnot a poisoned file: the file that hung four separate times **now reads instantly**, and its\nblocks went 0 → 770,048 — the repeated read attempts eventually completed the fetch. Six\nother placeholders read fine first try. Nothing was corrupt.\n\n**`~/.claude/scripts/verify-drive-backup.sh` (`0d2edb5`) is now the way to verify.** It\ncompares the Drive API's server-side `md5Checksum` against a local md5 — metadata only, no\nDriveFS read, batched ~25 names per query. First run on `~/.codex/sessions`:\n**256/256 verified, 0 mismatch, 0 missing.** The snapshot that logged `FAILED` for days was\ncomplete and byte-correct the whole time.\n\nDo not \"fix\" this by deleting Drive copies. Both files that hung were verified byte-identical\nto source *before* anything was touched — deleting would have destroyed a good backup to\nrepair a read path.\n\n**It is plain fetch latency, scaling with file size** — no mystery left. A full read-scan of\nall 256 files with a 6s per-file cap: 33 stalled, and those averaged **2,301 KB vs 1,156 KB\nfor the set overall** (max 15.8 MB). Bigger file, longer fetch. Nothing file-specific,\nnothing corrupt. The scan itself moved the mirror from 122 → 230 materialized (48.5 → 161.8 MB\nlocal), which is exactly what \"reading materializes it\" predicts.\n\nCaution when testing this: **reading a file materializes it**, so any scan warms the cache and\na later run passes for reasons that will not hold once Drive evicts again. Timestamp results\nand never treat a post-scan rsync run as a clean verification.\n\n### Why DriveFS is unhealthy — found 2026-08-14 18:20\n\n**Google Drive is mirroring `~/DEV` as a device-backup folder named \"My Mac\"**, including\n**34 `.git` directories / 3,470 files**. VS Code has `\"git.autofetch\": true`, which rewrites\nevery repo's `FETCH_HEAD` on a 180s cycle; Drive uploads all 34 each time. That is the source\nof the `PRECONDITION_FAILED` storms on `FETCH_HEAD` and the stuck threads in `drive_fs.txt`,\nand therefore of the slow placeholder fetches upstream.\n\nProof (not inference): local `~/DEV/*/.git/FETCH_HEAD` all stamped 18:22:09 while Drive's\nserver copies read `2026-08-14T23:22:09Z` — same second — and the Drive parent chain resolves\n`FETCH_HEAD → .git → DataBase_GPS_Reporting → DEV → My Mac`.\n\n**`OneDrive-Import` was a red herring** — it holds 22 `.git` dirs / 7,292 files, but they are\nstatic archives and do not churn. Don't chase it.\n\n**FIXED 2026-08-14 ~18:28 — Eric removed `~/DEV` from Drive Preferences → \"Folders from your\ncomputer\" (keeping the cloud copy). Verified, and it worked:**\n- Server-side `FETCH_HEAD` froze at `23:28:12Z` while local `~/DEV` copies kept updating\n  (18:32:28) — churn decoupled, `git.autofetch` left ON as intended.\n- `PRECONDITION_FAILED` 14 → **0**. `ProcessDownloadEvent` errors 11 → **0**.\n  `HandleFailedChange` stable (idle retry backlog, not growing).\n- Google Drive CPU **64.6% → 0.0%**.\n- **End-to-end: 5/5 genuine placeholders (blocks==0) fetched in 2–3s each**, where the same\n  class of read previously stalled for minutes or indefinitely. This is the actual cure for\n  the `snapshot:sessions` wedge — the watchdog only bounded it.\n\nOriginal recommendation, for the record: remove `~/DEV` from Drive Preferences → \"Folders\nfrom your computer\", choosing KEEP for the existing cloud copy. The mirror is redundant and\nstrictly worse than the nightly, which already covers `~/DEV` via 68 manifest entries →\nSSD + GitHub with `.git` excluded and credential files denied (6 projects hit that policy on\n2026-08-14). **Do not** \"fix\" this by disabling `git.autofetch`: that masks the symptom, costs\na useful feature, and leaves 3,470 `.git` files still mirrored to churn on every commit and\ncheckout. Verify afterward by re-querying `FETCH_HEAD` modifiedTime via the Drive API and\nre-checking `drive_fs.txt` for `ThreadsStuck` / `PRECONDITION_FAILED`.\n\nHow to check whether anything is pending before any Drive surgery: sample\n`grep -c HandleModifiedLocalItem/HandleFailedChange/DownloadMergeQueueItem` in\n`~/Library/Application Support/Google/DriveFS/Logs/drive_fs.txt` over ~90s — flat counters\nmean idle queues. **Do not** use the presence of `com.google.drivefs.item-id` as an\nupload-pending signal: 774 files in Backups lacked it and were all already on the server.\n\nRejected: `--ignore-existing` (would freeze any mid-session rollout file at its partial\nsize in Drive permanently) and leaving the watchdog to absorb it (~15 min wasted nightly,\nand two standing errors would camouflage real ones).\n\nNote `-W` (whole-file) is already default for local transfers, so `--whole-file`,\n`--size-only` and delta-algorithm tuning are all dead ends here — the block happens on\nopen, before any comparison strategy matters.\n\n## The SSD layer's real failure mode — schedule/mount mismatch, found 2026-08-29\n\n**The nightly is healthy; the SSD layer is simply absent at run time.** `com.keystone.dailybackup`\nfires at 00:30 and CRASHBOX is normally unplugged then, so every run logs\n`TARGET SSD /Volumes/CRASHBOX/AssistantBackup NOT MOUNTED - orphan and working-tree capture\nSKIPPED this run` and ends `OK (0 errors)`. A clean exit code is therefore **not** evidence the\nSSD captured — the skip is not an error. Check `_receipts/run-*.txt` mtime on the drive instead.\n\nReceipt history proves the pattern: 10 SSD captures total, clustered on days the drive happened\nto be attached (Aug 6, Aug 14 ×5, Aug 15, Aug 16, Aug 17), then a **12-day gap** to Aug 29. Eric\nread this as \"the SSD backup has never run\"; it had, just rarely.\n\n**Manual run is the remedy and takes ~6 minutes**, not the ~45 the 2026-08-14 notes imply:\n`/opt/homebrew/bin/pwsh -File ~/.claude/scripts/daily-backup.ps1` (no SSD-only switch exists; do\nnot add one). Run 20260829-073713: Drive SNAPs finished in **6 seconds** — the `codex/sessions`\nwedge is genuinely gone since the `~/DEV` de-mirroring fix — DEV capture 07:37–07:40, `.claude`/\n`.codex`/`.gemini`/SecondBrain state 07:41–07:42, `RECEIPT ssd : verified` at 07:42:50,\n`end: OK (0 errors)` at 07:43:14. SSD 25 → 27 GB. The long tail in the 00:34 nightly was the\nmemsearch INDEX step (00:51→01:18), which runs *after* the backup and is not part of it.\n\nVerification trap repeated here: **top-level SSD directory mtimes stay old after a successful\ncapture** (`SecondBrain` still read Aug 17, `claude` Aug 14) because `rsync -a` preserves source\nmtimes and a parent dir's mtime only moves when an entry is added or removed at that level. The\n`STATE-SSD ... : OK` log lines and the receipt are the evidence; mtimes are not. See also\n[[keystoneos-verification-habits]].\n\n**RESOLVED 2026-08-29**: schedule moved 00:30 -> 09:00 in\n`~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.keystone.dailybackup.plist` (PlistBuddy `Set :StartCalendarInterval:Hour 9`,\nthen `launchctl bootout gui/$(id -u)/com.keystone.dailybackup` + `bootstrap`; live schedule confirmed\n`Hour => 9, Minute => 0` via `launchctl print`). Old plist kept at `.plist.bak-2026-08-29`. 09:00 was\nchosen from mount evidence (drive attached 07:31 on 08-29; the 08-17 capture ran 08:05). **Do not\nverify this by mtime or by exit code** — the only proof is a fresh `_receipts/run-*.txt` on the drive\ndated 08-30 or later. If receipts still cluster, the drive is not attached at 09:00 and the hour is wrong.\n\n`~/.claude/global-memory/transcripts/.errors.log` archived to `.errors.log.archived-2026-08-29` and\ncleared the same day. It held two **still-open** issues, not stale history: memsearch `onnxruntime`\nimport failure (2026-08-22, corroborated by `INDEX full set: Indexed 0 chunks` on 08-28/08-29), and\nstack-doctor weekly flagging `errors-log`, `flush-stale`, `memfile-full:MEMORY.md` (2026-08-24).\n\n**Both cleared by end of 2026-08-29** — stack-doctor returned `ALL CLEAR`: memsearch `search: OK`,\n`last-flush.json OK (0h ago)`, no `FLUSH_ERROR` in recent dailies, `errors.log: clean`. The\n`onnxruntime` import failure did not need a reinstall, so **do not** reflexively run\n`uv tool install --force memsearch` on the next `ETIMEDOUT` — that strips the local flush patch\nand creates a worse problem. Confirmed here: `memsearch --version` answered instantly (0.4.17) and\n`pgrep -fc memsearch` was 0, ruling out both a broken binary and the\n[[milvus-lock-misdiagnosis]] lock-contention pattern.\n\nA separate `memsearch-sync: index ETIMEDOUT Nx consecutively` streak ran 6→13 during the afternoon\nsession of 08-29 and then self-cleared. Per the skill's own note, `Indexed 0 chunks` is usually a\nstarved flush pipeline rather than a dead indexer — **check `flush-stale` before touching\nmemsearch**. The streak counter is reset by the nightly backup, so a climbing count mid-session is\nexpected noise unless it survives a backup run. Both cleared by end of 2026-08-29 —\nstack-doctor returned ALL CLEAR and the thread is closed.\n\nGuard trap: shell truncation (`: > file`) anywhere under `$HOME` is blocked by dcg\nrule `core.filesystem:redirect-truncate-root-home`; `cp /dev/null file` is allowed, and macOS has no\n`truncate` binary. This file is itself near the `memfile-full` threshold — trim before appending more.\n\n## A stalled run with no rsync child = an unanswered macOS access prompt (2026-09-03)\n\n`daily-backup.ps1` gates both optional destinations on a bare `Test-Path` with **no timeout**:\n`Test-Path $GDrive` (section 2a) and `Test-Path $SsdRoot` (section 2b). When macOS has a pending\nTCC/removable-volume permission prompt for that path, the call blocks **forever** — and because 2a\nruns before 2b, the SSD capture never happens and no receipt is written.\n\n**The tell:** `pwsh` alive in `S`/`SN` state, **zero rsync children**, and the log frozen mid-run.\nThat is not a slow copy — a slow copy always shows an `rsync` process. `ps -axo pid,stat,comm` on\nmacOS marks I/O wait `U`, not `D`; a `D`-state grep finds nothing and proves nothing.\n\n**The fix is the human, not the shell — look at the screen for a pending prompt FIRST.** On\n2026-09-03 both `~/Library/CloudStorage/GoogleDrive-*` and `/Volumes/CRASHBOX` were unresponsive to\na plain `ls`. The Drive half cleared after a quit/relaunch of Google Drive, **but Eric was\napproving access prompts in that same window — the relaunch is unproven and may have done nothing.**\nDo not treat killing Google Drive as the known fix. The SSD half cleared only when he approved the\nprompt. **Do not force-unmount CRASHBOX** (exFAT, no journal) — ask first.\n\nProbably (unverified): the Claude Code shell and the launchd/reconcile jobs are different TCC\nprincipals, so this prompt should not recur for the scheduled runs — but it may fire once per new\napp that launches the script.\n\n**Never re-run without `pkill -f daily-backup.ps1` first.** A hung run is harmless, but several hung\nruns unblock *together* the moment access is granted and race onto the SSD — the two-writer\ncorruption this whole system exists to prevent.\n\n**Timing that matters:** reconcile replays the backup at 07:00 if no `OK` is logged for today, and\nlaunchd fires at 09:00 regardless (see \"Current state\" above). A manual run finishing before 07:00\nsuppresses the replay; one finishing after 07:00 can collide with it.\n\nOpen gap, not yet fixed: neither `Test-Path` has a watchdog, unlike `Invoke-Rsync`\n(`$RsyncTimeoutSec = 900`). Worth wrapping both.\n\n## `/backup` skill + the completeness signal (2026-09-04)\n\n`~/.claude/skills/backup/SKILL.md` is the on-demand path — the launchd job only fires if the\nMac is awake at 09:00. Standing routine: plug in CRASHBOX, then type `/backup`.\n\n**Job count, not error count, is the completeness signal.** A run with CRASHBOX unmounted\nreports `OK (0 errors)` with **9 jobs**; the same run with it attached reports **31 jobs**.\nNever report \"backup complete\" from a 0-error run without checking `okCount` and naming any\nskipped target.\n\n`repo-push:*` failures are usually a transient `Could not resolve host: github.com`, not a\ncredential or repo problem — commits are already local, so a re-run pushes them. Seen\n2026-09-04 09:05 on `global-memory` and `skills`; one re-run cleared both.\n\nA USB-C drive that powers on but never mounts is almost always a charge-only cable.\n`diskutil list external` printing nothing at all is the fast check — that is a cable/port\nproblem, not software.\n\nFull handoff: `~/.claude/global-memory/handoffs/2026-09-04-backup-skill.md`.\n\n## Manifest coverage closed + job-count baseline reset (2026-09-09)\n\n**The 49 `manifest-unmanaged` folders are gone.** They were never a classification backlog —\nthey were fallout from the GitHub consolidation migration (see [[github-consolidation]]).\nAll 49 added to `backup-manifest.json` in one pass: 47 are git repos with `erash11` remotes\n(`isRepo:true`, `captureOrphans`/`captureWorkingTree` true, destinations `[\"ssd\",\"github\"]`),\nand 2 are plain dirs — `_ARCHIVE` (4.3 GB) and `docs` — destinations `[\"ssd\"]` only.\n`baylor` deliberately omitted from every new entry; it is deprecated and Eric's own newest\nentry (`DEV/Life-OS`, commit `3444c0b`) already omits it.\n\n**`class` is inert.** Grep of `daily-backup.ps1` shows it is read nowhere — only `isRepo`,\n`captureOrphans`, `captureWorkingTree` and `destinations` drive behavior. The\ninstitutional/personal call is documentation. Do not stall a manifest add waiting on it.\n\n**A DEV manifest entry never auto-commits or pushes that repo.** `Sync-Repo` is called on a\nhardcoded list (SecondBrain, global-memory, skills, dotclaude); the manifest loop only calls\n`Capture-RepoData` (SSD) or `Snapshot-Additive`. Adding 47 project repos was therefore safe\nwith open PRs and dirty trees — verify from source before assuming otherwise.\n\n**Job-count baseline is now 76, not 31.** The 2026-09-04 note above (9 = no SSD, 31 = SSD\nattached) is superseded. Full run with CRASHBOX attached = **76 jobs**. `CAPTURE` only logs a\nline when a repo has files git does not carry, so 18 log lines with 65 capture jobs is normal —\nread `okCount`, not the log line count.\n\n**Removed 2 dead entries**: `DEV/AI_Analyst` and `DEV/token-dashboard`. Both had moved into\n`~/DEV/_ARCHIVE/`, which is itself now backed up, so coverage was preserved, not dropped.\n`DexaProject` stays declared-but-absent on purpose — it lives on Google Drive.\n\n**`~/.claude` now tracks session transcripts.** Eric's commit `8a3d55c` (2026-09-09 15:44)\nun-ignored `projects/`, so the dotclaude repo carries ~368 MB of `.jsonl` transcripts and\nTier A memory. This **invalidates the \"Tier A memory is NOT in git\" trap above** — it now has\na third layer. No single file exceeds 40 MB, so pushes succeed; they are just slower.\n\nFull handoff: `~/.claude/global-memory/handoffs/2026-09-09-backup-manifest-coverage.md`.\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/project_book-scan-page-ordering.md\n\n---\nname: book-scan-page-ordering\ndescription: \"Putting phone-scanned book pages in page order: OCR of the folio is unreliable, read a contact sheet instead; sort by mtime, never filename\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  modified: 2026-09-05T15:14:58.631Z\n  originSessionId: 2a119c54-f684-43a9-b1a0-c8653e1c2320\n---\n\n**Learned 2026-09-03** putting 88 vFlat iPhone scans of the Myszka football book into page\norder. Cost several wasted OCR-tuning passes before the right method surfaced.\n\n## Do not trust OCR for the page number\n\nThree tuning attempts; **one made it worse** (80 of 88 unread). Two failure modes:\n\n- A bottom-strip crop catches **body text and citation years**. Taking the longest digit\n  token then picks a 4-digit year over a 2-digit folio.\n- Tesseract **confuses 5 with 9**. It read page **50 as 90 with 8/8 unanimous votes** — a\n  confident wrong answer, which is worse than an unread one. Vote counts do not rescue this.\n\n## What actually works — read a contact sheet\n\n1. Crop a tight band around the **centred** folio: `x 0.36–0.64`, `y 0.900–0.952` of the\n   page image. Autocontrast, grayscale.\n2. Tile ~44 of them into one PNG with the capture index labelled beside each.\n3. **Look at it.** Two sheets covered 88 pages, unambiguously, in two passes.\n\nFolios are large, bold and isolated — trivial to read by eye, and the thing OCR keeps\ngetting wrong. Do not keep tuning tesseract; switch methods after the second failure.\n\n## Ordering traps\n\n- **Sort by mtime, never by filename.** macOS appends ` (1)` on download collisions, and\n  `…17-35-17 (1).jpg` sorts *before* `…17-35-17.jpg` while the timestamps say the reverse.\n- **Same-second captures land out of page order.** Two pairs were swapped this way.\n- A page shot out of sequence (67 before 66) looks exactly like a **missing page** if your\n  sample happens to skip the tile it is on. Verify a \"gap\" before believing it.\n\n## Other findings\n\n- **A zero-word OCR page may just be blank.** One page returned no text and was genuinely\n  blank in the book — but still carried a printed folio.\n- **Bad pages cluster in time**, not randomly: all 7 poor scans fell inside one two-minute\n  window. Re-shoot the window, not the whole book.\n- **The scan folder is live** while the human is still working. Any script must re-verify\n  its map rather than assert on a file count — a new shot broke an `assert` mid-analysis.\n- Rename via a **temp prefix in two passes** so no collision is possible, and always write\n  an old→new manifest so the rename is reversible.\n\n## Quality bar that worked\n\nvFlat Scan on iPhone, ~2000×3200 px (~360 DPI) — above the 300 DPI OCR target. Median\ntesseract confidence **95.0**. Turn the app's OCR off, or just use\n`ocrmypdf --force-ocr`, which discards any text layer the app added and makes the setting\nirrelevant.\n\nPipeline: `img2pdf` (page order) → `ocrmypdf --force-ocr --rotate-pages --deskew` →\n`ingest-book` at **full-text tier**.\n\n## Second batch, 2026-09-05 — what changed\n\n157 more pages finished the book (printed 14–253, 240 pages). Median confidence **95.5**,\nconsistent with the first batch, so the quality bar above holds.\n\n**All of this is now code:** `DEV/RTP_Constraints_Course/scripts/scan_pipeline.py`. Read\nits docstring before the next book rather than rebuilding the method. `verify_pdf.py`\nbeside it proves page order afterwards.\n\n**The arithmetic gate is worth having but is NOT a page-order proof** — corrected after\nreview. `files − duplicates == last − first + 1` is enforced in code that refuses to\nrename, and it does catch gaps, doubled folios and collisions. But with no overrides the\nfolios are a running counter, so the identity holds *by construction* and the gate compares\na number with itself. **A transposition, a wrong start page, a missed duplicate and two\ncancelling errors all pass it.** What actually proved order was `verify_pdf.py` reading the\nfolio out of the finished PDF's full-page OCR — plus a fourth pass reading prose continuity\nacross every page boundary. Never let the gate stand in for those.\n\n**Collision pairs are not rare.** Twelve same-second ` (1)` pairs this batch against two\nlast time, and one pair (`08-08-44`) was genuinely inverted on disk relative to capture.\nThe mtime rule handles it; filename sort would not have.\n\n**Re-shoots cluster into runs.** Printed 170–173 were each shot twice, in two passes ~30 s\napart, with the *first* pass interleaved so 170 and 171 shared a second. Resolve duplicates\nby **measured OCR confidence**, not by eye or by assuming the later shot is better — here\nthe earlier copy won three of the four.\n\n**Blank versos carry folios.** Printed 62, 194 and 236 are blank in the book and each still\nprints its number. Open the page before calling a zero-word OCR result a failed scan.\n\n## Scan the front matter FIRST on the next book\n\nLearned 2026-09-05, the expensive way. The Myszka front matter was scanned *last*, as an\nafterthought, and it produced two things nothing else could:\n\n1. **The printed contents page is external ground truth for page order.** It listed all\n   thirteen landmarks and matched the scan-derived map exactly — a stronger check than any\n   of the four internal ones, and free.\n2. **It revealed that the book was longer than the scan.** The contents ended with \"About\n   the Authors 255\"; the scan stopped at 253. **Nothing in the body revealed the gap** — no\n   header, no folio, no cross-reference — and it survived five verification passes\n   unnoticed. Only the contents page exposed it.\n\nThe copyright page also settles the publication year, which the body never states.\n\n**So: photograph the contents page and copyright page before scanning the body.** It costs\ntwo shots, tells you where the book ends, and gives you the check to grade every later\npage against.\n\nRelated: [[dev-layout-and-discovery]] · [[ingest-review-gate]]\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/project_brain-audit-loop.md\n\n---\nname: brain-audit-loop\ndescription: \"The /brain-audit skill — what it scores, where reports and the trend live, and the manual-vs-cron failure it exists to catch\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: 83464d9d-8262-4ca8-9aed-13167ae4fa1f\n  modified: 2026-09-08T02:32:14.657Z\n---\n\nBuilt 2026-09-07 from a gap analysis of a Nate Herk AIOS video. The video's own\ncontent was mostly things already built better here (routing map, graphify,\ncommand centre); the four things missing were a **recurring scored audit**, a\n**level-up step**, a **topic-driven grill that files itself**, and a **rubric for\n\"is this working\"**. Analysis: `~/SecondBrain/Inbox/2026-09-07-aios-video-gap-analysis.md`.\n\n**The skill:** `~/.claude/skills/brain-audit/` — `SKILL.md` plus `measure.sh`\n(deterministic facts, ~7s). Scores the four C's (context, connections,\ncapabilities, cadence) 25 points each, runs three pass/fail tests, writes\n`~/SecondBrain/audits/brain-audit-YYYY-MM-DD.md`, appends one row to\n`~/SecondBrain/audits/_trend.md`, and proposes **exactly two** builds. Paths are\nregistered in `~/.claude/skills/_shared/config.json` as `vault_audits` /\n`vault_audit_trend`.\n\n**Baseline 2026-09-07: 67/100** (context 17, connections 22, capabilities 15,\ncadence 13). Two builds it proposed and Eric has not yet started: `/grill-me\n<topic>` writing to `Inbox/grills/`, and putting `/brain-audit` on the monthly\n`com.keystone.rituals` timer.\n\n**Why:** The recurring failure in this system is not bloat or drift — it is that\n**everything with a cron stays alive and everything manual freezes inside six\nweeks.** Measured twice: `Notes/` froze 2026-06-05, restarted after the\n2026-07-24 audit (11 → 23 notes), froze again 2026-08-03. The single prior audit\nsat alone for 45 days. A scored trend is the countermeasure, and it only works if\nit is itself on a timer.\n\n**How to apply:** Don't score `Inbox/` freshness as health — it is cron-fed by\n`meeting-sync` and grows daily regardless. `Notes/` and `Drafts/` are the honest\nsignal, which is why cadence is scored on `Notes/` rather than on job exit codes.\nWhen proposing builds, prefer automating an existing manual step over adding a new\ncapability, and check the proposal against [[user_working-conditions-bottleneck]] —\nthe grill-me build was chosen because that memory says the limiting factor is the\nabsence of a thinking partner and the right move is helping him capture what\nsurfaces. Stay off two neighbours' territory: `claude-stack-doctor` owns plumbing,\n`improve-system` owns note-level rot. Related: [[project_keystoneos-verification-habits]],\n[[feedback_ingest-review-gate]].\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/project_cfb-analytics-lab.md\n\n---\nname: cfb-analytics-lab\ndescription: \"CFB Analytics Lab workspace at ~/DEV/CFB_Analytics_Lab — resume protocol, and the CFBD API traps that cost real budget to rediscover\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: f6ecdcbb-242a-4647-a903-9d4ba12a4d93\n  modified: 2026-08-30T05:46:29.856Z\n---\n\n`~/DEV/CFB_Analytics_Lab` is a teaching workspace created 2026-08-13 for working through\ntechniques from *Football Analytics with Python and R* (Eric A. Eager & Richard A. Erickson,\nO'Reilly 2023) applied to **college** football data instead of the book's NFL/nflfastR data.\nPrivate GitHub repo `erash11/CFB_Analytics_Lab`; the foundation work is **PR #1, still open** as of\n2026-08-16 — `foundation` sits 21 commits ahead of `main`, and `main` holds only the spec and plan.\nCheck out `foundation`, not `main`, or the workspace looks empty.\n\n**To resume:** `cd ~/DEV/CFB_Analytics_Lab`, read `MISSION.md`, then `NOTES.md`, then the newest\n`learning-records/` entry. Self-describing by design — do not re-interview about the mission.\nSame pattern as [[terminal-fluency-workspace]].\n\n**Teaching protocol:** lessons are built one at a time, gated on the previous drill being\nreported. Lesson 0001 (stability) is built; the drill is not yet done. Remaining planned:\nEDA/regression, over-expected metrics (CPOE/RYOE), GLMs, clustering+PCA, tree ensembles,\nBayesian hierarchical. Bayesian is an extension past the book, which stops at frequentist GLMs.\n\n**CFBD API traps — verified live 2026-08-13, each cost real calls to establish:**\n- Free tier is **1,000 calls/month**, hard reset on the 1st. CFB and CBB share one pool.\n- **`GET /info` does NOT decrement the quota** — budget checks are free. Verified by two\n  consecutive calls leaving `usedCalls` unchanged.\n- **`/plays/stats` silently truncates at exactly 2000 rows** with no error or cursor — one week\n  returned 2000 rows spanning only 10 of ~107 games. Always narrow by `gameId` or `team`.\n- **`/plays` requires an explicit `week`** for both `regular` and `postseason`; omitting it is a\n  400. No whole-season call exists, so per-week iteration sets the cost floor (~23/season).\n- **`/plays` returns no `season`, `week`, or `seasonType` field** — they must be stamped on at\n  write time or every downstream aggregation breaks.\n- **`/plays` includes FCS games, and coverage roughly doubles at 2022** (887 → 1,459 games/season).\n  Cross-2022 year-over-year analysis on unfiltered plays compares ~217 teams against ~300 and\n  gives confident wrong answers. `fbs_only` collapses it to a flat 770–808.\n- Postseason weeks are **non-contiguous** (1, then 11–15, then 20), so break-on-empty stops at\n  week 2. Current pull is week 1 only; the missing 127 games are all FCS.\n- **A season query returns postseason rows mixed in, re-labelled with a regular-season week\n  number.** `/stats/game/advanced?year=2025&team=TCU` came back with two `week: 1` rows — one\n  `seasonType: \"regular\"` (North Carolina) and one `seasonType: \"postseason\"` (USC). Anything\n  keyed or sorted on `week` alone silently gains a phantom duplicate game, and per-week averages\n  are then wrong. **Always filter `seasonType == \"regular\"`** on season endpoints, not just\n  `/plays`. Found 2026-08-30 building the Baylor coach report, where it printed a fake second\n  week-1 opponent in a snap-count table.\n- Tier-gated (401 on free): `/live/plays`, `/scoreboard`, `/games/weather`, all `/wepa/*`\n  (opponent-adjusted EPA). Everything historical and play-level is available.\n\n**Local data:** `data/raw/plays/` holds 1,495,005 plays for 2019–2025 (73 MB Parquet), pulled for\n129 calls. Gitignored and regenerable via `python -m cfblab.cli pull`. Lesson 0001 needs no API\ncalls — it runs on `~/DEV/CFB_Victory_Model/data/cfbd/` CSVs, which also supply the `games_*.csv`\nneeded for FBS filtering.\n\n**Testing:** `pytest` is free and offline (49 tests). `pytest -m live` spends one real call.\n\n**Why:** the API constraints above are not in CFBD's docs and were established by probing that\nspent budget from a cap that only resets monthly. Rediscovering them costs calls and can strand\nthe project for weeks.\n**How to apply:** read this before touching CFBD from any project, including\n[[cfb-forecast-capture]] work, which shares the same key and the same 1,000-call pool.\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/project_cfb-forecast-capture.md\n\n---\nname: cfb-forecast-capture\ndescription: \"CFB Victory Model weekly forecast freeze — all 16 Big 12 teams via scripts/weekly_run.sh, archive mirror, Monday-anchored nag 2026-08-24 to 2027-01-23, no backfill, and how to tell a real missed week from a stale snapshot\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: 330c1f71-242a-47c7-ac7b-129bd6f5dd3c\n  modified: 2026-09-02T19:20:41.051Z\n---\n\nThe CFB Victory Model's pre-game forecasts are frozen once per week by the\n`/forecast-capture` skill, which snapshots predictions **before kickoff** and reports what\nmoved since the prior week.\n\n## Scope and entry point (changed 2026-09-02, per Eric)\n\n- **All 16 Big 12 teams every week**, not Baylor only. The list is the `BIG12` constant in\n  `weekly_capture.py` — one home; do not copy team lists into the wrapper or the skill.\n  `--teams`/`--team` still narrow a run. Budget ~10 min for the full sweep.\n- **One entry point:** `~/DEV/CFB_Victory_Model/scripts/weekly_run.sh` (capture → compare\n  `--all --archive`). It hardcodes the absolute `uv` path.\n- **Archive mirror:** every snapshot and comparison is copied to\n  `~/DEV/CFB_Forecast_Archive/<season>/`. Read-only copy; truth stays in\n  `CFB_Victory_Model/data/`. Registered in `backup-manifest.json` as `personal`/ssd —\n  see [[backup-sync-system]] for why a new `~/DEV` folder must be added there.\n- The ritual guard still keys off the newest `*_baylor.csv`, which the Big 12 sweep always\n  writes, so detection is unaffected.\n\n## A gap can mean the other machine captured it\n\nConfirmed 2026-09-09. The Mac's `data/weekly_forecast_log.csv` looked like it had\nmissed 2026-08-26 and 2026-08-31 — it jumped 07-31 → 09-02. Neither week was\nmissed. **Both were captured on the Windows box**, which runs the same wrapper\nagainst the same repo. The two machines had diverged, each holding captures the\nother lacked.\n\nBefore declaring a missed week, check the other machine's copy of the log.\nMerging is safe: the natural key is `(captured_at, season, team, week, game_id)`,\nand a union on that key had **zero conflicts** across 216 Mac + 348 Windows rows\n→ 540. Same key never carried different values. See [[github-consolidation]].\n\n## An old newest-snapshot file is not proof of a missed week\n\nChecked 2026-09-02: the newest snapshot was `2026-07-31_baylor.csv`, and both the skill and\nCOMMANDS.md asserted the 08-24 and 08-31 weeks were \"gone for good\". They were not — Baylor's\nopener was 2026-09-05, so no game had been played and nothing had been overwritten. **A week\nis lost only if a game was actually played inside the gap.** Verify before declaring a loss:\n\n```\ncurl -s \"https://site.api.espn.com/apis/site/v2/sports/football/college-football/teams/239/schedule?season=2026\"\n```\n\n(239 = Baylor; read each event's `status.type.name`.) Both docs were corrected.\n\n## Cadence\n\n- ~~**Wednesdays**, season window **2026-08-26 → 2027-01-20**~~ — superseded\n  2026-08-15. Current cadence is **Monday-anchored**, season window\n  **2026-08-24 → 2027-01-23**. See \"Cadence\" below, which is authoritative.\n\n## The constraint that makes the cadence non-negotiable\n\n**There is no backfill.** A pre-game forecast is only capturable before the game is played;\na missed week is permanently missing from the series, not a chore that rolls\nforward. This is why the reminder escalates rather than silently re-queuing.\n\n## Mac-side guard (built 2026-08-15)\n\n`ritual-check.ps1` now carries a fourth ritual, `forecast-capture`, so the cadence is\nenforced on this machine rather than depending on memory. It detects captures from the\nartifact itself — newest `*_baylor.csv` in `~/DEV/CFB_Victory_Model/data/weekly_snapshots/`\n— matching the script's existing \"the artifact is the record, not a state file\" rule.\n\n**The real deadline is Saturday's kickoffs, not a maintenance window.** The report\noverwrites its Chance of Victory column with the realised result once a game is *played* —\nthat is what destroyed 2019–2025. Nothing scheduled updates midweek.\n\nCadence (Monday-anchored, per Eric 2026-08-15 — he wanted the warning early in the week):\n**Mon DUE → Tue–Sat OVERDUE → Sun silent** (games played, week gone). Season bounds are the\nMonday of the opening week (2026-08-24) and the Saturday of the closing week (2027-01-23),\nwidened from the documented 08-26 → 01-20 so neither end truncates a live capture window.\n\nA third status, **RECHECK**, exists because an early capture is not proof of a good one: the\nsource may not have refreshed the coming weekend's numbers on Monday, so a Monday snapshot\ncan hold last week's figures. Treating \"captured this week\" as done would let a stale\nsnapshot pass for a good week — the exact risk that makes capturing early otherwise unsafe.\nSo a capture taken *earlier* in the week keeps surfacing through Wednesday. Capturing today\nalways satisfies today; RECHECK means *verify*, not *you missed it*.\n\nThree counterintuitive silences (off-season, Sunday, post-Wednesday-once-captured) plus the\nRECHECK window are pinned by `tests/keystone-ritual-forecast-capture.ps1`. Both a\ndays-since-last-capture and a naive \"captured this week → OK\" implementation fail them. The\nscript gained `-Today` and `-CfbSnapshotDir` seams to make this testable.\n\n**`-Notify` was mute on this Mac from the migration until 2026-08-15** — it shelled out to\n`msg.exe` (Windows-only), so every scheduled run hit the catch and logged \"desktop prompt\nunavailable\" where nobody reads it. Now `osascript`, dialog with a banner fallback. This\naffected *all* rituals, which is consistent with interview-me sitting 12 days overdue and\nweekly-review 15. If ritual nags ever go quiet again, test `-Notify` directly before\nassuming the rituals are simply current.\n\nSchedule: `com.keystone.rituals` fires Mon–Fri 17:00 plus Fri 15:30, so Monday's DUE and the\nTue–Fri escalation all land. **Saturday and Sunday have no trigger** — Saturday's OVERDUE is\ntherefore only visible if the script is run by hand. Acceptable because Fri 15:30 and 17:00\nare the last useful warnings anyway, but it does mean the Saturday branch is effectively a\nmanual-run safety net, not a live alarm.\n\nA Windows-side scheduled task `CFB-ForecastCaptureReminder` was left enabled when the\nWindows box was retired 2026-08-14 — it operates under that machine's own `~/Desktop/DEV`\nand touches none of the Mac repos, so it is harmless but is *not* the authoritative\nreminder. See [[backup-sync-system]].\n\nRelated model/data work lives in [[cfb-analytics-lab]] (a separate project —\n`~/DEV/CFB_Analytics_Lab` vs the Victory Model's own tree).\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/project_command-centre.md\n\n---\nname: command-centre\ndescription: \"The DEV front door — always-on read-only status page at http://localhost:7373, plus the rules that keep it trustworthy\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: b7d080c1-e0f8-4eb5-b181-d06b87224449\n  modified: 2026-09-02T01:44:00.951Z\n---\n\n**Built 2026-08-31.** Closes ARMS gap 5. `~/DEV/CommandCentre/server.py`, served by\n`com.keystone.commandcentre` (`RunAtLoad` + `KeepAlive`), own `DESIGN.md`.\n\n**<http://localhost:7373>** — one page answering \"is anything broken?\": agent\nhealth, backup result, graph progress, logged failures, active projects, and\nlinks to every generated report with its age.\n\nDesign rules that must hold, each learned the hard way:\n\n- **Standard library only.** Python 3.14 here is PEP 668-managed, so any\n  dependency means a venv to maintain forever. A read-only local page never\n  earns that.\n- **Rendered fresh per request.** Never cached, never regenerated on a timer. A\n  page that can be stale is a page you stop believing — that is exactly why the\n  on-demand `project-triage/dashboard.html` never became a front door.\n- **A dashboard that contradicts the CLI is worse than no dashboard.** The file\n  counter first used `total - len(done)` and disagreed with `gf_remaining.py` by\n  13, because the done-list holds entries no longer in the corpus. It now does\n  the same **set difference**, and the corpus selection is kept identical across\n  `server.py`, `gf_gemini.py` and `gf_remaining.py`.\n- **Judge a running service on its PID, not its last exit code.** A `KeepAlive`\n  daemon carries the exit code of the instance that *died*, so a restart leaves a\n  nonzero code behind permanently. `launchctl list`'s first column is the PID and\n  the second is that stale status — reading the wrong column makes a healthy\n  service look broken.\n- **Live `launchctl` state is the truth**; `run-ledger.json` is shown but never\n  allowed to claim a job succeeded. See [[keystoneos-verification-habits]].\n- **Every source is optional** — a missing input renders \"unknown\", never a 500.\n- **Bound to `127.0.0.1` only**, which is why it needs no auth. Do not change the\n  bind address without adding one. Read-only on purpose: no buttons, so a stray\n  browser tab cannot fire an agent. `~/.claude/scripts/skill-button.sh` exists\n  for the day buttons are wanted.\n\n**The threat to a localhost service is a web page you visit, not the network.**\nAny site your browser opens can make requests to `127.0.0.1`, so binding to\nloopback is not a security boundary on its own. **DNS rebinding** is the real\nattack: the attacker's domain re-resolves to `127.0.0.1`, their JavaScript\nbecomes same-origin, and it reads everything. The defence is a **`Host` header\nallowlist** — refuse any request whose Host is not a loopback name. Three review\nrounds were needed to get the parsing right: compare IPv6 numerically (so\n`[0:0:0:0:0:0:0:1]` is recognised), refuse a second `Host` header rather than\nguess, strip exactly one trailing DNS dot, and validate the port range. Also\nlearned: **curl silently collapses duplicate headers** — prove that guard over a\nraw socket.\n\n**An allowlisted name is not an allowlisted file.** `Path.exists()` and\n`read_bytes()` follow symlinks, so an exact-path allowlist still served\n`/etc/hosts` when the target was swapped for a symlink. Confine the resolved\nobject too, then **read through the same descriptor you checked** (`O_NOFOLLOW`,\nthen compare the fd's `(st_dev, st_ino)` to the pre-check `lstat`) — checking a\npath and reopening it by name is a check/use race. Close the fd in a `finally`\nthat catches every exception type, not just `OSError`.\n\n**Any number shown in two places must be computed by identical code.** A page\nthat contradicts the CLI destroys trust in both. Three separate counters here\nhad to be aligned four times: set difference not arithmetic, the same corpus\nselection, the same skip gates, the same `GF_MAXBYTES` env override — including\nits \"0 means no cap\" convention — and the gate applied to a progress line's\nnumerator *and* denominator.\n\n**Creating any new `~/DEV/<folder>` breaks the backup immediately** — the manifest\nis default-deny, so `daily-backup` exits 1 until the folder is added to\n`~/.claude/scripts/backup-manifest.json`. Working as designed; just add the entry\nin the same turn. The `class` field answers *who keeps this when you leave\nBaylor* — `institutional` also copies to Baylor OneDrive (\"lost when Baylor\naccess ends\"), `personal` stays yours. See [[backup-sync-system]].\n\n## The `/os` view — added 2026-09-01\n\nA second face over the same sources: **<http://localhost:7373/os>** (page:\n`CommandCentre/os.html`, data: `/api/state.json`). `/` is unchanged. Renders\nroutines, 148 skills, an artifact ring, Second Brain counts, health and projects.\nRationale lives in `DESIGN.md` → \"The `/os` view\". Backup: `server.py.bak-2026-09-01`.\nDesign study it came from (video frames + original spec): `CommandCentre/reference/rubric-agentic-os/`.\n\n- **It is read-only, and that is not a gap to fill casually.** It shows which model\n  and effort a skill *would* run at; it cannot run one. Run buttons and the model ×\n  effort matrix were deliberately **not rendered** — dead controls are worse than\n  none. `~/.claude/scripts/skill-button.sh` is the guarded write path for that day.\n- **Never add a CORS header to `/api/state.json`, and never open `/os` as\n  `file://`.** Same-origin is the only thing keeping the whole state object away\n  from any site the browser has open. A `file://` page would need CORS to fetch it\n  — that is the trap, not the fix.\n- **`/open` allowlists ring files by identical string, recomputed per click** —\n  same rule as `LINKS`, never a prefix or subtree test. The ring moves as files are\n  written, so a click can 404 when it shifted underneath. That is the correct\n  failure; widening the check is how a traversal gets in.\n- **The ring must stay capped per folder and per project** (`RING_PER_DIR=2`,\n  `RING_PER_PROJECT=6`). Uncapped \"40 most recent\" gave 22 of 40 from a single\n  06:34 generated batch and buried every real deliverable. Verified: capped = 13\n  projects.\n- **`RING_EXCLUDE` keeps `COG_S2_Position_Reports` off the ring** — those filenames\n  carry athlete names and the ring renders filenames as labels, so a glance would\n  put named cognitive results on screen. Excluded from this view only.\n  See [[s2-cognition-analysis]].\n- **`SENSITIVE_PATHS` is module-level now**, shared by `src_graph` and the ring, so\n  the graph count and the ring can never disagree about what is off-limits. Still\n  must match `gf_gemini.py`.\n- **Calendar and email cannot be on this page.** Both live behind MCP servers that\n  only exist inside a Claude session; a background HTTP server cannot reach them.\n  Caching them would break the never-stale rule the page is built on. They became\n  System Health and Projects instead.\n- `launchctl kickstart -k` leaves **exit `-15`** on the previous instance. Expected —\n  judge it on the live PID, which `src_agents` already does.\n\n- **To verify `/os` renders, use headless Chrome — not the claude-in-chrome\n  extension.** The extension returns `Frame with ID 0 is showing error page` for\n  `localhost:7373` on both `localhost` and `127.0.0.1`, while `curl` gets 200 and\n  the server logs nothing. That is the extension's site-permission gate, **not** a\n  server fault — do not go hunting for an IPv4/IPv6 bind bug (the server is\n  IPv4-only by design, `server.py:878`, and that is not the cause). Working check:\n  `\"/Applications/Google Chrome.app/Contents/MacOS/Google Chrome\" --headless \\\n  --disable-gpu --virtual-time-budget=6000 --screenshot=<out>.png \\\n  --window-size=1440,900 http://127.0.0.1:7373/os`\n  **`--virtual-time-budget` is required** — without it the shot fires before the\n  `/api/state.json` fetch resolves and every panel reads `LOADING`, which looks\n  exactly like a broken board.\n\nRelated: [[graphify-semantic-extraction]], [[dev-layout-and-discovery]],\n[[s2-cognition-analysis]]\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/project_dcg-guard-blocks.md\n\n---\nname: dcg-guard-blocks\ndescription: \"The dcg command guard blocks whole compound commands — which shapes trip it, and the rewrite that gets the work done\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: 80892ff4-3719-4a85-aa22-4eaf3500081d\n  modified: 2026-09-13T06:16:36.810Z\n---\n\nThe `dcg` guard sits in front of Bash and rejects a command **before any of it runs**.\nIt blocks the *whole* invocation, not the offending line — so a `set -e` script with\nfour good steps and one blocked step accomplishes nothing, silently.\n\n**Shapes confirmed to block (2026-09-07):**\n\n- `mv` where either side mentions `~`, `$HOME`, `/etc`, `/usr`, `/var`, `/home`.\n  Rule `core.filesystem:mv-sensitive-source-root-home`. It is guarding the\n  \"relocate then delete\" bypass (`mv /etc /tmp/x && rm -rf /tmp/x`), so it fires\n  on shape alone — an ordinary `mv ~/foo ~/DEV/foo` is caught.\n  **Fix: write both paths absolute** — `mv /Users/kristinarash/foo /Users/kristinarash/DEV/foo`\n  passes. Verified on four real moves.\n- `git branch -d` / `-D` / `-M` / `-C`. Rule `core.git:branch-force-delete`.\n  No rewrite gets past this one — deleting a merged branch needs the user to run it.\n  **Do not bundle it with a push**: a `git push … ; git branch -d …` one-liner is\n  rejected as a unit and the push never happens.\n\n- **Truncating `>` redirects into home paths (2026-09-13)**, e.g.\n  `cmd > ~/.claude/jobs/…/out.txt` or `cat a b > ~/…/c.md`. Rule\n  `core.filesystem:redirect-truncate-root-home`. Unlike `mv`, **absolute paths do\n  NOT pass** — `> /Users/kristinarash/...` was blocked too. `2>&1` alone is fine.\n  **Fix:** Write a small Python file with the Write tool that opens the output\n  file itself (`subprocess.run(..., stdout=fh)`), then run that file. For a quick\n  compare, use process substitution (`comm <(a) <(b)`) with no file at all.\n- `rm -rf` inside heredocs, and `git` inside `$(…)`, also block (seen 2026-09-12).\n\n**The habit that follows:** when a multi-step shell job touches home paths or refs,\nrun the steps as separate calls, or at minimum keep any guard-prone step out of the\nsame invocation as work you need to land. Then verify — a blocked command produces\nan error, but a *partially* useful compound produces nothing at all.\n\n`dcg explain \"<command>\"` prints the rule and suggested alternatives.\n\nRelated: [[zsh-shell-traps]], [[keystoneos-verification-habits]]\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/project_dev-layout-and-discovery.md\n\n---\nname: dev-layout-and-discovery\ndescription: \"How DEV projects must be laid out to stay visible to KeystoneOS discovery, and the gitignored-subtree trap that silently voids backups\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: ea41d487-7812-4180-a6a8-f76a99753f83\n  modified: 2026-07-29T13:05:18.996Z\n---\n\nEach project gets its own folder in `~/DEV`. KeystoneOS is a map and\ncontrol plane, not a monorepo — it maps projects by reference and never holds\ntheir source (`AGENTS.md` operating rules).\n\n**Depth limit.** `data/discovery-roots.json` scans `~/DEV` at `maxDepth 2`,\nso `DEV/Project` and `DEV/Group/Project` are discovered but `DEV/Group/Sub/Project`\nis invisible to `keystone.ps1 inventory` — it still works as code, it just never\nappears in the registry or dashboard. One level of grouping (e.g. `Football\\`) is\nsafe; two is not.\n\n**The trap worth remembering:** never put a project under a path whose whole\nsubtree is gitignored. On 2026-07-28 two real projects were found living in\n`KeystoneOS/outputs/`, which `.gitignore` covers with `outputs/**`. 53 of their 54\nfiles existed only on one laptop, including files edited three weeks earlier. The\ngitignore was correct for generated output; the mistake was storing source there.\nThe folder that looked most \"inside the system\" was the least protected thing in\nthe repo.\n\n**Concurrency caveat.** Agent sessions (Claude and Codex) commit to DEV repos\nwhile you are reading them — `SportScienceResearchRepo` moved HEAD and dropped\nfrom 47 dirty files to 0 mid-analysis on 2026-07-28. Re-verify git numbers at the\npoint you act on them, not from a scan taken minutes earlier.\n\nThe root's display name in that config is still the Windows-era string\n\"Desktop DEV\" — a label, not a path. The `path` field is `/Users/kristinarash/DEV`.\n\nRelated: [[keystoneos-verification-habits]]\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/project_dormant-threads.md\n\n---\nname: dormant-threads\ndescription: \"Parked (not dead) work threads with the exact open item each one stopped on, so they can resume without re-discovery\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: 262b8cae-aa37-4d79-b911-c0c4b636fd83\n  modified: 2026-09-04T00:20:43.875Z\n---\n\nThreads Eric parked deliberately. Each one has a specific open item; none is finished.\n\n- **Tezino CampPrep** — parked 2026-06-17. Open items: FC25 P4–P9 gap, DL weight, surgery date.\n- **FaceComps sim** — LOCKED 2026-06-15. Do not modify without Eric saying so.\n\n**Why:** These left the working-memory scratchpad on 2026-09-03 to free headroom under its 2,500-char cap. The facts still matter when either thread wakes up.\n\n**How to apply:** If Eric mentions Tezino, camp prep, or FaceComps, open this file first, then look for the project under `~/DEV` (see [[dev-layout-and-discovery]]). Resume from the open item listed here rather than re-auditing the project.\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/project_drive-fuse-rsync-mtime-loop.md\n\n---\nname: drive-fuse-rsync-mtime-loop\ndescription: \"Google Drive CloudStorage rounds mtimes it didn't write, making rsync -a re-send byte-identical files forever; fix is --modify-window=5\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: fa062f93-2c55-4016-b9c6-f1a32e0a7083\n  modified: 2026-08-15T03:03:01.294Z\n---\n\nAnything rsyncing into the Google Drive for Desktop mount (`~/Library/CloudStorage/GoogleDrive-*/`) needs `--modify-window=5`. Drive stores an mtime 1–2s below what rsync set, and for large files it stamps its own upload time instead (skew of hours). rsync's quick-check is size+mtime, so byte-identical files read as changed and are re-sent on **every** run, permanently.\n\nDiagnosed 2026-08-14 on `daily-backup.ps1` → `codex/sessions`: all 256 files size-identical, yet 134 re-sent nightly (252 MB). That perpetual write load into FUSE is what fed the wedge the watchdog was catching — the snapshot had been failing since 2026-08-06 (rsync exit 23, then 1, then 124 once the watchdog landed).\n\n**Why:** `du` on the mount reports less than the source (171M vs 289M) because Drive doesn't materialize every file — that discrepancy looks like an incomplete mirror and sends you hunting for missing data that isn't missing. Compare `stat -f%z` per file, not `du`.\n\n**How to apply:**\n- Diagnose with dry runs, not `du`: `rsync -an -i <src>/ <dst>/ | grep -c '^>f'` with and without `--modify-window=5`. A large gap between the two *is* the diagnosis.\n- Files already stamped with Drive's own upload time need a one-time `touch -r <src> <dst>`; the flag alone won't close a multi-hour skew. The repair sticks across Drive re-sync.\n- Never substitute `--size-only` — `claude-projects` has genuine same-size edits that only mtime catches.\n- Genuinely-new files re-send under both settings (gemini's 428 were all NEW). If the count is identical with and without the window, it's real work, not this bug.\n\n**`--modify-window` has a blind spot — cover it for SQLite (found 2026-08-15 while reviewing the fix).** The window makes rsync's quick-check tolerate a 5s mtime difference. That is safe for append-only logs, where any real change also moves the size. It is NOT safe for files rewritten in place at identical size — SQLite updates rows inside existing pages, so `~/.codex/sqlite/codex-dev.db` (229,376 B) can change content with size unchanged. If that write lands within 5s of the dest's Drive-rounded mtime, rsync skips it, and keeps skipping forever because neither side changes again.\n\nReproduced: two 8-byte files, same size, content differing, mtimes 3s apart → `--modify-window=5` copies **0** files (silent loss); adding `--checksum` copies **1**. Fix in `daily-backup.ps1`: `Invoke-Rsync`/`Snapshot` take an `$extraArgs` param and the `.codex/sqlite` snapshot alone passes `--checksum`. Do not apply `--checksum` broadly — it re-reads every byte on both sides; it is cheap only because that DB is ~224 KB.\n\n**How to apply:** when adding a new `Snapshot` call, ask whether the source can change without changing size. Databases, fixed-width records, and image/binary formats with in-place headers all can — give those `--checksum`. Logs and transcripts cannot — leave them on the window.\n\nRelated: [[backup-sync-system]]\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/project_fireflies-cli-broken-on-mac.md\n\n---\nname: fireflies-cli-broken-on-mac\ndescription: \"fireflies-pp-cli's misleading \\\"out of memory (14)\\\" error means the local DB was never synced — not a real failure\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: 412a89e3-b64f-44e6-9c87-4766d44e6c7a\n  modified: 2026-08-29T13:01:27.698Z\n---\n\n**Resolved 2026-08-11.** Recording the trap because the error message points the wrong way.\n\n`fireflies-pp-cli` failed on **every** command and every `--data-source` (including\n`live`) with:\n\n```\nError: querying: unable to open database file: out of memory (14)\n```\n\n\"Out of memory\" is wrong — SQLite 14 is `SQLITE_CANTOPEN`. The real cause: the local\nmirror had **never been created on this Mac**. Two things were missing after the\nWindows → Mac migration:\n\n1. No `FIREFLIES_API_KEY` anywhere — not env, not disk, not Keychain.\n2. No database. `sync` had never been run here.\n\n**`--data-source live` does not bypass the database.** Read commands open it regardless,\nso \"live\" fails identically until a sync exists. That is what makes the error so\nmisleading — it looks like a corrupt/locked DB rather than a missing one.\n\n**Fix:** the key is in the login Keychain as of 2026-08-13, not plaintext in\n`~/.zshrc`. `~/.zshrc` exports it by *reading* the Keychain:\n`export FIREFLIES_API_KEY=\"$(security find-generic-password -s FIREFLIES_API_KEY -a \"$USER\" -w)\"`.\nThen `fireflies-pp-cli sync`. See [[path-shadowing-and-cleanup]].\nSynced 189 transcripts / 200 records on first run.\n\n**The DB lives at `~/.local/share/fireflies-pp-cli/data.db`** (XDG data dir), *not*\n`~/.fireflies-pp-cli/` — that directory exists but stays empty and is a red herring.\n\n**Shell gotcha:** `~/.zshrc` exports are invisible to Claude Code's Bash tool (it runs\nnon-interactive bash and does not source zshrc). Load them explicitly per command:\n`eval \"$(grep -m1 '^[[:space:]]*export[[:space:]]\\+VARNAME' ~/.zshrc)\"`\n\n**Knock-on effect worth remembering:** `meeting-sync` hard-stops on auth failure and\n`weekly-review` calls it first, so this single missing credential silently disabled both\nrituals. The vault meeting corpus froze at 2026-08-03 and `Inbox/weekly-review/` went\nempty after 2026-07-31. A lapse that reads as a discipline problem was a missing env var.\nDesign rule: recurring skills must **degrade and report staleness**, never hard-stop.\n\n---\n\n## Transcript text requires `--live` (added 2026-08-13)\n\n`transcripts get <id>` returns metadata, speakers, and the AI summary but **no\n`sentences` array** — and neither does the local DB (`json_extract(data,'$.sentences')`\nis null) nor `transcripts export`, which emits only the summary (~2.6 KB).\n\nTo get the actual dialogue, add `--live`:\n\n```\nfireflies-pp-cli transcripts get <id> --live --json\n```\n\nThat returned 476 sentences / 284 KB for a 39-minute meeting, vs 6 KB without it. So\n`sync` does **not** mirror transcript bodies — only metadata and summaries. Plan any\ntranscript-reading work around one `--live` call, then condense locally (group\nconsecutive sentences by `speaker_name` into turns; 476 sentences collapsed to 74 turns\n/ 37 KB, which is readable in one pass).\n\nAlso: **speaker names in the AI summary can be wrong.** In the 8/13 meeting the summary\nnamed \"Aaron\" and \"Carrie\"; the actual `speakers` array had **Erin Boyette**, and Carrie\nnever spoke. Trust `speakers` / `sentences`, not `summary.action_items`.\n\n**Correction to the shell gotcha above:** as of 2026-08-13 `FIREFLIES_API_KEY` *is*\nvisible to the Bash tool — `doctor` reported `auth_source: env:FIREFLIES_API_KEY` with\nno manual sourcing. Verify before working around it.\n\nRelated: [[status-report-sourcing]], [[gws-forms-api]]\n\n\n---\n\n## Renaming meetings: both the CLI and the MCP tool are broken (added 2026-08-29)\n\nTwo independent failures, and the second one lies about succeeding.\n\n**1. `fireflies-pp-cli transcripts update` always fails.** It sends the id as a bare\nmutation argument; the API wants it wrapped in an input object:\n\n```\nCLI sends: updateMeetingTitle(id: ..., title: ...)\nAPI wants: updateMeetingTitle(input: {id: ..., title: ...})\n```\n\nResult is `HTTP 400 ... Unknown argument \"id\" on field \"Mutation.updateMeetingTitle\"`.\nNote the field really is `id`, **not** `meetingId` — a direct call with `meetingId`\nreturns `Field \"UpdateMeetingTitleInput.id\" of required type \"String!\" was not provided`.\nDon't \"fix\" the CLI by renaming the field; the shape is what's wrong.\n\n**2. The Fireflies MCP tool `fireflies_update_meeting_title` is unreliable and its\n`success:true` means nothing.** Writes are *erratically delayed*, not simply dropped:\non 2026-08-29 only 2 of 7 renames persisted promptly, 5 returned success and had not\nlanded after 8 minutes of polling, and one diagnostic write (\"… v2\") surfaced roughly\n20–30 minutes later. So a \"failed\" MCP rename can still land an hour on and clobber a\ngood title. **This looked exactly like a rate limit and is not one** —\n`name-meetings/REFERENCE.md` documents a \"hard daily rate limit\", which sent the\ndiagnosis down a 45-minute dead end. Never use MCP for renames; never send a throwaway\n\"test\" title through it, because late arrival makes the probe destructive.\n\n**Working path — call GraphQL directly with the keychain API key:**\n\n```python\nq = \"mutation($input: UpdateMeetingTitleInput!){ updateMeetingTitle(input:$input){ title } }\"\n# POST https://api.fireflies.ai/graphql, Authorization: Bearer <FIREFLIES_API_KEY>\n# variables: {\"input\": {\"id\": <meetingId>, \"title\": <newTitle>}}\n```\n\nAll 7 renames applied first try this way, verified by read-back. Reusable script:\n`~/.claude/jobs/*/tmp/rename_direct.py` (regenerate it; job dirs are cleaned up).\n\n**Verification traps:**\n- `transcripts get <id> --data-source live --no-cache` is trustworthy and is the\n  read-back to use. `--no-cache` matters.\n- The **list** endpoint (what `sync` + `transcripts find` read) lags the single-transcript\n  endpoint by minutes. A fresh rename can be correct live and stale in `find` after a\n  `sync --full`. Wait ~4 min and re-sync before exporting, or the vault filename bakes in\n  the old title.\n- Never verify a rename by exit code or by the mutation's own response.\n\n**Backgrounded polling scripts appear to produce no output** until they exit — the file\nstays 0 bytes mid-run. Don't conclude the script died; read it again after it completes.\n\nRelated: [[fireflies-cli-broken-on-mac]]\n\n---\n\n## `transcripts find --json` returns a bare LIST (added 2026-09-02)\n\n`fireflies-pp-cli transcripts find --json --agent` emits a **top-level JSON array**, not an\nenvelope object. A parser written as `d.get(\"data\") or d.get(\"items\") or d` dies with\n`AttributeError: 'list' object has no attribute 'get'`. Guard with\n`items = d if isinstance(d, list) else d.get(\"data\", [])`.\n\n**The rename → export loop works end to end** (verified 2026-09-02, 3 meetings):\n`scripts/rename.py <id> \"<title>\"` → `sleep 270` → `sync --full --resources transcripts`\n→ `find` shows new titles → `meeting-sync/scripts/sync.py --no-sync` exports with the\ngood slug. The 4-minute wait is mandatory; skipping it bakes the stale title into the\nvault filename. Run the sleep as a **background** Bash job — the foreground tool caps at 120s.\n\n**Vault header placement:** `assets/meeting-header.md` goes after the metadata block's\n`---` rule (line 9 of the export), *before* `## Summary` — not at line 1. Exported files\nhave no YAML frontmatter, so \"after frontmatter\" reads as line 1 and would be wrong;\nmatch the 237 existing files instead.\n\n**Open to-do (moved out of Tier B working memory 2026-09-09): rotate the Fireflies API\nkey.** Rotate it in the Fireflies dashboard, then update the stored secret — secrets on\nthis machine live in the macOS Keychain, never in `~/.zshrc`.\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/project_github-consolidation.md\n\n---\nname: github-consolidation\ndescription: \"How ~/DEV maps to GitHub after the 2026-09-09 consolidation — the thin root repo, _ARCHIVE, DexaProject's new home, and the traps that bit\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: 7755f8f2-3158-4d2a-8623-b9e9b0bb0679\n  modified: 2026-09-09T23:55:25.460Z\n---\n\nOn 2026-09-09 every project under `~/DEV` was put on GitHub under `erash11`\n(private by default). 128 folders: git, committed, pushed. Windows had pushed\n~70 repos earlier the same day, so most folders hit a repo that already existed.\n\n**`~/DEV` is itself a thin repo** — `erash11/DEV`. It tracks only the 8 router\n`.md` files plus `AGENTS.md`, `docs/STATUS.md`, `docs/WINDOWS-SYNC.md`. Its\n`.gitignore` begins with `/*`, which is the only thing stopping it from\nswallowing all 128 nested repos. **Never remove that line.** `DEV/CLAUDE.md` is\ndeliberately NOT the usual one-line `@AGENTS.md` pointer — it is the workspace\nmap and Claude Code loads it directly.\n\n**`~/DEV/_ARCHIVE/`** is the parking lot: course downloads never used\n(`token-dashboard`, `AI_Analyst`) and duplicate second checkouts. It is\nintentionally not a git repo, so any sweep skips it. Everything inside is\nalready on GitHub, so nothing there is unbacked-up.\n\n**DexaProject left `~/DEV`.** Files now at\n`~/Library/CloudStorage/GoogleDrive-eric.rash11@gmail.com/My Drive/00_Primary_Storage_Drive/DexaProject`,\ngit remote renamed to `DISABLED-do-not-push`. It holds a 3,505-row CSV with\nathlete names, birthdates, addresses and insurance. See [[dev-layout-and-discovery]].\n\n## Traps that actually bit\n- **`--no-rebase` is a `git pull` flag, not `git merge`.** Passing it to merge\n  fails silently-ish; every merge no-ops and every push is then rejected as\n  non-fast-forward. The symptom looks like a conflict storm; it is not.\n- **Unrelated histories make identical files conflict.** Windows-created repos\n  share no ancestor with the Mac's, so every common path is an add/add\n  conflict even when the bytes match. Of 663 conflicts, 417 were byte-identical.\n- **Most \"real\" conflicts were CRLF.** Of the 36 non-scaffolding conflicts, 26\n  differed only by line endings. Normalise with `tr -d '\\r'` before judging.\n- **`.gitignore` must be unioned, not chosen.** Keeping the Mac side dropped the\n  Windows ignore rules, re-arming the 100MB push limit on that machine.\n- **`gitignore` cannot filter by size.** Every file >95MB needs its explicit path.\n- **A repo can enforce its own convention.** `Football/PFF_Data_Project` has a\n  pre-commit hook requiring `CLAUDE.md` to be a full copy of `AGENTS.md`.\n- Long inline shell with `git -C $var` inside command substitution trips the dcg\n  guard. Write a script file and run it. See [[dcg-guard-blocks]].\n\n## Open\n- DONE 9/9: `erash11/DexaProject` DELETED from GitHub. Before deleting, its one\n  remote-only commit (Windows scaffolding) was merged into the Google Drive copy,\n  so nothing was lost. Nothing depended on the repo: no submodules, no code,\n  0 forks/issues. `Tezino_CampPrep` names DEXA only to record it as a dead end\n  (the .mdb held demographics only, 0 scan rows across 3,504 patients).\n  The ONE live dependency was `~/.claude/scripts/backup-manifest.json`, which had\n  `github` as a destination — that is what pushed the patient CSV up nightly.\n  It now points at the Google Drive path with destinations `[\"ssd\"]` only.\n  **If a DEXA path ever reappears in that manifest with a non-ssd destination,\n  that is a regression.**\n- SETTLED 9/9: `SportScienceResearchRepo` stays PUBLIC. Flipping it breaks the\n  GitHub Pages site, and Eric is not distributing or charging for access. The\n  ~4,700 PDFs are a personal reading library. Do not re-raise this.\n- RESOLVED 9/9: `CFB_Victory_Model/data/weekly_forecast_log.csv` merged.\n  Mac had the 09-02 capture, Windows had 08-26 and 08-31. Union on\n  `(captured_at, season, team, week, game_id)` → 540 rows, zero conflicts,\n  nothing dropped. See [[cfb-forecast-capture]].\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/project_graphify-runtime-setup.md\n\n---\nname: graphify-runtime-setup\ndescription: \"graphify's Python package lives in a venv inside the skill dir, not globally; SKILL.md's own pip install step fails under PEP 668\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: 53bc7e39-8ea3-4b0c-9787-4ed6d66f5acc\n  modified: 2026-09-12T22:33:10.641Z\n---\n\n**Set up 2026-08-29.** The `graphify` skill's Step 1 says `pip install graphifyy` — that\n**fails on this Mac** (Python 3.14 is PEP 668-managed). The package is instead installed\nin a dedicated venv:\n\n```\n/Users/kristinarash/.claude/skills/graphify/.venv/bin/python\n```\n\nRun every `scripts/*.py` step with that interpreter, not bare `python3`. `graphify install`\ndoes not touch the venv, so an upgrade leaves it intact.\n\n**Since 2026-09-12 there is ONE skill copy.** `~/.agents/skills/graphify` and\n`~/.codex/skills/graphify` are symlinks to `~/.claude/skills/graphify/` (made by\n`sync-to-codex.sh`). Upgrade with `--platform claude` ONLY.\n\n**Never run `graphify install --platform agents` now.** Its installer writes\n`~/.agents/skills/graphify/SKILL.md` and `references/` by path, so it follows the link and\noverwrites the canonical Claude copy with the Codex variant (\"Task tool\", \"AGENTS.md\").\nThe daily backup would then commit that. Before 9/12 the advice was \"update both\"; that\nadvice is now wrong. The old Codex variant is archived at\n`~/.agents/skills-archive/pre-sync-2026-09-12/graphify`.\n(Two more stamps exist under `~/.codex-migration-backups/` — those are backups, leave them.)\n\n**The old manifest was Windows-era.** A `--update` in `~/SecondBrain/Inbox/meetings` reported\nall 264 files as new because the June manifest held `graphify-out\\GRAPH_REPORT.md`\n(backslash). After a path migration, `--update` is not incremental — plan a full rebuild.\n\n**Two traps when rebuilding a folder that already has a graph:**\n1. Move the old `graphify-out` **outside** the corpus before re-detecting. Left as a sibling\n   backup inside the folder it inflated the corpus from 265 to 327 files (its cache JSON got\n   scanned as code).\n2. The semantic cache keys on file paths, so a path change makes it a 0% hit — budget for\n   full re-extraction, not a cheap top-up.\n\n**Extraction agents can die mid-write.** A session rate limit killed 3 of 12 agents and left\none chunk's JSON truncated. Do not re-run blindly — a truncated chunk is usually salvageable\nby `raw_decode`-ing each complete object out of the `nodes`/`edges` arrays and dropping the\npartial tail. That recovered all 22 files of the dead chunk with nothing lost.\n\nRelated: [[dev-layout-and-discovery]]\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/project_graphify-semantic-extraction.md\n\n---\nname: graphify-semantic-extraction\ndescription: \"graphify's Gemini semantic layer: one oversized data-dump file — not the API quota — is what stalls a run; plus per-model quota buckets, rc=0 on 429, and a merge check that under-reports\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: b7d080c1-e0f8-4eb5-b181-d06b87224449\n  modified: 2026-08-31T14:36:03.171Z\n---\n\n**Measured 2026-08-31 finishing the `~/DEV` graph's semantic layer.**\n\n**The blocker was file size, not quota.** Two converted office files were 99% of the\noffice corpus by bytes: a **185 MB** `25-26 KINEXON_*.md` GPS telemetry export and an\n11 MB Baylor medical diagnostics report. graphify slices every file at\n`_FILE_CHAR_CAP = 20_000` chars, so that one 185 MB export expanded to **2,319 LLM\nchunks**. A free-tier model gets 20 requests/day, so every prior pass spent its whole\nbudget on one spreadsheet and never reached a real document. The other 81 office docs\nwere **1.2 MB combined** and finished in minutes.\n**Run `os.path.getsize` over the queue before believing a quota diagnosis.**\n\nThat medical file was queued to be sent to Google. `~/DEV/CLAUDE.md` \"Handling care\"\nforbids it. `gf_gemini.py` now gates on a `SENSITIVE` deny-list plus `GF_MAXBYTES`\n(1.5 MB) **before** dispatch.\n\n**Gemini free tier is 20 req/day AND 5 req/min, PER MODEL** — per model is the lever,\nso rotating models multiplies the budget. **Aliases share a bucket:**\n`gemini-flash-latest` → `gemini-3.7-flash`, `gemini-3.1-flash-lite-preview` →\n`gemini-3.1-flash-lite`. Retrying an alias after its concrete model dies can never\nwork. `gemini-2.5-flash` and `gemini-2.5-flash-lite` return zero nodes with no 429 —\nnot usable. That leaves ~6 real buckets ≈ **120 free requests/day**, which moved\n214 → 453 files in one day.\n\n**Two failure modes that waste hours:**\n- **A Gemini quota failure exits rc=0.** A loop that breaks only on nonzero rc will\n  happily burn every batch against a dead model. Grep the output for `429` and\n  `attribution: 0/` instead.\n- **`GRAPHIFY_MAX_RETRIES` defaults to 6** with exponential backoff. Against a daily\n  quota 429 (which never recovers within the day) that is ~20 min of pure waiting per\n  dead model. Set it to 3; a dead model then rotates in ~2 min.\n\n**`build_from_json` deterministically re-keys every non-AST node id** from its own\n`source_file` (graphify #1504/#1509). So comparing a producer file's raw ids against\n`graph.json` ids **under-reports merge completeness** — it read 62% for a layer that\nwas actually 96% merged. Count a node present if its id survived **or** its\n`(source_file, label)` pair is in the graph. Check the measurement before chasing a\ncompleteness scare.\n\n**Verification caught more defects in the FIXES than in the original work.** Three\nCodex rounds: round 3 found the real privacy hole (a `SENSITIVE` gate that covered\nlive dispatch but not the semantic *cache*, so pre-gate health-data nodes stayed\nin the sidecar); rounds 4 and 5 then found five defects introduced *while fixing*\nit. The transferable rules:\n\n- **A wrong provenance link is worse than a missing one.** Never repair an\n  unresolved `source_file` by bare-basename fallback, and never on a match with\n  under two directory levels unless the basename is corpus-unique — both produced\n  confident mis-attributions.\n- **Never mass-deduplicate on `(source_file, label)`.** It looked like a clean\n  invariant and deleted 454 legitimate nodes; AST symbols like `__init__` recur\n  many times inside one file by design. Collapse only collisions your own change\n  created.\n- **Scrubbing must never sit behind a credential check or an early exit.** A purge\n  placed after `sys.exit(2)` silently does nothing on a keyless run.\n- **Match a sensitivity rule against the path the policy names, not node text.** A\n  whole-node text scan flagged the user's own aggregate injury write-up, which\n  merely cites a report and holds no identifiable data.\n- Ask the verifier to hunt for **new** defects and hand it the previous report;\n  one that only re-checks the original claims misses what the fix broke.\n\nDurable runbook and the full trap list live in `~/DEV/graphify-out/RESUME.md`;\n`scripts/rotate_loop.sh` is the one command that resumes it.\n\nRelated: [[graphify-runtime-setup]], [[dev-layout-and-discovery]]\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/project_gws-forms-api.md\n\n---\nname: gws-forms-api\ndescription: \"gws CLI can create and populate Google Forms with only the drive scope — no forms.body scope needed; create is title-only, items require batchUpdate\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: 05f058aa-4fa5-47d6-82ee-5c1357733538\n  modified: 2026-08-14T00:40:43.648Z\n---\n\nThe `gws` CLI (`/opt/homebrew/bin/gws`, personal account eric.rash11@gmail.com) can\ncreate and fully populate Google Forms even though **no `forms.body` scope is\ngranted**. The 12 granted scopes include `https://www.googleapis.com/auth/drive`,\nand that is sufficient for both `forms forms create` and `forms forms batchUpdate`.\n\n**Why:** worth recording because the Contacts API on this same auth returns 403 for a\nmissing scope (see [[fireflies-cli-broken-on-mac]] for the sibling env traps), so the\nreasonable expectation is that Forms would fail too. It doesn't. Don't skip Forms work\non a scope assumption — try it.\n\n**How to apply:**\n\n- `gws forms forms create --json '{\"info\":{\"title\":\"...\",\"documentTitle\":\"...\"}}'`\n  accepts **title only**. The API rejects description, items, and settings on create.\n- Everything else goes through `forms forms batchUpdate` with a `requests` array:\n  `updateFormInfo` (with `updateMask`) for the description, then `createItem` with an\n  incrementing `location.index` per question.\n- Item shapes: `questionItem.question.textQuestion{paragraph:bool}`,\n  `...choiceQuestion{type: RADIO|CHECKBOX|DROP_DOWN, options:[{value}]}`,\n  and `pageBreakItem` for a section.\n- Editing an existing question needs `updateItem` with the item's `itemId`, the\n  question's `questionId`, `location.index`, **and** an `updateMask` such as\n  `questionItem.question.choiceQuestion.options`. Omitting itemId/questionId appends a\n  duplicate instead of editing in place.\n- `--dry-run` validates locally before sending. Use it; batchUpdate is not idempotent\n  and a re-run duplicates every item.\n- New forms are created **published and accepting responses**. On a personal account\n  that is a loose end — unpublish or delete after use.\n- Gmail drafts work the same way via `gmail users drafts create/update` with a\n  base64url RFC822 `message.raw`. Passing an existing draft `id` to `update` edits in\n  place rather than creating a duplicate.\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/project_headless-claude-tool-deny-gap.md\n\n---\nname: headless-claude-tool-deny-gap\ndescription: \"`claude -p --disallowedTools Bash` still runs shell commands through context-mode MCP tools — a headless smoke test is not read-only\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: 9823c1f0-89f5-4638-9223-c6892607ac2b\n  modified: 2026-09-13T06:16:42.437Z\n---\n\n`claude -p \"...\" --disallowedTools Bash` does **not** stop shell execution. The\ncontext-mode plugin's MCP tools (`ctx_execute`, `ctx_batch_execute`,\n`ctx_execute_file`) run shell commands and are not covered by denying `Bash`.\nSeen 2026-09-12: a headless \"audit my AI OS\" smoke test with Bash denied still\nran `aios-metrics.py` through the MCP shell.\n\n**Why:** a smoke test believed to be read-only can run, and in principle write,\nanything the MCP shell can.\n\n**How to apply:** for a truly read-only headless run, also deny the\n`mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__*` tools (or disable the plugin). Then\ncheck for side effects afterwards (git status, memory dirs). Never report a headless\nrun as \"no side effects possible\" on the Bash deny alone.\n\nRelated: [[dcg-guard-blocks]], [[keystoneos-verification-habits]]\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/project_hook-two-copies-trap.md\n\n---\nname: hook-two-copies-trap\ndescription: \"A Claude Code hook patch works, then silently reverts — hooks live in two directories and both must be patched\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: 9422b1a6-8624-4c86-a4f6-3c4b1a4c9fc8\n  modified: 2026-09-09T15:26:04.471Z\n---\n\nThe **entire hook directory is mirrored** across two harnesses — not one stray file.\nVerified 2026-09-09: ~55 near-identical entries in each of\n\n- `~/.claude/hooks/` — Claude Code\n- `~/.codex/hooks/` — Codex\n\n**Patch both, always.** Editing one leaves the other on the old code. Whichever\nharness runs next re-triggers the bug, which reads as \"the fix reverted\".\n\nThe two are not exact mirrors, so do not assume drift means a failed patch:\n`.claude/hooks/` additionally holds `herdr-agent-state.sh` and an older\n`memsearch-sync.js.bak-20260814`. Those are legitimate, not drift.\n\nWorked example — `transcript-capture.js` (Stop hook):\nCodex invokes it with a null `transcript_path`, which the script did not handle.\nPatched 2026-09-07 to exit quietly on null instead of erroring.\n**Tell that a copy drifted:** `missing transcript_path` lines reappear in\n`~/.claude/global-memory/transcripts/.errors.log`. When they do, diff the two\ncopies before debugging the script itself.\n\nVerify after any hook edit — one file, or the whole set:\n\n```bash\ndiff ~/.claude/hooks/transcript-capture.js ~/.codex/hooks/transcript-capture.js\ndiff -rq ~/.claude/hooks ~/.codex/hooks     # whole set; expect only the two known extras\n```\n\nHook *failures* surface at session start via `.errors.log` — see\n[[keystoneos-verification-habits]] for what a clean exit code does and does not\nprove, and [[backup-sync-system]] for the archive-before-clearing rule on that log.\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/project_kenny-boyd-doc-review-preferences.md\n\n---\nname: kenny-boyd-doc-review-preferences\ndescription: \"Kenny Boyd's editorial constraints on Athletics policy/framework docs — no language implying past incompetence, no judgmental tier labels, name the seniority of reviewers\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: 126c5de4-d928-4658-88d2-1922d2464a29\n  modified: 2026-08-11T23:13:32.529Z\n---\n\nKenny Boyd (reviewer on the Baylor Athletics Software and Technology Management\nFramework) consistently pushes back on three things in draft policy language:\n\n1. **No language that implies the department was previously incompetent.** He cut\n   \"so the department stops discovering it is in the final year of an agreement\n   with no time to act\" for exactly this reason. Rewrite as forward-looking\n   capability (\"advance visibility of contract end dates and the lead time to\n   evaluate options\"), not as a problem being fixed.\n2. **No judgmental or ranking-flavored labels.** He rejected the Light / Standard /\n   Elevated review tiers. Neutral, descriptive, escalating names work better —\n   Level 1 Standard / Level 2 Enhanced / Level 3 Comprehensive Review. He floated\n   Basic / Intermediate / Advanced, which reads as sophistication of the\n   *technology* rather than depth of *review*.\n3. **Name the seniority of the reviewer, not just the office.** For ITS he wanted\n   it clear that a supervisory-level person with authority to commit resources is\n   involved, not a line technician. Frame this as decision authority the request\n   requires, never as competence of the staff.\n\nHe also wants Baylor branding carried through headers/footers and draft status\nmarked on every page.\n\n**Why:** These are tone constraints, not content ones — the same facts land or\nfail on phrasing. Getting them wrong costs a full review cycle.\n\n**How to apply:** Before sending any Athletics policy or framework draft for his\nreview, scan for blame-implying rationale, ranking adjectives on process tiers,\nand unqualified office names where seniority matters.\n\nRelated: [[status-report-sourcing]]\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/project_keystoneos-verification-habits.md\n\n---\nname: keystoneos-verification-habits\ndescription: \"Where KeystoneOS state claims are authoritative vs misleading, and which readings have already produced wrong conclusions\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: ea41d487-7812-4180-a6a8-f76a99753f83\n  modified: 2026-09-12T13:34:11.490Z\n---\n\nThis system is well-instrumented, which makes its few misleading surfaces\ndangerous — they look authoritative. Check these before drawing conclusions.\n\n**\"Did a job run today?\"** Only `KeystoneOS Morning` runs through `run-job.ps1`;\nAgySync, DailyBackup, MsysKeepAlive, Reconcile, Rituals and StackDoctor invoke\ntheir scripts directly and write **no ledger row**. So `outputs/run-ledger.json`\nunder-reports. Correctness is fine — `Test-JobCompleteOn` checks the ledger *or*\nthe job's `successProbe`. Ask `.\\scripts\\keystone-reconcile.ps1 -DryRun -Json`,\nnever the ledger alone and never `Get-ScheduledTaskInfo`.\n\n**memsearch timeout streaks are not failures.** The SessionEnd hook is\ndeliberately best-effort (`daily-backup.ps1:122-125`), wired at `timeout: 50` in\n`~/.claude/settings.json`; the uncapped nightly absorbs backlog and resets the\ncounter. That nightly moved 00:30 → **09:00 on 2026-08-29**, so a streak now\nclears in the morning, not overnight. A streak of 16 on 2026-07-28 was\nread as a broken indexer and produced a whole plan step to \"fix\" it — the nightly\ncleared it the same night, exactly as designed. Alarm threshold is 6.\n\n**A scheduler's exit code is not the job's outcome.** It reports the last\n*scheduled* run, so a green log and a red status routinely coexist: a manual or\nout-of-band run never clears it, and a sleep/wake kill mid-run sets it while the\nday's work still completed on a later retry. Read the job's own log for a\nsame-day clean end before treating a red status as rot. Two recorded instances:\nWindows `KeystoneOS-DailyBackup` exit 3221225786 on 2026-08-01 (four killed\nstarts 08:27-11:32, clean end 16:36), and `launchctl list` on 2026-08-31 still\nshowing the failed 09:00 `com.keystone.dailybackup` after the corrected re-run\ncompleted with 0 errors. Both cleared themselves at the next scheduled run; a\nforced re-run would have been cosmetic. (Generalised from the retired\nWindows-only `backup-task-sleepkill-signature` memory, archived 2026-09-01.)\n\n**The test suite is main-checkout-only.** `Get-KeystoneDevRoot` resolves the DEV\nroot as `<repoRoot>\\..`, which from a worktree yields\n`KeystoneOS\\.claude\\worktrees\\`. `tests\\keystone-reconcile.ps1` therefore always\nthrows in a worktree. Baseline on main is 23 pass / 1 fail\n(`skill-verification-contracts.ps1`, unrelated). Always baseline against main\nbefore blaming a change.\n\n**Three claims failed verification in one session (2026-07-28/29):** that the\nregistry pointed at a stale clone, that the reconciler was wasting hours on\nredundant backups, and that the indexer was chronically broken. All three were\nplausible from a point-in-time reading and all three were wrong. State a claim\nabout this system only after re-running the check that produces it.\n\n**`canonicalPath` in `data/projects.json` must be a real filesystem path — never a URL.**\nLearned the hard way 2026-08-15: retiring a project, I set it to the GitHub URL so the field\nwould not point at a deleted directory. That took down the entire 06:00 morning cadence at\nthe dashboard step. `Join-Path`, `Get-Item`, `Resolve-Path`, `Get-ChildItem` and\n`Convert-Path` all throw *\"Cannot find drive. A drive with the name 'https' does not exist\"*\non a URL. **`Test-Path` is the trap: it returns `False` on a URL instead of throwing**, which\nis exactly why the `if (-not (Test-Path $Path))` guards in `Get-PathSummary` and\n`Get-GitSummary` sail past it and the failure lands somewhere further downstream.\n\nA path that simply *does not exist* is handled cleanly by those guards, so the correct value\nfor a retired project is its old directory; put the repo URL in a separate field\n(`restoreFrom`) that nothing treats as a path.\n\n**Recurrence 2026-09-12 — same class, cross-machine variant: a Windows drive letter.**\nThe 2026-09-09 GitHub consolidation merge left a **duplicate** `reMarkale_extract` entry in\n`data/projects.json` whose `canonicalPath` was `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\...`. On macOS\nthat throws *\"Cannot find drive. A drive with the name 'C' does not exist\"* — identical\nmechanism to the URL case, different scheme. It killed morning-cadence on **every run from\n2026-09-10 through 2026-09-12** (5 runs), and the failure was **invisible in the usual\nplaces**: `outputs/jobs/morning-cadence-*.{out,err}.log` were both 0 bytes and\n`morning-cadence.err` was empty. The only record was `outputs/morning/cadence.log` —\ngrep it for `cadence FAILED` first, not the job logs. Fixed by deleting the duplicate\n(the Mac entry was intact); BOM preserved on the write, see [[settings-json-bom-trap]].\n\n**The asymmetry is the durable lesson: this fault is one-way.** Windows tolerates a\n`/Users/...` path (`Test-Path` just returns `False`); macOS *throws* on `C:\\` under\n`$ErrorActionPreference = \"Stop\"`. So any shared JSON that both machines write will break\nthe Mac and never the Windows box — and it will recur every time Windows registers a new\nproject. Expect it after any cross-machine merge; grep shared `data/*.json` for\n`eric_rash` or `C:\\\\` as part of post-merge cleanup. See [[github-consolidation]].\n\n**How to apply:** after editing any `data/*.json` that KeystoneOS scripts consume, run the\nconsumers before trusting the edit — `scripts/keystone.ps1 dashboard`, `cockpit`, `snapshot`\nare quick and cover most path handling. A JSON-validity check proves nothing here; the file\nparsed fine and still broke the cadence. See [[morning-briefing-retired]].\n\nRelated: [[dev-layout-and-discovery]]\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/project_memory-compiler-pipeline.md\n\n---\nname: memory-compiler-pipeline\ndescription: How the claude-memory-compiler flush/index pipeline fails and how to tell which stage broke; SessionStart injection was rewritten to a pointer 2026-08-29\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: 174cde34-d23d-454b-8db8-9365e7f728f2\n  modified: 2026-08-29T20:05:21.521Z\n---\n\nThe compiler at `~/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/` has three stages. Each fails\ndifferently and the symptoms look alike, so identify the stage before fixing.\n\n**Stage 1 — `hooks/session-end.py`.** Writes `scripts/session-flush-<uuid>-<YYYYMMDD>-<HHMMSS>.md`,\nthen spawns `flush.py`. Logs every firing to `scripts/flush.log` as `SessionEnd fired`.\nIf `flush.log` has no such line for days but the hook works when run by hand, sessions\nare being *closed* rather than exited cleanly — not a config break. Self-verifying: end\none session normally and re-check the tail.\n\n**Stage 2 — `scripts/flush.py`.** Calls the Agent SDK, appends to `daily/<date>.md`, writes\n`scripts/last-flush.json` **only on success**, and **deletes its context file on success**.\nTherefore **every leftover `session-flush-*.md` is a flush that died**, and its datestamp\nmaps to a missing `daily/<date>.md`. That is the cheapest diagnostic in the whole system —\ncount the leftovers. A stale `last-flush.json` means flushes are *dying*, not that the hook\nnever fired. Recover one by hand:\n`uv run --directory ~/.claude/claude-memory-compiler python scripts/flush.py <context-file> <session-id> [YYYY-MM-DD]`\nSuccess = `Result: FLUSH_OK` + `Flush complete` in `flush.log`.\n\n**The third arg is a backfill date, added 2026-08-29.** Without it `flush.py` always writes\nto *today's* log, so recovering old stranded files silently misfiles them under the current\ndate. Always pass the date parsed from the filename when backfilling; entries are then\nmarked `(backfilled …)`.\n\n**Batch-recovery trap — the 60-second dedup guard destroys data.** `flush.py` skips a run\nwhose `session_id` matches `last-flush.json` within 60s, and **deletes the context file on\nthat skip path too**. One session can have several stranded files (different timestamps), so\na loop that hits the same uuid twice inside a minute silently loses the second one — it still\nprints as consumed. Hit 2026-08-29: 12 files ran, 10 flushed, 2 dropped. Before trusting a\nbatch, check `grep -c \"Skipping duplicate flush\" flush.log`, and either group runs so repeats\nof a uuid are >60s apart or run repeats individually.\nRecovery is possible because `~/.claude` is captured to the SSD: the deleted files came back\nfrom `/Volumes/CRASHBOX/AssistantBackup/claude/claude-memory-compiler/scripts/`.\n\n**Stage 3 — memsearch index.** `INDEX full set: Indexed 0 chunks` in the nightly log is\n**usually not an indexer fault** — it means nothing new arrived. A dead stage 2 starves it\nand looks identical. Check leftovers/`last-flush.json` before suspecting memsearch.\nmemsearch is a **uv tool**: binary `~/.local/bin/memsearch`, env\n`~/.local/share/uv/tools/memsearch/`. Test an import directly with that env's python\n(`.../memsearch/bin/python -c \"import onnxruntime\"`) rather than inferring from index counts.\nReinstall with `uv tool install --force memsearch`, never pip — and re-apply the local flush\npatch afterwards, which any reinstall strips.\n\n**`hooks/session-start.py` — rewritten 2026-08-29.** It used to paste all of\n`knowledge/index.md` into every session. That file is ~48k chars against a 20k cap, so the\nindex was truncated mid-table **and the `## Recent Daily Log` section was pushed off the end\nentirely** — the one part that is always relevant never arrived. It now emits a pointer\n(article counts + paths + the `memsearch search` command) and always includes the log:\n20,039 → 682 chars. If a session needs the index, read\n`~/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md` on demand. Backups of the pre-change\nfiles are `*.bak-2026-08-29` beside each.\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/project_memsearch-hook-timeout-cap.md\n\n---\nname: memsearch-hook-timeout-cap\ndescription: \"the SessionEnd memsearch hook's fixed 45s cap was outrun by corpus growth, so every session-end index timed out and incremental recall silently degraded to the 09:00 nightly; fixed 2026-09-02 by detaching the indexer\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  modified: 2026-09-02T12:33:29.526Z\n  originSessionId: 00fe7fc2-f53c-471d-b407-c777e8cc679c\n---\n\n`~/.claude/hooks/memsearch-sync.js` (SessionEnd) ran `memsearch index <10 paths>` through\n`spawnSync` with a **45s cap**, sized in July against a \"~17s fixed floor, ~5s incremental\"\nmeasurement. Measured again 2026-09-02 against the current corpus (~1,450 files;\n`SecondBrain/Inbox/meetings` alone is 593 and `SecondBrain/Knowledge` 334):\n\n| run | time |\n|---|---|\n| no-op, nothing new to embed | **33s** (was ~17s) |\n| one day of accumulated content | **278s** (232 chunks, ~1s/chunk) |\n\nSo the cap left ~12s of embedding budget — under a dozen new chunks. **Every ordinary\nsession-end index was timing out.** Incremental indexing had quietly degraded to \"whatever\nthe 09:00 nightly full reindex picks up\", leaving search up to 24h stale.\n\n**Why it stayed invisible for weeks.** Three dampers stacked:\n`daily-backup.ps1` resets `.memsearch-timeout-streak` every morning, the escalation\nthreshold is 6 consecutive timeouts, and the hook writes only to `.memsearch-index.log`\nbelow that. So a normal day of 2-4 session-ends never reached the threshold and never\nsurfaced. It took **six session-ends inside 47 minutes** (2026-09-01, 21:09-21:56) to\nproduce a 7-long streak and one `.errors.log` line. The alarm fired on burst *frequency*,\nnever on the underlying constant failure.\n\n**The misread that line invites:** ETIMEDOUT + a clustered burst pattern-matches\n[[milvus-lock-misdiagnosis]] (session-end lock contention), and the hook's own comments\nsay exactly that. It was not contention — `pgrep`/`lsof` showed no holder and the DB was\nhealthy. **Time the actual command before believing the comment.**\n\n**Fix (2026-09-02): detached background worker.** The hook now re-execs itself as\n`node memsearch-sync.js --worker` with `detached: true, stdio: 'ignore'` and `.unref()`,\nthen exits — measured **42ms**. The worker keeps the entire original body, so it owns the\nPID lock (clustered session-ends still elect one indexer), writes `INDEX_LOG`, and runs\nevery error branch. Its `spawnSync` cap is now 600s: a runaway guard, not a budget, so an\nETIMEDOUT there genuinely means \">10 minutes\".\n\n- `stdio: 'ignore'` is **not optional** — inherited or piped stdio keeps the harness's pipe\n  open and it waits for the child anyway, rebuilding the timeout with extra steps.\n- Self-respawn, **not** an `sh -c` wrapper: the wrapper would discard the lock ownership\n  and all four error-classification branches.\n- The old header justified staying synchronous with a **Windows** process-group race.\n  That rationale died with the 2026-08-05 migration; the comment now says so, because the\n  next reader would otherwise \"restore\" the bug.\n\n**How to apply:**\n- A fixed timeout sized against a measured corpus is a **dated** number. When the corpus is\n  something that grows every day (the vault, meetings, dailies), the cap has an expiry date\n  nobody writes down. Prefer detaching over raising a cap; if a cap must stay, record the\n  measurement and the date beside it so drift is visible.\n- Never size a SessionEnd hook to block. Session teardown cannot wait minutes, so any\n  design where \"correct\" means minutes must be asynchronous.\n- Both harnesses wire their own copy. `~/.codex/hooks/memsearch-sync.js` is now a\n  **symlink** to the Claude copy (2026-09-02) so the pair cannot drift again — same fix as\n  `~/.codex/AGENTS.md`. Before \"fixing a hook\", ask which copy the harness wires; see\n  [[memsearch-index-path-drift]] for the four-copy history.\n- `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/codex-hooks/memsearch-sync.ps1` still has a 45s\n  `WaitForExit(45000)`, but nothing wires it (Codex uses `hooks.json` → the `.js`). Dead\n  Windows-era code; left alone deliberately.\n\nRelated: [[milvus-lock-misdiagnosis]], [[memsearch-index-path-drift]],\n[[memory-compiler-pipeline]], [[backup-sync-system]], [[keystoneos-verification-habits]]\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/project_memsearch-index-path-drift.md\n\n---\nname: memsearch-index-path-drift\ndescription: \"the SessionEnd memsearch hook read its path config from a Windows-era ~/Desktop/DEV path and silently fell back, indexing 4 of 10 memory paths for months after the Mac migration\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  modified: 2026-08-15T04:07:30.329Z\n  originSessionId: 75381393-88b6-4b65-960d-6613e08f7060\n---\n\n`~/DEV/KeystoneOS/data/memory-index-paths.json` is the single source of truth for which directories memsearch indexes. Three writers read it: the SessionEnd hook `~/.claude/hooks/memsearch-sync.js`, `~/.claude/scripts/daily-backup.ps1` (nightly; 09:00 since 2026-08-29), and the Codex hook `KeystoneOS/scripts/codex-hooks/memsearch-sync.ps1`.\n\n**Found 2026-08-14:** the JS hook pointed at `~/Desktop/DEV/KeystoneOS/data/memory-index-paths.json` — the pre-migration Windows layout. DEV lives at `~/DEV` on this Mac, so the read threw, the `catch` returned a hardcoded fallback, and the hook indexed **4 paths instead of 10** for months. The fallback itself was also stale (`~/Desktop/SecondBrain/...`, which does not exist since the vault move). `daily-backup.ps1` used `$U/DEV` and was already correct; the Codex hook self-locates from its own script path and was fine. So only the JS hook drifted — and it is the one wired into `settings.json` (`SessionEnd`, 50s timeout).\n\nUnindexed as a result: `SecondBrain/Knowledge`, `SecondBrain/Inbox/meetings`, `SecondBrain/morning-briefings`, the Mac memory slug `-Users-kristinarash-DEV/memory`, `KeystoneOS/decisions`, `.codex/memories`. Incremental recall for all of those depended entirely on that nightly.\n\n**Why:** the failure mode was invisible. A silent `catch → fallback` means a migration-broken config path degrades into a quieter, smaller, still-succeeding job — no error, no log line, no failed run. Nothing surfaces until someone asks \"why didn't that turn up in search?\" The bug was not the wrong path; it was that the wrong path cost nothing to have.\n\n**How to apply:**\n- Any config-file fallback in this stack must log to `.errors.log` when it fires. The JS hook now does. A fallback that never announces itself is a silent outage with a long tail.\n- When auditing post-migration breakage, grep for `Desktop/DEV` and `Desktop/SecondBrain` — those two strings mark Windows-era assumptions. Do not trust that a script \"works\" as evidence its paths resolved; check what it actually resolved to.\n- Verify path config by resolving it in isolation (`node -e` reading the same JSON, printing EXISTS/MISSING per entry) rather than by running the indexer — that proves the fix without taking the Milvus Lite write lock.\n- Paths in the JSON that do not exist yet are harmless; every writer filters through an existence check before passing them to memsearch.\n\n**There were TWO copies of this hook, and fixing one did not fix the other (2026-08-15).** A Codex review found `~/.codex/hooks.json` wires its *own* copy at `~/.codex/hooks/memsearch-sync.js`, separate from the Claude copy at `~/.claude/hooks/memsearch-sync.js`. The Claude copy was repaired 2026-08-14; the Codex copy still pointed at `~/Desktop/DEV/...`, so **every Codex session-end indexed a stale 5-path fallback** that also referenced the old `~/Desktop/SecondBrain` — missing SecondBrain/Knowledge, Inbox/meetings, morning-briefings, KeystoneOS decisions and the Mac memory slug. Now fixed and verified resolving all 11 configured paths.\n\nOrdering trap in that repair: `const ERR_LOG` was declared *after* `const PATHS = resolvePaths()`. Adding a `logErr()` call inside `resolvePaths` therefore hit the const temporal dead zone, and `logErr`'s own `try/catch` swallowed the ReferenceError — reproducing the silent fallback the fix was meant to remove. `ERR_LOG` must be declared before `PATHS`.\n\n**How to apply:** when fixing a hook, first ask *which copy the harness actually wires* — check `~/.codex/hooks.json` and `~/.claude/settings.json`, not just the file you happened to open. Grep the whole home tree for the hook's basename before assuming there is one.\n\n**A THIRD hook had the same stale path, found 2026-09-01 during `/doctor-plus`.** `~/.claude/hooks/keystone-unified-snapshot.js` (SessionStart) read `~/Desktop/DEV/KeystoneOS/outputs/dashboard/unified-snapshot.md`, threw ENOENT, and its `catch (_) { emit(null) }` injected **nothing at all** — for every session since the migration. The snapshot file was being written correctly the whole time at `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/outputs/dashboard/`; only the reader was wrong. Zero symptoms: no error, no log, valid JSON on stdout, session starts normally.\n\nThe 2026-08-14/15 sweep fixed the two `memsearch-sync.js` copies but did not catch this one, so **\"grep for `Desktop/DEV`\" must cover every file in `~/.claude/hooks/` and `~/.codex/hooks/`, not only the hook currently being repaired.** Run it as a sweep across the whole hooks tree, not per-file.\n\nThat sweep then found a **fourth** copy — `~/.codex/hooks/keystone-unified-snapshot.js`, byte-identical and wired in `~/.codex/hooks.json`. Also fixed 2026-09-01.\n\n**A second signature the `Desktop/DEV` grep cannot catch:** `~/.codex/AGENTS.md` (dated 2026-08-08) had been through a bad `claude` to `Codex` find-replace, so it told Codex to read `~/.Codex/global-memory/`, `~/.Codex/projects/<slug>/memory/` and `~/.Codex/Codex-memory-compiler/daily/` — none of which exist. Grep for `.Codex` (capital C) as well as `Desktop/DEV` when auditing migration damage. Also check which AGENTS.md the harness actually reads: `~/.claude/AGENTS.md` was correct but referenced by nothing.\n\nResolved 2026-09-01: `~/.codex/AGENTS.md` is now a symlink to `~/.claude/AGENTS.md`, so the pair has one home and cannot drift again. `setup-agents-symlinks.sh` was also sourcing `CLAUDE.md` for that link — the one thing `CLAUDE.md` explicitly forbids — and now sources `AGENTS.md`. Running that script used to recreate the bug.\n\nRepaired 2026-09-01: path corrected to `~/DEV`, required sources now push a visible \"SessionStart hook could not read a required memory file\" block into `additionalContext` instead of failing silently, and the hook now also injects `global-memory/USER.md` + `MEMORY.md` (replacing a prose rule in `CLAUDE.md` that nothing enforced). The KeystoneOS snapshot is pointed at, not pasted.\n\nRelated: [[milvus-lock-misdiagnosis]] (found while fixing that), [[secondbrain-vault-location]] (the vault move that stranded the fallback), [[backup-sync-system]]\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/project_milvus-lock-misdiagnosis.md\n\n---\nname: milvus-lock-misdiagnosis\ndescription: milvus-lite reports lock contention as a version-incompatibility error; following its advice destroys a working memsearch index for hours\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: fa062f93-2c55-4016-b9c6-f1a32e0a7083\n  modified: 2026-08-15T02:42:26.304Z\n---\n\nWhen memsearch fails to open `~/.memsearch/milvus.db`, milvus-lite prints:\n\n> Failed to open the local Milvus Lite database. If this database was created with an older Milvus Lite release, it may not be compatible with Milvus Lite 3.x. Move the existing .db file aside, then rebuild the index...\n\n**That text is a generic open-failure string, not a version verdict.** It fires just as readily on plain lock contention. Diagnosed 2026-08-14: the underlying error was `BlockingIOError: [Errno 35]` from `flock`, and `lsof +D ~/.memsearch/milvus.db` named the holder — a concurrent manual `memsearch index` running after the SecondBrain vault move. The same DB with the same milvus-lite 3.1.1 had indexed successfully hours earlier.\n\n(Attribution caution: at the time this was written the holder was assumed to be \"another Claude session,\" inferred from an unfamiliar PID and shell-snapshot id. Eric corrected that — it was not. `lsof` tells you *which process*, never *which agent or person*; state the process and leave the actor unnamed.)\n\n**Why:** following the message's advice (move the .db aside, rebuild) throws away a working index and costs an hours-scale re-index of the SecondBrain library — a self-inflicted outage in response to a transient collision.\n\n**How to apply:**\n- Before believing any milvus-lite \"incompatible database\" message, run `lsof +D ~/.memsearch/milvus.db` and `ps aux | grep memsearch`. A live holder means wait, not rebuild.\n- Milvus Lite is single-writer. `daily-backup.ps1`'s advisory lock (`.memsearch-index.lock`, ~line 416) coordinates the nightly reindex with the SessionEnd hook only — it cannot see a hand-run `memsearch index`, so manual indexing during a backup window collides. Avoid running `memsearch index` by hand while the nightly backup may fire.\n- `memsearch-index:full-set` in `.errors.log` is therefore usually transient. Confirm the lock is free, then let the next scheduled run clear it.\n\n**Tooling mitigation added 2026-08-14 (because the written warning was not enough).** Later the same day a session ran `memsearch reset --yes` anyway and triggered a ~2h full rebuild — the exact outage this note warns about, with the note already on disk. Prose guidance does not survive contact with a scary error message, so the guard is now in code:\n\n- `~/.local/bin/ms-search` — lock-aware retry wrapper for reads (`search` / `stats` / `expand` / `transcript`; write subcommands are refused). Retries with backoff to 60s (`MS_SEARCH_MAX_WAIT`), then exits **75** saying the DB is healthy and not to run `reset`. It retries **only** on `DataDirLockedError` / `another process holds the lock` / `Resource temporarily unavailable` / `_acquire_lock` / `fcntl.flock`; it deliberately does **not** key on the \"may not be compatible with Milvus Lite 3.x\" sentence, since that same wording is emitted for genuine incompatibility — so real corruption still fails fast and passes its exit code through.\n- `~/.claude/hooks/memsearch-sync.js` now classifies those same lock signatures as transient instead of escalating them as hard errors. Caveat: `bumpTransientStreak` shares one counter across all transient classes at threshold 6, so a cluster of session-ends during a long rebuild can still escalate — downgraded, not eliminated.\n\n**How to apply (updated):** prefer `ms-search \"query\"` over `memsearch search` for recall; it waits out a busy index instead of surfacing the misleading message. Reserve bare `memsearch` for writes.\n\nRelated: [[backup-sync-system]] (carries the earlier two-writer lesson), [[memsearch-index-path-drift]] (the config drift found while fixing this), [[drive-fuse-rsync-mtime-loop]]\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/project_morning-briefing-retired.md\n\n---\nname: morning-briefing-retired\ndescription: \"morning-briefing retired 2026-08-14 - removed from disk, code on GitHub, 56 briefings kept in vault and indexed\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: fa062f93-2c55-4016-b9c6-f1a32e0a7083\n  modified: 2026-08-15T02:38:48.999Z\n---\n\nThe morning-briefing project was **retired 2026-08-14** at Eric's request — he wasn't using it and it was overhead the system had to manage. He may approach the idea differently in future.\n\nWhat was done:\n- `com.keystone.morningbriefing` (7:20 AM) booted out and `disabled`; **plist retained** at `~/Library/LaunchAgents/`. Do not confuse it with `com.keystone.morning` (6:00 AM KeystoneOS `morning-cadence`), which is unrelated and still live.\n- `~/DEV/morning-briefing/` deleted (338 MB — mostly a 305 MB `.venv` and a 32 MB authenticated Outlook browser profile). Code is intact at `github.com/erash11/morning-briefing` (0 unpushed commits at removal).\n- Local `secrets/` destroyed (Outlook `owa-profile`, `gmail_credentials.json`, `gmail_token.json`) plus `~/.config/morning-briefing/`. Outlook auth had already lapsed; briefs were emailing 0 threads.\n- **`KeystoneOS/data/jobs.json` entry removed — this is the one that actually mattered.** Disabling launchd was not enough: the entry had `reconcile: true` / `maxAttemptsPerDay: 2`, and `keystone-reconcile.ps1` (which dot-sources `run-ledger.ps1`, reading `data/jobs.json` live) re-launched the briefing at 19:39 on 2026-08-14 *despite* launchd being disabled — logged as `MAKE-UP morning-briefing`. Its `authWarnProbe` is also what wrote the \"Outlook auth expired / run reauth.sh\" line into `.errors.log`. When retiring any KeystoneOS job, remove the `jobs.json` entry or the reconciler resurrects it.\n- Lost in the removal (accepted): uncommitted `config.toml` edits from 19:37 that day, and `logs/` (508 KB, 113 run logs). Briefing *outputs* were in the vault; run logs were not.\n- Dropped from `backup-manifest.json`, stack-doctor checks, and `CREDENTIALS.md` (which had a stale **OUTSTANDING** `GMAIL_APP_PASSWORD` item, now moot).\n- KeystoneOS: brief moved to `projects/retired/morning_briefing.md`, `registry.md` + `data/projects.json` marked Retired.\n\n**Why it matters:** the 56 briefings at `~/SecondBrain/morning-briefings/` were **kept**, added to the memsearch index set, and **verified retrievable** 2026-08-15 (916 chunks indexed; queries like \"Matt Tardif Firstbeat intro\" and \"FORMS TASKS OWED timecard Playfly\" return `morning-briefings/*.md`). Note recall needs *specific* terms — generic queries like \"meetings to schedule\" rank other sources above them. 42 of them carry synthesized digests (who asked for what, meetings to schedule, tasks owed) spanning 2026-05-22 → 08-14. That's the recall value; the project code was not the point. Expect some noise in results: a `STILL OPEN FROM PRIOR DAYS` list bloated to ~254 stale items and leaked browser-automation strings.\n\n**How to apply:**\n- To revive: re-clone from GitHub, re-add to `stack-check.ps1`, then `launchctl enable gui/$(id -u)/com.keystone.morningbriefing` && `launchctl bootstrap`. Credentials must be re-created — do not restore tokens from backup.\n- Same edit added `{HOME}/.claude/projects/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/memory` to the index set. That path — where all Mac-era memory notes live — had **never** been indexed; only the two pre-migration `C--Users-eric-rash*` slugs were. Verified retrievable 2026-08-15: targeted queries now return these notes as top hits. Config lives at `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/data/memory-index-paths.json`, not under `~/.claude/scripts/`. See [[memsearch-index-path-drift]] — this omission was one symptom of a broader config-resolution fault.\n\nRelated: [[backup-sync-system]], [[milvus-lock-misdiagnosis]], [[automation-cadence]]\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/project_path-shadowing-and-cleanup.md\n\n---\nname: path-shadowing-and-cleanup\ndescription: \"PATH shadowing repair 2026-08-13 — measure PATH with env -i, grep hardcoded paths before deleting a binary, broken symlinks evade find -type f\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  modified: 2026-08-16T18:39:28.064Z\n  originSessionId: 7c7ca38f-e834-48b6-bae9-d7d80bbbbb95\n---\n\nRepairs made 2026-08-13 on this Mac, and the four traps hit doing it. All four generalise.\n\n**What was wrong and is now fixed:**\n- `uv` existed twice — `~/.local/bin/uv` 0.11.6 (Apr) shadowed Homebrew's 0.12.1 (Jul), so a\n  three-month-old build was winning. Fixed with `uv self update` → 0.12.3, then `brew uninstall uv`.\n- PATH had duplicate `~/go/bin` and `~/.local/bin` entries (17 entries → 15). Sources: a redundant\n  export in `.zshrc`, a duplicated `go/bin` line, and two unguarded `brew shellenv` calls in `.zprofile`.\n- An entire pre-migration Node install at `/usr/local` (~690 MB: node, npm, npx, corepack, stale\n  codex/gemini/gws) removed. `/usr/local/bin` now holds only Docker Desktop + kubectl.\n- `FIREFLIES_API_KEY` moved from plaintext in `.zshrc` into the login Keychain.\n\n**The four traps — these are the reusable part:**\n\n1. **A PATH read from inside a tool shell is a lie.** Any earlier `export PATH=…` in the session\n   pollutes it. This produced a wrong claim (`~/.bun/bin` reported as duplicated when it was not).\n   Honest measurement:\n   `env -i HOME=\"$HOME\" USER=\"$USER\" TERM=xterm SHELL=/bin/zsh /bin/zsh -lic 'echo $PATH'`\n2. **PATH order only governs lookups by name.** Deleting `~/.local/bin/uv` would have silently\n   killed three hooks in `~/.claude/settings.json` (SessionStart/PreCompact/SessionEnd) that call\n   the memory-compiler by *absolute path*. Always `grep -rl '/full/path' ~/.claude ~/DEV` first.\n3. **Scheduled jobs are the blind spot.** Eight KeystoneOS launchd agents referenced `/usr/local`\n   — harmlessly, as PATH entries — but nothing in shell config would have shown them. Check\n   `~/Library/LaunchAgents` before touching a shared prefix.\n4. **`[ -e ]` and `find -type f` both skip broken symlinks.** 92 npm man pages were symlinks into\n   a just-deleted tree; the first cleanup pass removed 1 of 92 and reported success. Use\n   `find … \\( -type l -o -type f \\) -delete`.\n\n**Still open (his action):** rotate the Fireflies key — the old value persists in two `.zshrc`\nbackups, session transcripts, and `~/.claude/file-history/`; the Keychain does not retract those.\nAlso `sudo rm -rf /usr/local/include/node` (62 MB) and `sudo rmdir /usr/local/lib/node_modules`.\n\n**Why:** each of these cost real debugging time, and three of them fail *silently* — the wrong\nversion runs, the hook stops, the file never gets deleted, with no error.\n**How to apply:** before asserting where a tool lives or deleting any binary on this machine, run\nthe `env -i` measurement and the hardcoded-path grep. See also [[zsh-shell-traps]] and\n[[terminal-fluency-workspace]].\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/project_personal-site-build-routes.md\n\n---\nname: personal-site-build-routes\ndescription: \"Eric's personal site is an Artifact with hard constraints — what that forbids, why scroll-craft cannot run on it, and where the standalone build lives instead\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: a6dfb3da-c339-44b1-a836-2a6884e07ea6\n  modified: 2026-09-02T23:05:38.332Z\n---\n\nTwo separate things, both under `~/DEV/Website/` (which **is** a git repo,\nremote `erash11/AppliedPerformance-Site`, though `personal/` is deliberately\nuntracked):\n\n**1. The live site — `~/DEV/Website/personal/index.html`.** Published as a Claude\nArtifact; republish with the stored `url` to keep the link. Current brief is\n`BRIEF-V3.md` (\"Night Field\", dark-first, Texas, kitsch ban) — V1 and V2 are\nsuperseded, so quote V3. Its hard rules: one Google Fonts link and **no other\nexternal resource**, no libraries, no fetch, Canvas 2D and inline SVG only,\nnothing readable parked at `opacity: 0`, `prefers-reduced-motion` fully usable.\n\n**These constraints structurally forbid scroll-craft**, which needs an engine\nJS/CSS pair on disk, external video, and scroll-assembled text. It is not a\ntuning problem; the two cannot both be satisfied. Anything premium wanted on the\nArtifact has to be hand-built inline: layering via transforms, stacking cards,\ncounting numbers.\n\n**2. The standalone build — `~/DEV/Website/scrollcraft/builds/eric-rash/`.**\nEric's chosen answer (2026-09-02). A real scroll-craft build, split-stage\ngrammar, no video, all visuals code-drawn. Serve it:\n\n```bash\ncd ~/DEV/Website/scrollcraft/builds/eric-rash\nnode ~/.agents/skills/scroll-craft/scripts/serve.mjs --root . --port 4500\n```\n\nNot hosted, and it does not touch the Artifact. `lab/` holds a few hundred\nverification PNGs and is gitignored; it reached 339MB in one session, so never\nstage it.\n\n**Before critiquing his site, read it.** It already leads with the problem\n(\"Most performance problems are not data problems. They are question problems.\")\nand already has a scroll-driven jump-trace scrub. Assuming otherwise produced a\nwrong claim that had to be retracted.\n\nRedesign notes live in `~/DEV/Website/personal/DESIGN-IDEAS.md`.\nRelated: [[vendored-skill-install]], [[dev-layout-and-discovery]].\n\n- **Cowork install route (2026-09-02):** Claude Code and Codex load scroll-craft directly. Cowork cannot; it needs the skill uploaded as a zip from `~/Downloads`.\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/project_portfolio-registry-write-provenance.md\n\n---\nname: portfolio-registry-write-provenance\ndescription: \"project-triage's portfolio.json is written from outside Claude sessions; how to prove who wrote it, and why registry changes get their own commit\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: 4c8d85d1-3921-4284-adc0-0c1165479df3\n  modified: 2026-08-16T18:37:45.500Z\n---\n\n`~/DEV/project-triage/portfolio.json` is edited outside Claude sessions — Eric's\nown board, the `/project` skill, and the KeystoneOS cadence all write it. It\nchanged twice mid-session on 2026-08-16 (a re-rank, then a promotion). Expect a\ndirty registry that you did not cause, and check before assuming a bug.\n\n**Proving who wrote it:** `portfolio.save_registry` always refreshes\n`portfolio.json.bak` before replacing the file. An untouched `.bak` therefore\nrules out `board.py` and the CLI. A `.bak` whose mtime tracks the registry means\nsomething in this repo wrote it.\n\n**Never run `board.py` against the live registry to test.** Copy\n`portfolio.json` + `board.html` into a scratch dir and point\n`board.make_server(Path(scratch), ...)` there. The sandbox growing its own\n`.bak` is the proof your writes stayed out of the real file.\n\n**Commit registry changes separately**, with a `data:` subject line, never folded\ninto a code commit — the repo already does this and it keeps registry history\nreadable against code history. Say what moved in the message; only ranks and\nmembership ever change.\n\nRelated: [[keystoneos-verification-habits]], [[dev-layout-and-discovery]]\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/project_printing-press-spec-traps.md\n\n---\nname: printing-press-spec-traps\ndescription: \"Spec-authoring and pipeline traps discovered printing catapult + zotero CLIs (Aug 2026) — pagination params, envelope-nested fields, probe fixtures\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: a86cfb00-e6d1-46b9-8cdc-35e21acd9931\n  modified: 2026-08-06T23:21:52.213Z\n---\n\nTraps hit while printing `catapult-openfield-connect` and `zotero-research-library` (2026-08-05/06), each cost a debugging cycle:\n\n- **Offset pagination MUST name `cursor_param`** in the spec (e.g. `cursor_param: start` for Zotero). Omitted, the generator defaults to `after`; servers that ignore unknown params return page 1 forever while the cursor climbs (silent infinite loop, same 25 rows).\n- **Envelope-nested APIs leave all extracted store columns empty.** Zotero items nest fields under `$.data.*`; the store's `LookupFieldValue` reads top-level keys only, so title/date_added/name columns are NULL. Hand commands must read `json_extract(\"data\", '$.data.<field>')` and search via `resources_fts` (joins on its `id` column — NEVER on rowid, which is hashed).\n- **Novel command names collide with framework commands** (`search`, `periods`) — validate-narrative catches it late; check the framework command list at absorb time. Renamed to `ground`.\n- **Dogfood probes run with PP_DOGFOOD=1** which curtails syncs to one page — happy-args fixtures must resolve against a ~25-row cache. Use dynamic specials (`top`, `last`) instead of baked keys.\n- **`pp:happy-args` grammar**: positional labels need angle brackets (`<query>=hamstring`); `--flag=value` tokens pass flags; probes time out at 30s so unbounded commands need a bounding flag in happy-args.\n- **generate --force preserves hand files but re-emits generated docs** — keep a `fix-generated-docs.py` per run to strip phantom \"Covered paths\" bullets after every regen.\n- **Novel child commands must use direct `cmd.AddCommand(newXxxCmd(...))` calls.** The public library's SKILL.md verifier discovers subcommands by regex-scanning for that exact pattern; any helper indirection (e.g. `addNovelCommandIfAbsent`) hides children and their flags, failing CI on doc examples. Guard with a `hasSubcommandNamed` check instead of wrapping AddCommand.\n- **Zotero /deleted returns a category map** (collections/items/searches/tags/settings arrays in one object), not a list — generic extractors keep only `items` and silently drop the rest; flatten all categories into keyed tombstones.\n- **Catapult API v6**: date filter on POST /stats hangs server-side — express windows as activity_id sets; `requested_only=false` needed for athlete/activity/date columns; dates arrive MM/DD/YYYY.\n- Related: [[zsh-shell-traps]]\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/project_rtp-cla-course.md\n\n---\nname: rtp-cla-course\ndescription: \"RTP Constraints-Led course — book ingest complete with zero-offset PDF; lesson 0002 is next\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: b89c2f0b-04a6-4248-9c74-5f9b535a314c\n  modified: 2026-09-10T17:00:00.000Z\n---\n\n`DEV/RTP_Constraints_Course` — a return-to-play course built on constraints-led\npractice design, sourced from Myszka et al., *Enhancing Skill*.\n\n**Ingest state (as of 2026-09-05): DONE.** 84 atomized advisor entries live in a\nnew SecondBrain vault domain:\n`Knowledge/library/skill-acquisition/enhancing-skill--myszka/`.\n\n**Page facts — do not re-derive these.**\n- Phone scans on disk cover printed pages p001–p253.\n- The PDF was rebuilt on 2026-09-10 and holds printed p001–p253.\n- PDF page N equals printed page N. The offset is **0**.\n- Ordering was verified five separate ways, including against the book's own\n  printed contents page, then verified again after the rebuild.\n  See [[book-scan-page-ordering]].\n\n**Deliberate limit.** Printed pages 254 onward are a blank verso and About the\nAuthors. Eric chose not to scan them because they add no course material.\n\n**Also open:**\n- Import `sources/myszka-references-new.ris` (122 records) into Zotero via\n  File → Import.\n- Write lesson 0002.\n\nBefore any further expensive atomization here, apply the arithmetic gate in\n[[ingest-review-gate]].\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/project_s2-cognition-analysis.md\n\n---\nname: s2-cognition-analysis\ndescription: \"S2 Cognition data traps (vendor reference group, P/K short battery, invalid reports) and the standing rule that any flagging threshold must be null-calibrated and pre-registered before the data is touched\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: 856bd54e-8db3-42f5-a770-103ee081614e\n  modified: 2026-08-16T18:37:34.242Z\n---\n\nS2 Cognition analysis lives at `~/DEV/S2_Evaluation` (private repo `erash11/S2_Evaluation`). V1 shipped 2026-08-16 with a **null finding**: no offensive lineman is cognitively atypical once the flagging threshold is calibrated against a null model.\n\n**Vendor data traps** (learned the hard way, not documented by S2):\n\n- Percentiles are scored against **\"NFL PROSPECTS - ALL POSITIONS\"**, not a college or position-specific reference group. So a percentile is only comparable *within* that reference group — cross-position pooling is legitimate (Baylor rooms range 42nd–82nd), but \"50th percentile\" does not mean \"average for his position.\"\n- The **P/K battery has only 7 measures**, not the usual 9, with zero N/A cells. That is structural to the vendor's short battery, not a parse bug — don't chase it.\n- Some reports fail S2's own **validity check**; the vendor marks them INVALID. Exclude them from analysis and surface as a retest recommendation instead of silently dropping.\n- Test years are mixed across a roster. Flag the year as a diagnostic; don't filter on it.\n\n**Standing methodological rule** (ADR 0013, applies beyond S2): never set a \"flag the outlier\" threshold without first simulating what a **pure-noise room** produces at that threshold. The original 1.5 SD rule flagged 10 of 15 linemen; a homogeneous null room flags 7–13 of 15, so the rule was measuring nothing. Related: with 9 categories, max-over-N inflation makes almost any naive threshold vacuous.\n\nCorollary — because Eric is his own reviewer on side-lane projects, thresholds get **pre-registered in a timestamped git commit before the data is on the machine**. Changing a threshold after seeing results requires a new ADR stating what was wrong; never retune silently.\n\nRelated: [[cfb-analytics-lab]], [[status-report-sourcing]]\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/project_secondbrain-vault-location.md\n\n---\nname: secondbrain-vault-location\ndescription: SecondBrain vault lives at ~/SecondBrain (moved off the iCloud-synced Desktop 2026-08-14); config.json is the source of truth but ~29 files hardcode the path\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: a344e278-9d2a-4998-836a-fc0ece8896e2\n  modified: 2026-08-15T00:50:26.229Z\n---\n\nThe SecondBrain vault is at `/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain`. It was relocated from\n`~/Desktop/SecondBrain` on 2026-08-14.\n\n**Why it moved:** macOS Desktop & Documents syncing was on, so the vault sat inside the iCloud\ncontainer (`~/Desktop` is a firmlink to `~/Library/Mobile Documents/com~apple~CloudDocs/Desktop` —\nsame inode, not a copy). That consumed iCloud storage, and \"Optimize Mac Storage\" can evict files\ninto `.icloud` placeholder stubs, which would break every scripted read (sync ledgers, memsearch\nindexing, graphify) on files that still *appear* to exist. `~/SecondBrain` is outside iCloud sync.\n\n**How to apply:**\n- Never write vault files to `~/Desktop/SecondBrain` — that path no longer exists.\n- `~/.claude/skills/_shared/config.json` is the documented source of truth, but it is NOT the only\n  place the path lives. A relocation touches ~29 live files: 13 skill docs, `backup-manifest.json`\n  (entry is `$HOME`-relative: `SecondBrain`), `daily-backup.ps1`, `restore.command`,\n  `setup-agents-symlinks.sh`, KeystoneOS `data/*.json` (discovery-roots, memory-index-paths,\n  memory-sources, projects), KeystoneOS `scripts/*.ps1`, `tests/keystone-memory-index-paths.ps1`\n  (asserts exact path strings — update in lockstep or the test goes red), and\n  `morning-briefing/config.toml`.\n- Do NOT rewrite generated outputs (`KeystoneOS/outputs/**`), historical design docs, session\n  `.jsonl` logs, or `file-history/` — those are records and regenerate on their own.\n- Before any vault move, `launchctl bootout` the 7 loaded `com.keystone.*` agents first. `agysync`\n  fires every 6h and would recreate a partial vault at the old path — the same two-writer failure\n  recorded in [[backup-sync-system]].\n- Vault contains zero self-referential absolute paths, so it is portable; `ditto` + a clean\n  `git status` at the destination is sufficient proof of a good copy.\n\nRelated: [[backup-sync-system]], [[automation-cadence]]\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/project_settings-json-bom-trap.md\n\n---\nname: settings-json-bom-trap\ndescription: \"A bad Windows write to ~/.claude config files does TWO things at once — adds a UTF-8 BOM (dcg then reports the hook as missing) and CP1252-double-encodes every non-ASCII character (statusline bars turn to junk). Check settings.json, CLAUDE.md and AGENTS.md, and check for both faults.\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: 7741462f-dac7-4551-8692-76574bbfd202\n  modified: 2026-09-12T03:37:42.609Z\n---\n\nOne bad write from the Windows machine damages `~/.claude` config files in\n**two independent ways**. They arrive together and are fixed together, but they\nsurface at completely different times, so finding one does not mean you found\nboth. On 2026-09-10 only the BOM was caught; the mojibake sat undetected for two\nmore days until the statusline was looked at directly on 2026-09-11.\n\n## Fault 1 — the UTF-8 BOM (loud, misleading)\n\n`~/.claude/settings.json` acquires a **UTF-8 BOM** — the 3 invisible bytes\n`EF BB BF` at the start. Claude Code parses the file anyway, so nothing in\nClaude Code looks broken. `dcg` (Destructive Command Guard) does not, so it\nprints at every session start:\n\n```\n[dcg] Hook missing from ~/.claude/settings.json — run: dcg install\n```\n\n**The message is wrong, and its suggested fix is the wrong fix.** The hook was\npresent and correct all along. Running `dcg install` would have added a second\nhook to a file the tool cannot read.\n\n**Diagnose, don't trust the banner.** `dcg doctor` names the real fault:\n\n```\nChecking hook wiring... ERROR\n  Invalid JSON: expected value at line 1 column 1\n```\n\n\"Line 1 column 1\" on a file that is visibly valid JSON means a BOM.\n\n## Fault 2 — CP1252 double-encoding (silent, cosmetic-looking)\n\nThe same write reads the file's UTF-8 bytes as **CP1252**, then saves them back\nas UTF-8. Every non-ASCII character becomes 2–3 junk characters. Nothing errors.\nNothing warns. JSON stays valid. You only notice by *looking at rendered output*.\n\nThe statusline is where it shows, because it is the only config that draws\nbox characters:\n\n| Intended | Becomes | Where |\n|---|---|---|\n| `░` U+2591 | `â–‘` | statusline meter bar (empty) |\n| `▓` U+2593 | `â–“` | statusline meter bar (filled) |\n| `↑ ↓ →` | `â†‘ â†“ â†’` | git ahead/behind, cache arrow |\n| `—` em-dash | `â€”` | CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md prose |\n| `…` `’` `·` | `â€¦` `â€™` `Â·` | CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md prose |\n\nThe `â` prefix is the tell — it is `0xE2`, the first byte of most UTF-8 symbols,\nread as Latin-1.\n\n## It is not only settings.json\n\nThe 2026-09-11 sweep found the damage in **three** files. Two of them also\ncarried BOMs that the 9/10 fix never looked at:\n\n- `~/.claude/settings.json` — 113 mangled chars (no BOM by then, already fixed)\n- `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` — 8 mangled chars **+ BOM**\n- `~/.claude/AGENTS.md` — 25 mangled chars **+ BOM**\n\nAlways sweep the whole tree, never just the file that complained.\n\n## Fix — strip BOM, then reverse the encoding, then verify rendering\n\n```python\nimport json\np = \"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/settings.json\"\ns = open(p, encoding=\"utf-8\").read().lstrip(\"﻿\")\nfixed = s.encode(\"cp1252\").decode(\"utf-8\")   # exact inverse\njson.loads(fixed)                             # must still parse\nopen(p, \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\", newline=\"\\n\").write(fixed)\n```\n\n`.encode(\"cp1252\")` raising means the file is only *partly* mangled, or a BOM is\nstill present — strip the BOM first, and never force the round-trip on a file\nwhere it fails. Sweep for survivors by scanning for these patterns:\n\n```python\npats = ['â–‘','â–“','â†‘','â†“','â†’','â€”','â€™','â€¦','Â·']\n```\n\n**Verify the statusline by rendering it**, not by reading the JSON — pipe a\nsample payload into the `statusLine.command` and look at the bars.\n\nWriting the repaired file needs the Write tool, not a shell heredoc: `dcg`\nblocks any truncating `>` redirect into `~` (`core.filesystem:redirect-truncate-root-home`).\n\n## The generalisation\n\nTwo tolerant readers hid this for two days. Claude Code tolerates the BOM, so\nthe statusline kept running; the terminal tolerates mojibake, so it kept drawing\n*something*. **The strict reader complains about the wrong thing (`dcg`), and the\ncosmetic damage never complains at all.** When a config file has been touched by\nthe other machine, check the first bytes *and* grep for `â` — one fault does not\nimply the other has been handled.\n\nRelated: [[dcg-guard-blocks]], [[hook-two-copies-trap]], [[github-consolidation]],\n[[path-shadowing-and-cleanup]]\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/project_skill-bloat-and-promotion.md\n\n---\nname: skill-bloat-and-promotion\ndescription: \"Measured skill bloat in this setup, the project-until-promoted rule that fixes it, and the command that re-measures usage from .jsonl session logs\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: f1097c6d-9db5-4bb5-a0f7-c42d645cf75d\n  modified: 2026-09-13T06:38:22.028Z\n---\n\nMeasured 2026-09-12 from a gap analysis of a Nate Herk / **Mark Kashef** video\n(\"How to Actually Choose the Right AI Agent\", 2026-09-11). Full note:\n`~/SecondBrain/Inbox/2026-09-12-harness-over-model-mark-kashef.md`.\nSame shape as the earlier audit in [[brain-audit-loop]] — that one scored the\n**vault**, this one scores the **skill layer**.\n\n## The measurement\n\n| Metric | Count |\n|---|---|\n| Global skills in `~/.claude/skills/` | 160 (→ **117** after the Tier 1 archive, 2026-09-12) |\n| Plugin skills exposed per session | ~37 (6 enabled, 18 disabled) |\n| SKILL.md files in plugin cache | 606 — disabled plugins + old versions, NOT the load |\n| Project-level skills anywhere in `~/DEV` | **0** |\n| `.jsonl` sessions scanned | 414 |\n| Distinct real skills **ever** invoked | **58** |\n| Used 3+ times | 30 |\n| Used exactly once | 19 |\n\nEverything is global. That is the anti-pattern, not the achievement.\n\n## The rule that fixes it — promotion\n\n**Everything stays project-level until it earns global.** A skill lives in\n`<project>/.claude/skills/` until it proves itself across 2+ projects, then it\ngets promoted to `~/.claude/skills/`. Keeps the global load small and the blast\nradius tight — so when something underperforms you can tell a *model* problem\nfrom a *harness* problem from an *organisation* problem.\n\n## The audit question\n\nNot \"how often did I run this skill?\" but **\"would the model do this fine without\nthe skill now?\"** A skill is a crutch; as the brain gets smarter the crutch starts\nto *limit* it. Seasonal skills (`forecast-capture`) and occasional ones\n(`ingest-book`) are low-count by design — low usage alone is not a delete signal.\n\n## Re-measure (BUILTIN filter is the whole trick)\n\nWithout the `BUILTIN` set you also count `/exit`, `/clear`, `/compact` and friends,\nwhich inflates the total to 74 and buries the real skills.\n\n```bash\npython3 - <<'EOF'\nimport glob,re,collections\nBUILTIN={'exit','clear','compact','rename','color','login','logout','plugin','doctor',\n'help','model','config','resume','cost','status','init','agents','mcp','hooks','memory',\n'review','vim','terminal-setup','install-github-app','add-dir','bug','release-notes',\n'pr-comments','ide','permissions','statusline','output-style','todos','export',\n'privacy-settings','upgrade','fast','artifacts','tasks','loop','context','usage','rewind',\n'sandbox','feedback','worktrees','skills','reload-skills','reload-plugins','bashes',\n'keybindings','theme'}\nc=collections.Counter()\nfor f in glob.glob('/Users/kristinarash/.claude/projects/**/*.jsonl',recursive=True):\n    try:\n        for line in open(f,encoding='utf-8',errors='ignore'):\n            if 'Skill' not in line and 'command-name' not in line: continue\n            for m in re.finditer(r'\"name\"\\s*:\\s*\"Skill\"\\s*,\\s*\"input\"\\s*:\\s*\\{[^}]*?\"skill\"\\s*:\\s*\"([^\"]+)\"', line):\n                c[m.group(1)]+=1\n            for m in re.finditer(r'<command-name>/([a-zA-Z0-9:_-]+)</command-name>', line):\n                c[m.group(1)]+=1\n    except Exception: pass\nreal={k:v for k,v in c.items() if k.split(':')[-1] not in BUILTIN and k not in BUILTIN}\nprint(\"distinct real skills ever invoked:\",len(real))\nprint(\"used >=3 times:\",len([k for k,v in real.items() if v>=3]))\nprint(\"used exactly once:\",len([k for k,v in real.items() if v==1]))\nfor k,v in sorted(real.items(),key=lambda x:-x[1]): print(f\"{v:5d}  {k}\")\nEOF\n```\n\n## Decision, 2026-09-12\n\n**Do the skill audit BEFORE adopting Pi** (`pi.dev`) as an alternative harness.\nRationale: the leverage is the bloat, not the harness. Auditing first means any\nfuture harness move ports ~30 good skills instead of ~200 mixed ones.\n\nPi facts, verified from `pi.dev/docs/latest` 2026-09-12 — full note at\n`~/SecondBrain/Inbox/2026-09-12-pi-harness-research.md`:\n\n1. **No sandbox, no approval flows.** \"Pi does not include a built-in sandbox.\"\n   Project trust only guards resource *loading*, not what the agent then does.\n   Never run Pi in `~/DEV` or near `DexaProject`. Trial it in `~/pi-trial` or a\n   container (`sbx` keeps the credential off the container).\n2. **Skills port for one line.** Pi implements the Agent Skills standard and already\n   auto-discovers `~/.agents/skills/` — where 45 of the skills live. Add the rest with\n   `{\"skills\": [\"~/.claude/skills\"]}` in `~/.pi/agent/settings.json`. It loads\n   `AGENTS.md` **or** `CLAUDE.md` directly.\n3. **Hooks → TypeScript extensions is a bounded port, not a rewrite** (earlier note\n   said \"weeks\", that was wrong). Hooks here are already `.ts`; Pi's `tool_call` event\n   returns `{block, reason}` — the same shape as the dcg guard.\n4. **Claude subscription through Pi bills per token as \"extra usage\", NOT against plan\n   limits.** Changes the economics of brain-swapping.\n\nConfig lives in `~/.pi/agent/` and survives npm uninstall.\nSee also [[hook-two-copies-trap]] and [[dcg-guard-blocks]].\n\n## Audit outcome, 2026-09-12\n\n**Both tiers executed.** 99 skills archived, global **160 → 61**, roster cost\n**19,158 → 6,428 tokens/session (a 66% cut)**. Zero regressions: none of the 99\nhad ever been invoked in 414 sessions.\n\nDecision 2026-09-12: keep the `mattpocock-skills` and `superpowers` **plugins** and\narchive the local bare-name duplicates. The plugin (v1.2.3, 35 skills) ships every\nduplicate removed and is newer than the July symlinks. Rule that follows: when a\nplugin and a local skill share a name, **the plugin wins** — delete the local copy.\n\nScript: `~/.claude/skills-audit-2026-09-12.sh` (archives, never deletes — undo with\n`mv ~/.claude/skills-archive/2026-09-12/* ~/.claude/skills/`).\nReport: `~/SecondBrain/Inbox/2026-09-12-skill-audit.md`.\n\nTraps found while doing it:\n1. **CORRECTED 2026-09-12 — `~/.claude/skills/` IS version-controlled.** It is its own\n   git repo, remote `https://github.com/erash11/claude-skills.git`, and the audit was\n   committed as `3ab4467 chore: apply global skill audit` and pushed. The earlier note\n   that it is \"gitignored, archive is the only undo\" was true only of the *`dotclaude`*\n   repo, which ignores this path — a different thing. **Git IS the safety net here, and\n   it is also how the cut reaches Windows.** Undo with\n   `git -C ~/.claude/skills revert <sha> && git push`, not the archive folder\n   (`skills-archive/` is not in the repo and never reaches the other machine).\n2. **A cut in `~/.claude/skills/` does NOT reach `~/.agents/skills/`.** After the audit,\n   98 of the 99 archived skills were still live in the pool (306 entries there vs 61\n   canonical). Claude Code stopped seeing them; **Pi auto-discovers that folder**, so\n   installing Pi would have re-exposed all of them. Always cut both.\n3. **Skills live in three places and it is easy to edit the wrong copy.**\n   `~/.claude/skills/` (19 entries were symlinks pointing *out* to the pool),\n   `~/.agents/skills/` (Pi, Cowork), `~/.codex/skills/` (Codex, 27 symlinks + 5 real\n   dirs). Count with `find -L`; plain `find` undercounts.\n4. **`sync-to-codex.sh` did `rm -rf \"$dst/$s\"; cp -R \"$SRC/$s\"`** over a 7-name\n   allowlist. If `$SRC/$s` ever became a symlink into `$dst`, that deletes the real\n   data. `dev-sync` tells you to run it after every pull. A `.ps1` twin has the same\n   shape — patch both.\n5. **`~/.claude/skills/_shared/`** holds `config.json` + `Inference/` and is resolved by\n   10+ skills via `parents[2]/_shared/config.json`. It has **no SKILL.md**. Never treat\n   \"no SKILL.md\" as \"orphan, delete\".\n6. **\"606 plugin skills\" is cache, not load.** Only 6 of 24 plugins are enabled\n   (~37 skills exposed). The rest are disabled plugins and old cached versions.\n\n## Executed 2026-09-12 — traps the live run found\n\n- **Views are live links now.** `~/.agents/skills/<owned>` and `~/.codex/skills/<owned>`\n  point into canonical, so anything that writes a view path writes canonical\n  (`graphify install --platform agents` does; see [[graphify-runtime-setup]]).\n- **`skillOverrides` in `~/.claude/settings.json` breaks parity silently.** Codex and Pi\n  ignore that file, so an owned skill set `\"off\"` there is hidden in Claude Code but live\n  elsewhere. Perfect frontmatter does not help; `skill-portability-check.py` fails it.\n  Resolved 2026-09-13: Eric re-enabled the 7 (ArXiv, ISA, Science, capture-insight,\n  library-review, pp-espn, session-miner) — `~/.claude` commit `21c686d`, checker 42/42.\n  Never set an owned skill \"off\" again; retire it from canonical instead.\n- **Git's spelling wins, not the Mac's.** APFS is case-insensitive: the folder showed\n  `Harvest` while git recorded `harvest/`. Check `git ls-files` before \"fixing\" a\n  name to match the disk.\n- **A `closeout` in another session commits your uncommitted work** (it swept Task 5's\n  34 SKILL.md edits into its own commit). Commit each verified step immediately.\n- **Description budget:** keep each description under ~650 chars; Claude Code caps the\n  combined listing.\n\n## Architecture decided 2026-09-12 (executed through Task 5 the same day)\n\n**Canonical = `~/.claude/skills/`** (the git repo, real directories only, **never commit\na symlink** — Windows pulls this repo). `~/.agents/skills/` and `~/.codex/skills/` are\n**generated views**, rebuilt by `sync-to-codex.sh` from a complete manifest of every\ncanonical dir holding a SKILL.md, recorded in a `.generated-by-sync-to-codex` ownership\nfile so an audit can tell a generated entry from a third-party `npx skills add` install.\nSymlink on Mac, copy on Windows.\n\nPortable frontmatter contract (fires in Claude Code *and* Codex): `name` must equal the\ndirectory name (TitleCase is legal), and `description` needs **both** a positive\n\"Use when …\" clause with literal trigger phrases *and* a negative \"Do not use …\" clause.\nCodex reads only `name` + `description` (+ optional `metadata.short-description`) and\nignores `argument-hint` / `allowed-tools`. Codex over-fires without the negative clause;\nClaude will not trigger without the literal phrases.\n\nPlan (Codex-approved after 6 review rounds, 10→0 findings):\n`~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-12-harness-agnostic-aios.md`.\nSee [[dev-layout-and-discovery]], [[hook-two-copies-trap]], [[vendored-skill-install]].\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/project_status-report-sourcing.md\n\n---\nname: status-report-sourcing\ndescription: \"Where the Applied Performance status report gets its facts, and which sources lie\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: 412a89e3-b64f-44e6-9c87-4766d44e6c7a\n  modified: 2026-08-11T23:02:16.886Z\n---\n\nThe twice-monthly status report to Chris Ruf (cc Kenny Boyd) is specified by **KR 4.1**\nin `SecondBrain/Projects/Applied-Performance-OKRs-2026-27.md` — five sections\n(Shipped / In Progress / Decisions Needed / Risks–No-Surprises / Next Two Weeks),\nunder 300 words. Built by the `status-report` skill; template at\n`SecondBrain/Templates/status-report.md`.\n\n**Source rules that matter:**\n\n- **\"Shipped\" comes only from `SecondBrain/output/_ledger.md`** — never from\n  `portfolio.json` `stage`. The registry marks projects shipped that still have\n  stakeholder sign-off outstanding (Sport_Tech_Vetting was `shipped` while KR 1.2 showed\n  Chris's sign-off pending). Reporting that to a supervisor claims approval that doesn't\n  exist. Portfolio `stage` feeds \"In Progress\" only.\n- **Read the OKR doc (v3, 14 KRs), never the cadence doc's KR table** — that one is the\n  superseded v2 set of 9 and will produce wrong facts.\n- **`portfolio.json` mixes personal tooling with departmental work.** Filter\n  `lane == \"job\"`. Canonical reportable list lives at\n  `SecondBrain/Projects/Applied-Performance-Active-Projects.md`.\n- **Only 4 of 14 job-lane projects ladder to a KR**, and 10 of 14 KRs have no project.\n  The registry and the OKRs track different halves of the job; neither alone gives an\n  accurate picture.\n\n**Why the report exists:** primarily alignment, secondarily credit — specifically for\ninvolvement with sports beyond football and basketball. The sport tags and the PDF's\ncoverage panel are the mechanism, not decoration. Do not pad the coverage panel; a thin\none is real information and padding destroys the evidentiary value of the honest ones.\n\nRelated: [[fireflies-cli-broken-on-mac]]\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/project_supervisor-log-pipeline.md\n\n---\nname: project-supervisor-log-pipeline\ndescription: \"How the nightly supervisor log feeds the biweekly Kenny/Chris status report, and what the report still cannot source\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: 497f3a39-2043-486a-af25-95ec8b23acec\n  modified: 2026-09-08T02:17:39.699Z\n---\n\nBuilt 2026-09-07. `interview-me` opens with **Part 0 — supervisor log**: three factual\nquestions (equipment/installs, who you talked to + sport, waiting-on/risks), 90 seconds,\nasked *before* the four reflection questions. Output → `~/SecondBrain/Inbox/status-log/YYYY-MM-DD.md`\n(config key `vault_status_log`). Nothing to report writes **no file**.\n\n`status-report` reads that folder first (Step 2b item 1b), skips standing interview\nquestions the logs already answered, and prints \"N of D days logged\" in Step 8.\n\n**Why:** roughly two thirds of what belongs in the supervisor email — equipment arriving,\ninstalls, hallway conversations, follow-ups awaiting a reply — appears in **no** automated\nsource. `portfolio.json` and `_ledger.md` track building work; the report describes\ndepartmental operations. A draft from automated sources alone is about one-third finished.\n\n**How to apply:** Part 0 goes first and that ordering is the design, not a preference —\nreflection is expensive when tired and this ritual already lapsed 49 days once. The\n\"N of D\" count is the nudge; never backfill missing days to make it look better. Never\npad the log — `status-report` copies entries into an email to supervisors with light\nediting, so an invented install becomes a false claim in front of Chris Ruf.\n\nTraps the 09-07 cycle exposed:\n- Meeting **action items are assignments, not outcomes.** Three lines nearly shipped as\n  done that were only to-dos. Same failure class as `portfolio.json` `stage: shipped`.\n- Writing in ASD-STE/plain-English mode **strips Eric's contractions** and the email stops\n  sounding like him. Contract everything; the sent 08-24 reference is the voice test.\n- `fireflies-pp-cli action-items list` exits 0 and returns empty `{}` objects — reads as\n  \"no open commitments\" if trusted. Use the digest's inline action items.\n\nRelated: [[project-status-report-sourcing]], [[project-automation-cadence]]\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/project_tech-inventory-refresh.md\n\n---\nname: tech-inventory-refresh\ndescription: \"Baylor Tech Inventory survey — which forms platform is really live, why live responses cannot be pulled from the CLI, and the refresh command\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: b189fe96-f0c2-4eb0-87ed-6c5f33cadcd7\n  modified: 2026-09-08T22:39:48.668Z\n---\n\nAnalysis project: `~/DEV/Tech_Inventory_Analysis` (**not a git repo** — nothing to\ncommit there). Survey data feeds the framework in `~/DEV/SoftwareTech_Mgmt_Framework`.\n\n**The forms-platform trap.** The live survey runs on **Microsoft Forms**. The 08-29\nexport header row proves it: `ID · Start time · Completion time · Email · Name ·\nLast modified time`. But `SoftwareTech_Mgmt_Framework/docs/INVENTORY_FORMS_LINKS.md`\ndocuments two **Google** Forms with real form IDs. The Google forms are 08-13\nproposal-era prototypes — their dropdown has 26 options against the 33 units in\n`clean/coverage.csv`.\n\n**Consequence:** `gws` can reach those Google form IDs and will return few or no\nresponses. That is a **false negative**, not evidence that nobody new replied.\nLive responses cannot be reached from the CLI at all. Eric must download the\nexport from Microsoft Forms by hand.\n\n**Baseline checked 2026-09-07** (verified against the .xlsx itself, not the derived\nCSV): newest export anywhere on disk is `raw/Quick_Inventory_export_2026-08-29.xlsx`\n— 37 responses, 27 of 33 units, 147 distinct technologies. The OneDrive copy at\n`~/Library/CloudStorage/OneDrive-BaylorUniversity/Documents/` is byte-identical\n(same md5). Nothing newer exists in `~`.\n\n**Two OneDrive copies — one is stale.** `OneDrive-BaylorUniversity/Documents/\nQuick_Inventory_export_2026-08-29.xlsx` is current. But\n`OneDrive-BaylorUniversity/Athletics Technology - Quick Inventory.xlsx`, at the\nOneDrive **root** with the survey's display name, is the **08-24** export — md5\n`0f7874bbddb3cc10592805687aff6ea1`, identical to `raw/..._2026-08-24.xlsx`. Eric\nlinked that root file on 2026-09-08 believing it was the update. Its friendly name\nmakes it look canonical; it is five days behind. Always md5 an OneDrive copy against\n`raw/` before treating it as new.\n\nUnits with no response: Golf (M) · Tennis (M) · Tennis (W) · Volleyball ·\nBusiness Office. `Other` also shows zero but is a catch-all box, not a department —\ndo not report it as a sixth missing unit. Department soft deadline: **29 September 2026**,\nso gaps before then are expected, not a coverage failure.\n\n**Reminder sent 2026-09-08.** Eric emailed all five non-responding units as five\nseparate per-unit notes (not one group email), with a **Tuesday 29 September**\ndeadline. He edited the wording himself before sending, so the file record is the\nask, not a transcript: `~/DEV/SoftwareTech_Mgmt_Framework/docs/\nINVENTORY_REMINDER_2026-09-08-sent.md`. **Kenny Boyd and Jovon Overshown were\ndeliberately left off the CC** — the 08-13 original CC'd them, and holding that\nback keeps it available as escalation for a second reminder. Do not suggest\nsending a first reminder again; the open question is now whether responses arrived.\n\n**Refresh path:** save the download as `raw/Quick_Inventory_export_YYYY-MM-DD.xlsx`\n(never edit `raw/`), then\n`cd ~/DEV/Tech_Inventory_Analysis && uv run --with openpyxl python3 scripts/clean_inventory.py`.\nThe script always picks the newest `raw/` file. Then confirm `clean/unmapped_review.csv`\nis empty; if it has rows, add them to `scripts/aliases.py` and re-run.\n\n**Why:** the two documents disagree about which platform is live, and the disagreement\nis invisible unless you read the export's header row. Answering \"did any data change?\"\nfrom the Google forms would look like a clean check and be wrong.\n\n**How to apply:** to answer \"has anyone new responded\", first confirm no newer file\nexists on disk, then say plainly that a manual download is required — do not attempt\na `gws` pull. Read the header row before trusting the forms-links doc. Related:\n[[ath-tech-framework]] (the framework these findings feed), [[gws-forms-api]],\n[[dcg-guard-blocks]].\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/project_terminal-fluency-workspace.md\n\n---\nname: terminal-fluency-workspace\ndescription: \"Stateful CLI-learning workspace at ~/DEV/Terminal_Fluency — mission, resume protocol, and the teach-skill traps\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  modified: 2026-08-16T18:38:42.018Z\n  originSessionId: 7c7ca38f-e834-48b6-bae9-d7d80bbbbb95\n---\n\n`~/DEV/Terminal_Fluency` is a multi-session teaching workspace created 2026-08-13 via the\n`mattpocock-skills:teach` skill. Three lessons written as of 2026-08-16.\n\n**Mission:** fluency at the command line specifically to drive Claude Code and Codex rather than\nthe desktop/web UIs — control, composability, headless runs. *Not* general shell mastery; writing\nshell scripts and building CLIs are out of scope (the latter is [[printing-press-spec-traps]]).\n\n**To resume:** `cd ~/DEV/Terminal_Fluency`, then read `MISSION.md` → `NOTES.md` (teaching\npreferences, verified environment, lesson ledger) → newest `learning-records/`. Self-describing by\ndesign; do **not** re-interview about the mission. Lessons 01–03 done; 04 is sessions/permission\nmodes, 05 quoting+exit codes grounded in [[zsh-shell-traps]], 06 `--output-format json` + jq.\n\n**How he learns — the single most useful fact here.** Twice now the pattern has been: learn a\nconcept → find it applies to a real defect on his own machine → fix it → ask for the next lesson.\nBuild drills from his actual files and tools, never synthetic examples. A lesson that ends in a\nreal repair sticks; an exercise does not.\n\n**Non-obvious traps, all learned the hard way:**\n- The `teach` skill has `disable-model-invocation: true`, so it is absent from the registered skill\n  list and the Skill tool cannot load it. Read\n  `~/.claude/plugins/cache/mattpocock/mattpocock-skills/<ver>/skills/productivity/teach/SKILL.md`\n  plus its four `*-FORMAT.md` siblings and follow them directly.\n- The skill forbids parametric knowledge — every `RESOURCES.md` entry must be fetched before citing.\n- Glossary terms are promoted only after he *uses* them correctly, never on introduction. Four\n  lesson-01 terms are still pending; lesson 03's footer asks him to name them.\n- Standalone HTML needs an explicit `<meta charset=\"utf-8\">` or every em-dash renders as mojibake,\n  and a `.js` file's own non-ASCII breaks separately from the HTML's.\n- `~/DEV/CLI_Dev` is an unrelated empty stub. Not this workspace.\n\n**Why:** the mission grounds every lesson; losing it makes lessons drift abstract and the zone of\nproximal development uncomputable.\n**How to apply:** when Eric asks to continue learning the CLI, open this workspace rather than\nteaching ad hoc, and build the next lesson only after the previous drill has been reported.\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/project_vendored-skill-install.md\n\n---\nname: vendored-skill-install\ndescription: \"Installing a third-party skill so Claude Code, Codex and Cowork can all use it — the canonical store, the two symlinks, the CLI flag trap, and why Cowork is different\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: a6dfb3da-c339-44b1-a836-2a6884e07ea6\n  modified: 2026-09-04T13:43:21.110Z\n---\n\nThird-party skills live in **`~/.agents/skills/<name>/`** (the real files, ~360 of\nthem) with `~/.agents/.skill-lock.json` as the install ledger. Both harness\ndirectories are symlinks into it:\n\n- `~/.claude/skills/<name>` → `../../.agents/skills/<name>` (relative)\n- `~/.codex/skills/<name>` → `~/.claude/skills/<name>` (absolute, hopping through\n  the Claude symlink — verified on disk 2026-09-04. It does **not** point straight\n  at `~/.agents/skills/`.)\n\nInstall with the CLI that owns that store when you can:\n\n```bash\nnpx -y skills@latest add <owner>/<repo> -g -a claude-code -a codex -s <skill> -y\n```\n\n**Trap: in Claude Code auto mode the CLI itself is blocked.** The permission\nclassifier denies `npx -y skills@latest add …` outright (hit 2026-09-04).\nHand-install is then the working route, and it is enough — the files plus the two\nsymlinks are all a harness needs:\n\n```bash\nmkdir -p ~/.agents/skills/NAME\ncp REPO/CAT/NAME/SKILL.md ~/.agents/skills/NAME/\ncp -R REPO/CAT/NAME/scripts REPO/CAT/NAME/agents ~/.agents/skills/NAME/   # when present\nchmod +x ~/.agents/skills/NAME/scripts/*.sh\nln -s ../../.agents/skills/NAME ~/.claude/skills/NAME\nln -s ~/.claude/skills/NAME ~/.codex/skills/NAME\n```\n\nCopy `agents/openai.yaml` too whenever the skill ships one — it is the\nCodex-facing manifest, and dropping it leaves Codex with a half install.\n\n**Trap: never put `rm -rf` in the install script.** The `dcg` PreToolUse guard\nblocks the *entire* command on rule `core.filesystem:rm-rf-general`, even when the\ntarget is a subfolder you just created yourself. Copy only what you want instead of\ncopy-then-delete. `dcg` also false-positives on a `>` inside a heredoc'd Python\nscript (rule `core.filesystem:redirect-truncate-root-home`) — use the Edit tool for\nthat write rather than fighting the pattern.\n\n**Trap: a hand-install is invisible to the ledger.** It gets no entry in\n`~/.agents/.skill-lock.json`, so `skills update` will never touch it.\n`skillFolderHash` is not a plain sha1 of the files and is not worth\nreverse-engineering — have Eric run the official `add` command himself behind a `!`\nprefix to register it.\n\n**Trap: `-a` will not take a comma-separated list.** `-a claude-code,codex`\nfails with \"Invalid agents: claude-code,codex\" and then prints a valid-agent\nlist containing both names. Repeat the flag instead.\n\n**Trap: the installer does not always wire Codex.** It reports\n`universal: Codex` and creates no symlink. Check\n`ls -l ~/.codex/skills/<name>` after every install and make the symlink by hand\nif it is missing.\n\nThe CLI runs a security scan and may report alerts. Read\n`https://skills.sh/<owner>/<repo>` before trusting a skill.\n\n**Cowork cannot take a locally-dropped skill.** Its store is\n`~/Library/Application Support/Claude/local-agent-mode-sessions/skills-plugin/<a>/<b>/skills/`\nwith a sibling `manifest.json`, and every entry carries a **server-minted id**\n(`skill_01…`). A folder placed there is not in the manifest and is wiped on the\nnext sync. The only route is a zip uploaded through claude.ai → Settings →\nCapabilities → Skills, with the skill folder at the zip root. Expect Cowork's\nsandbox to lack ffmpeg/Chrome, so any skill's verification or media step will\nnot run there even after a successful upload.\n\nWhere a skill puts its *output* is separate: some resolve a workspace from the\nnearest `.git` walking up, so run them from inside the target project or set\ntheir env override, or builds land wherever cwd happened to be.\n\nSee [[vendored-skill-repair]] for what to do when one of these is broken, and\n[[path-shadowing-and-cleanup]] before deleting any copy.\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/project_vendored-skill-repair.md\n\n---\nname: vendored-skill-repair\ndescription: \"A vendored skill fails silently or with a confusing error — check for removed ffmpeg flags first, patch every live copy, and expect the fix to revert on update\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: a6dfb3da-c339-44b1-a836-2a6884e07ea6\n  modified: 2026-09-02T23:05:23.955Z\n---\n\nWhen a third-party skill produces **empty output rather than an error**, suspect\na CLI flag the underlying tool has since removed, not the skill's own logic.\n\n**The instance (2026-09-02):** `/watch` returned zero frames with no useful\nmessage. Cause: `frames.py` passed ffmpeg `-vsync`, removed in ffmpeg 7+. This\nmachine runs **ffmpeg 9.0.1**. Fix is `-vsync` → `-fps_mode` (same `vfr` value,\nsame position, exists since ffmpeg 5.0).\n\n**Patch every live copy, and know which ones matter.** A skill can exist three\ntimes on this machine:\n\n1. `~/.agents/skills/<name>/…` — the vendor store, serves `~/.claude/skills/` and Codex.\n2. `~/.claude/plugins/cache/<plugin>/<skill>/<version>/skills/<name>/…` — the\n   plugin copy. **This is the one a `/slash` invocation actually loads.** Patch\n   only the vendor store and the skill stays broken.\n3. The Cowork copy under `Library/Application Support/Claude/…` — **do not\n   patch.** It is server-synced and reverts. See [[vendored-skill-install]].\n\nConfirm which file is live by reading the \"Base directory for this skill\" line\nthe harness prints when the skill loads.\n\n**Every such patch is temporary.** Check upstream before assuming a fix is\nneeded, and after patching, assume `skills update` or a plugin update reinstalls\nthe bug. For `/watch` the upstream is `bradautomates/claude-video`, still\ncarrying `-vsync` as of its 2026-06-30 commit.\n\nVerify by running the affected code path and **counting the output**, not by\nreading the exit code — the original failure exited 0.\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/project_zsh-shell-traps.md\n\n---\nname: zsh-shell-traps\ndescription: \"zsh-specific shell traps hit repeatedly on this macOS machine — path/UID/status reserved variables, word-splitting\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: a86cfb00-e6d1-46b9-8cdc-35e21acd9931\n  modified: 2026-08-07T00:53:25.947Z\n---\n\nzsh quirks that have repeatedly broken scripts in Claude sessions on this machine:\n\n- **Never use `path` as a variable name** — zsh ties lowercase `path` (array) to `PATH`; assigning it a scalar clobbers PATH mid-script (\"command not found\" for everything after).\n- `UID` and `status` are read-only in zsh — use `ZUID`, `st`, etc.\n- Unquoted `$VAR` does NOT word-split in zsh — use `${=VAR}` when splitting is intended.\n- Background-task shells get a minimal PATH; start scripts with `export PATH=\"/usr/bin:/bin:/opt/homebrew/bin:$PATH\"` and use `$HOME/go/bin` for Go CLIs.\n- `head` at the end of a pipeline masks grep's exit code — use `grep -q` for presence tests, never `grep -l ... | head && echo FOUND`.\n- **git fetch/push/pull to private repos hangs silently** in sandboxed/background shells — no keychain access means git blocks on an invisible credential prompt (looks exactly like a network outage; `ls-remote` on public paths still works). Fix: `GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0 git -c credential.helper= -c 'credential.helper=!gh auth git-credential' <cmd>`.\n\n**Why:** each cost a debugging round in the Aug 2026 restore/printing sessions.\n**How to apply:** prefer these idioms proactively in any zsh/bash-on-macOS scripting here.\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/reference_zero-edit-youtube-pipeline.md\n\n---\nname: zero-edit-youtube-pipeline\ndescription: \"Jack Roberts' 5-phase Claude Code video pipeline — full breakdown lives in the SecondBrain vault\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: reference\n  originSessionId: a7e959b5-d0eb-427b-b807-3a7774559ff9\n---\n\nVault note: `~/SecondBrain/Knowledge/frameworks/zero-edit-youtube-pipeline-claude-code.md` (source: youtube.com/watch?v=SwKW_D964Tk, Jack Roberts).\n\nThe \"ZeroEdit\" pipeline Eric wants to replicate: Phase 0 deconstruct winning channels' formulas with Claude Code → Phase 1 find outlier topics (1of10, ≥2× outlier score, recent) → Phase 2 deep-research + script beats (human rewrites the words) → Phase 3 record own voice, enhance via Auphonic API → Phase 4 timestamp audio + generate synced images via Higgsfield CLI → Phase 5 Claude assembles the finished video (~$2/45-min video). Key rules: no AI voiceover, no verbatim AI scripts, medium model strength for execution / max for foundational research.\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/user_child-dental-plan.md\n\n---\nname: child-dental-plan\ndescription: \"Eric's 4-year-old has cavities; the open next step is a pediatric dentist and the SDF / n-HAp treatment question\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: user\n  originSessionId: 9422b1a6-8624-4c86-a4f6-3c4b1a4c9fc8\n  modified: 2026-09-09T15:24:09.736Z\n---\n\nEric's 4-year-old was found to have cavities on 2026-06-25.\n\n**Open next step:** book a *pediatric* dentist, and raise two minimally-invasive\noptions in that visit:\n\n- **SDF** — silver diamine fluoride, a brush-on liquid that arrests a cavity without\n  drilling. Trade-off: it stains the treated spot black.\n- **n-HAp** — nano-hydroxyapatite, a fluoride-free remineralising toothpaste\n  ingredient, used for prevention and early lesions rather than as a fix for an\n  existing cavity.\n\nStill open as of 2026-09-09 — no appointment booked yet. This moved out of Tier B\nworking memory on that date to free cap headroom; it is a slow-moving personal\nto-do, not an active work thread. Deeper personal context: `~/DEV/ContextVault`.\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/user_eli5-output-style.md\n\n---\nname: ELI5 output style\ndescription: Eric prefers concise, plain-language output across Claude Code and Codex.\nmetadata:\n  type: user\n---\n\nUse the ELI5 output style: ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English, active voice, one idea per sentence, short words, short paragraphs, and only necessary details. Reports should say what changed, whether it worked, and what Eric should do next. Give no more than two options for decisions and recommend one. Keep paths and commands exact. Codex mirrors this preference through `model_verbosity = \"low\"` and `developer_instructions` in `~/.codex/config.toml`.\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/user_sports-teams.md\n\n---\nname: sports-teams\ndescription: \"Sports teams the user follows — use for score, schedule, and game-summary requests\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: user\n  originSessionId: 03577981-6908-4eec-a664-27fdaa0e34e5\n---\n\nThe user follows the **Indiana Fever** (WNBA, Eastern Conference; ESPN team id `5`, abbr `IND`, home: Gainbridge Fieldhouse, Indianapolis). Default to this team for ambiguous WNBA score/schedule asks.\n\nBuilt the user a printable 2026 Fever schedule (HTML + PDF, full + one-page variants) via the [[pp-espn]] CLI skill at `~/DEV/indiana-fever-schedule/`. The wife is a Fever fan — the schedule was a gift for her.\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/user_working-conditions-bottleneck.md\n\n---\nname: working-conditions-bottleneck\ndescription: \"Eric's self-identified bottleneck is working conditions, not effort — he needs a thinking partner / sharing outlet, and lacking one he goes looking for people and loses time\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: user\n  originSessionId: 583baf57-8dc4-46c7-8842-501ae1fdeb04\n  modified: 2026-08-03T23:18:09.932Z\n---\n\nEric's limiting factor on deep work is environmental, not motivational. He is highly productive in short bursts once work actually starts; what breaks is the path to starting. The specific disruptor he named (2026-07-28) is the pull to share what he's working on — with no standing outlet for it, he goes and finds people, which costs him the session.\n\n**Corrected 2026-07-29.** The pull toward people is not a leak, it's the generative stage. His own observation: alone he wants company; in company the project ideas arrive. Solitude is where he refines, not where he generates. A long unbroken solo block with nothing in the hopper is waiting, not deep work.\n\n**Second brake, same day:** he holds ideas back from fear of overstepping into others' lanes, against an unstated expectation that he act unasked. He is deliberately moving off this position (\"if I don't act, ideas wither\"). Pressfield's Resistance is his frame for it.\n\n**Qualified 2026-08-03.** The permission gate is not entirely self-imposed. He acted unasked on a device another staffer was already championing (ShiftWave chair, second trial unit) and took real organizational blowback for it — careful, low-key handling bought nothing, because the claim was made by initiating, not by presenting. So the caution is warranted on ground someone has visibly attached themselves to, and unwarranted everywhere else. His same-day reaction (\"pander to the people in power, keep your head down\") is a bruise, not necessarily a settled position — don't treat it as one.\n\n**Why:** Both reframe what looks like procrastination. The first is an unmet structural need (input/ideation) improvising in real time; the second is a self-imposed permission gate that reads as professional courtesy. Neither is a discipline failure, and treating them as one produces useless advice.\n\n**How to apply:** Act as the thinking partner in-session — reflect his reasoning back, name what's interesting in it, and give him something to react to rather than only executing tasks silently. When he's ideating, don't rush to implementation; the ideation *is* the need being met, and it's the stage that feeds everything else — help him capture what surfaces. Prefer short, well-scoped blocks with a clear starting line. When he hesitates on whether something is his to do, first ask whether anyone has visibly claimed it — if not, name the permission gate rather than validating the caution; if someone has, the hesitation is a correct read and the useful move is how to approach the owner, not encouragement to proceed. Related: [[company-generates-solitude-refines]], [[waiting-for-the-ask-kills-the-idea]], [[ownership-not-merit-governs-the-reaction]], [[design-the-conditions-not-the-discipline]] in the SecondBrain vault.\n\n\n## Instructions\n\nLook for:\n- Direct contradictions (one file says X, another says not-X)\n- The same fact stated in two files, where one is more current than the other\n- Statements that name a path, tool, schedule or version that another file says has moved,\n  been retired, or been replaced\n- Advice in one file that a later file's experience overturns\n\nFor each issue found, output EXACTLY one line in this format:\nCONTRADICTION: [file1] vs [file2] - description of the conflict\nREDUNDANT: [file1] vs [file2] - what is duplicated and which looks newer\nSTALE: [file] - what it asserts and what supersedes it\n\nIf no issues found, output exactly: NO_ISSUES\n\nDo NOT output anything else - no preamble, no explanation, just the formatted lines."},"uuid":"2fc77778-965a-4e5c-871f-5b4d631296b4","timestamp":"2026-09-13T07:32:13.360Z","permissionMode":"auto","promptSource":"sdk","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/global-memory","sessionId":"c922703c-e012-455a-8f07-d26099b07dc4","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"2fc77778-965a-4e5c-871f-5b4d631296b4","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"deferred_tools_delta","addedNames":["CronCreate","CronDelete","CronList","DesignSync","EnterWorktree","ExitWorktree","ListMcpResourcesTool","Monitor","NotebookEdit","PushNotification","ReadMcpResourceDirTool","ReadMcpResourceTool","RemoteTrigger","SendMessage","TaskCreate","TaskGet","TaskList","TaskOutput","TaskStop","TaskUpdate","WebFetch","WebSearch","mcp__claude_ai_Excalidraw__create_view","mcp__claude_ai_Excalidraw__export_to_excalidraw","mcp__claude_ai_Excalidraw__read_checkpoint","mcp__claude_ai_Excalidraw__read_me","mcp__claude_ai_Excalidraw__save_checkpoint","mcp__claude_ai_Fireflies__fireflies_create_soundbite","mcp__claude_ai_Fireflies__fireflies_fetch","mcp__claude_ai_Fireflies__fireflies_get_active_meetings","mcp__claude_ai_Fireflies__fireflies_get_analytics","mcp__claude_ai_Fireflies__fireflies_get_channel","mcp__claude_ai_Fireflies__fireflies_get_rule_executions","mcp__claude_ai_Fireflies__fireflies_get_soundbites","mcp__claude_ai_Fireflies__fireflies_get_summary","mcp__claude_ai_Fireflies__fireflies_get_transcript","mcp__claude_ai_Fireflies__fireflies_get_transcripts","mcp__claude_ai_Fireflies__fireflies_get_user","mcp__claude_ai_Fireflies__fireflies_get_user_contacts","mcp__claude_ai_Fireflies__fireflies_get_usergroups","mcp__claude_ai_Fireflies__fireflies_list_channels","mcp__claude_ai_Fireflies__fireflies_move_meeting","mcp__claude_ai_Fireflies__fireflies_revoke_meeting_access","mcp__claude_ai_Fireflies__fireflies_search","mcp__claude_ai_Fireflies__fireflies_share_meeting","mcp__claude_ai_Fireflies__fireflies_update_meeting_privacy","mcp__claude_ai_Fireflies__fireflies_update_meeting_title","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__apply_sensitive_message_label","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__apply_sensitive_thread_label","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__create_draft","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__create_label","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__delete_label","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__forward","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__get_draft","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__get_message","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__get_thread","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__label_message","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__label_thread","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__list_drafts","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__list_labels","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__mark_message_spam","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__mark_thread_spam","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__reply","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__search_threads","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__send_message","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__trash_message","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__trash_thread","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__unlabel_message","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__unlabel_thread","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__unmark_message_spam","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__unmark_thread_spam","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__untrash_message","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__untrash_thread","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__update_draft","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__update_label","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__update_message_labels","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Calendar__create_event","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Calendar__delete_event","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Calendar__get_event","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Calendar__list_calendars","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Calendar__list_events","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Calendar__respond_to_event","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Calendar__search_events","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Calendar__suggest_time","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Calendar__update_event","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__copy_file","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__create_file","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__download_file_content","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__get_file_metadata","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__get_file_permissions","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__list_recent_files","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__read_file_content","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__search_files","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__share_file","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__trash_file","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__update_file","mcp__claude_ai_HyperFrames_by_HeyGen__compose","mcp__claude_ai_HyperFrames_by_HeyGen__get-design-import-job-status","mcp__claude_ai_HyperFrames_by_HeyGen__get-send-to-hyperframes-guide","mcp__claude_ai_HyperFrames_by_HeyGen__get_project","mcp__claude_ai_HyperFrames_by_HeyGen__get_project_status","mcp__claude_ai_HyperFrames_by_HeyGen__get_render_status","mcp__claude_ai_HyperFrames_by_HeyGen__import-claude-design-from-url","mcp__claude_ai_HyperFrames_by_HeyGen__internal_get_project_status","mcp__claude_ai_HyperFrames_by_HeyGen__internal_get_render_status","mcp__claude_ai_HyperFrames_by_HeyGen__list_projects","mcp__claude_ai_HyperFrames_by_HeyGen__render_video","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_doctor","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_index","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_insight","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_purge","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_stats","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_upgrade","mcp__zotero__analytics","mcp__zotero__cache","mcp__zotero__cache_index_fulltext","mcp__zotero__cache_reindex","mcp__zotero__cache_status","mcp__zotero__cite","mcp__zotero__collections_get","mcp__zotero__collections_items","mcp__zotero__collections_list","mcp__zotero__collections_subcollections","mcp__zotero__collections_top","mcp__zotero__collections_tree","mcp__zotero__context","mcp__zotero__deleted_list","mcp__zotero__export","mcp__zotero__full_text_deep_search","mcp__zotero__fulltext_changed","mcp__zotero__ground","mcp__zotero__items_children","mcp__zotero__items_fulltext","mcp__zotero__items_get","mcp__zotero__items_list","mcp__zotero__items_recent","mcp__zotero__items_top","mcp__zotero__items_trash","mcp__zotero__keys_current","mcp__zotero__learnings","mcp__zotero__learnings_candidates","mcp__zotero__learnings_confirm","mcp__zotero__learnings_forget","mcp__zotero__learnings_list","mcp__zotero__learnings_reject","mcp__zotero__learnings_stats","mcp__zotero__playbook","mcp__zotero__playbook_amend","mcp__zotero__playbook_list","mcp__zotero__recall","mcp__zotero__search","mcp__zotero__searches_get","mcp__zotero__searches_list","mcp__zotero__sql","mcp__zotero__sync","mcp__zotero__tags_list","mcp__zotero__tail","mcp__zotero__teach","mcp__zotero__teach_lookup","mcp__zotero__teach_pattern","mcp__zotero__teach_playbook","mcp__zotero__workflow","mcp__zotero__workflow_archive","mcp__zotero__workflow_status"],"addedLines":["CronCreate","CronDelete","CronList","DesignSync","EnterWorktree","ExitWorktree","ListMcpResourcesTool","Monitor","NotebookEdit","PushNotification","ReadMcpResourceDirTool","ReadMcpResourceTool","RemoteTrigger","SendMessage","TaskCreate","TaskGet","TaskList","TaskOutput","TaskStop","TaskUpdate","WebFetch","WebSearch","mcp__claude_ai_Excalidraw__create_view","mcp__claude_ai_Excalidraw__export_to_excalidraw","mcp__claude_ai_Excalidraw__read_checkpoint","mcp__claude_ai_Excalidraw__read_me","mcp__claude_ai_Excalidraw__save_checkpoint","mcp__claude_ai_Fireflies__fireflies_create_soundbite","mcp__claude_ai_Fireflies__fireflies_fetch","mcp__claude_ai_Fireflies__fireflies_get_active_meetings","mcp__claude_ai_Fireflies__fireflies_get_analytics","mcp__claude_ai_Fireflies__fireflies_get_channel","mcp__claude_ai_Fireflies__fireflies_get_rule_executions","mcp__claude_ai_Fireflies__fireflies_get_soundbites","mcp__claude_ai_Fireflies__fireflies_get_summary","mcp__claude_ai_Fireflies__fireflies_get_transcript","mcp__claude_ai_Fireflies__fireflies_get_transcripts","mcp__claude_ai_Fireflies__fireflies_get_user","mcp__claude_ai_Fireflies__fireflies_get_user_contacts","mcp__claude_ai_Fireflies__fireflies_get_usergroups","mcp__claude_ai_Fireflies__fireflies_list_channels","mcp__claude_ai_Fireflies__fireflies_move_meeting","mcp__claude_ai_Fireflies__fireflies_revoke_meeting_access","mcp__claude_ai_Fireflies__fireflies_search","mcp__claude_ai_Fireflies__fireflies_share_meeting","mcp__claude_ai_Fireflies__fireflies_update_meeting_privacy","mcp__claude_ai_Fireflies__fireflies_update_meeting_title","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__apply_sensitive_message_label","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__apply_sensitive_thread_label","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__create_draft","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__create_label","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__delete_label","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__forward","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__get_draft","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__get_message","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__get_thread","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__label_message","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__label_thread","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__list_drafts","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__list_labels","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__mark_message_spam","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__mark_thread_spam","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__reply","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__search_threads","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__send_message","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__trash_message","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__trash_thread","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__unlabel_message","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__unlabel_thread","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__unmark_message_spam","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__unmark_thread_spam","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__untrash_message","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__untrash_thread","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__update_draft","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__update_label","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__update_message_labels","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Calendar__create_event","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Calendar__delete_event","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Calendar__get_event","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Calendar__list_calendars","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Calendar__list_events","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Calendar__respond_to_event","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Calendar__search_events","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Calendar__suggest_time","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Calendar__update_event","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__copy_file","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__create_file","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__download_file_content","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__get_file_metadata","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__get_file_permissions","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__list_recent_files","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__read_file_content","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__search_files","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__share_file","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__trash_file","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__update_file","mcp__claude_ai_HyperFrames_by_HeyGen__compose","mcp__claude_ai_HyperFrames_by_HeyGen__get-design-import-job-status","mcp__claude_ai_HyperFrames_by_HeyGen__get-send-to-hyperframes-guide","mcp__claude_ai_HyperFrames_by_HeyGen__get_project","mcp__claude_ai_HyperFrames_by_HeyGen__get_project_status","mcp__claude_ai_HyperFrames_by_HeyGen__get_render_status","mcp__claude_ai_HyperFrames_by_HeyGen__import-claude-design-from-url","mcp__claude_ai_HyperFrames_by_HeyGen__internal_get_project_status","mcp__claude_ai_HyperFrames_by_HeyGen__internal_get_render_status","mcp__claude_ai_HyperFrames_by_HeyGen__list_projects","mcp__claude_ai_HyperFrames_by_HeyGen__render_video","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_doctor","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_index","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_insight","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_purge","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_stats","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_upgrade","mcp__zotero__analytics","mcp__zotero__cache","mcp__zotero__cache_index_fulltext","mcp__zotero__cache_reindex","mcp__zotero__cache_status","mcp__zotero__cite","mcp__zotero__collections_get","mcp__zotero__collections_items","mcp__zotero__collections_list","mcp__zotero__collections_subcollections","mcp__zotero__collections_top","mcp__zotero__collections_tree","mcp__zotero__context","mcp__zotero__deleted_list","mcp__zotero__export","mcp__zotero__full_text_deep_search","mcp__zotero__fulltext_changed","mcp__zotero__ground","mcp__zotero__items_children","mcp__zotero__items_fulltext","mcp__zotero__items_get","mcp__zotero__items_list","mcp__zotero__items_recent","mcp__zotero__items_top","mcp__zotero__items_trash","mcp__zotero__keys_current","mcp__zotero__learnings","mcp__zotero__learnings_candidates","mcp__zotero__learnings_confirm","mcp__zotero__learnings_forget","mcp__zotero__learnings_list","mcp__zotero__learnings_reject","mcp__zotero__learnings_stats","mcp__zotero__playbook","mcp__zotero__playbook_amend","mcp__zotero__playbook_list","mcp__zotero__recall","mcp__zotero__search","mcp__zotero__searches_get","mcp__zotero__searches_list","mcp__zotero__sql","mcp__zotero__sync","mcp__zotero__tags_list","mcp__zotero__tail","mcp__zotero__teach","mcp__zotero__teach_lookup","mcp__zotero__teach_pattern","mcp__zotero__teach_playbook","mcp__zotero__workflow","mcp__zotero__workflow_archive","mcp__zotero__workflow_status"],"removedNames":[],"readdedNames":[],"pendingMcpServers":[],"needsAuthMcpServers":[]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"780f13be-bdd8-4937-b561-931282248b1a","timestamp":"2026-09-13T07:32:13.354Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/global-memory","sessionId":"c922703c-e012-455a-8f07-d26099b07dc4","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"780f13be-bdd8-4937-b561-931282248b1a","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"agent_listing_delta","addedTypes":["claude","codex:codex-rescue","Explore","general-purpose","Plan","statusline-setup"],"addedLines":["- claude: Catch-all for any task that doesn't fit a more specific agent. FleetView's default when no agent name is typed. (Tools: *)","- codex:codex-rescue: Proactively use when Claude Code is stuck, wants a second implementation or diagnosis pass, needs a deeper root-cause investigation, or should hand a substantial coding task to Codex through the shared runtime (Tools: Bash)","- Explore: Read-only search agent for broad fan-out searches — when answering means sweeping many files, directories, or naming conventions and you only need the conclusion, not the file dumps. It reads excerpts rather than whole files, so it locates code; it doesn't review or audit it. Specify search breadth: \"medium\" for moderate exploration, \"very thorough\" for multiple locations and naming conventions. (Tools: All tools except Agent, Artifact, ExitPlanMode, Edit, Write, NotebookEdit)","- general-purpose: General-purpose agent for researching complex questions, searching for code, and executing multi-step tasks. When you are searching for a keyword or file and are not confident that you will find the right match in the first few tries use this agent to perform the search for you. (Tools: *)","- Plan: Software architect agent for designing implementation plans. Use this when you need to plan the implementation strategy for a task. Returns step-by-step plans, identifies critical files, and considers architectural trade-offs. (Tools: All tools except Agent, Artifact, ExitPlanMode, Edit, Write, NotebookEdit)","- statusline-setup: Use this agent to configure the user's Claude Code status line setting. (Tools: Read, Edit)"],"removedTypes":[],"isInitial":true,"showConcurrencyNote":true},"type":"attachment","uuid":"98a9f33d-12ae-43de-bff5-3653bcebaa78","timestamp":"2026-09-13T07:32:13.354Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/global-memory","sessionId":"c922703c-e012-455a-8f07-d26099b07dc4","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"98a9f33d-12ae-43de-bff5-3653bcebaa78","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"mcp_instructions_delta","addedNames":["claude.ai HyperFrames by HeyGen"],"addedBlocks":["## claude.ai HyperFrames by HeyGen\n\n[HeyGen HyperFrames MCP] — Programmable HTML video projects.\n\n## When to use this MCP\n\nThis MCP is the primary HyperFrames surface for hosted chat clients (Claude.ai\nweb/desktop, ChatGPT, Grok, and similar), where the user has no local filesystem\nand the agent cannot author HyperFrames code directly.\n\nFrom a CLI/IDE agent on the user's machine (Claude Code, Cursor, Codex, the\nlocalhost MCP Inspector, etc.), **`compose` and `render_video` are disabled** — these clients have a local\nfilesystem, so author HyperFrames with the local HyperFrames skills instead, which\nproduce standalone HTML compositions the user can edit, diff, and commit. Calls to\nthose two tools from such clients are rejected with a pointer to install them:\n\n    npx skills add heygen-com/hyperframes\n\nThe read tools (list_projects, get_project, get_project_status, get_render_status)\nstay available to every client.\n\nAfter installation they expose slash commands like `/hyperframes` (compose),\n`/hyperframes-cli` (init, lint, preview, render), and `/hyperframes-media`\n(TTS, transcribe, remove-background). See\nhttps://github.com/heygen-com/hyperframes for the full skill list and docs.\n\nBut the skills and this MCP are *not* drop-in equivalents:\n\n- Local skills → standalone HTML/GSAP files on disk. No `project_id`, no hosted\n  artifact, no integrated cloud render pipeline.\n- This MCP → hosted HeyGen project with a `project_id`, a live canvas at\n  `app.heygen.com/projects/{id}`, and the cloud render queue.\n\nRoute by what the user actually wants:\n- \"Make a local HTML composition / something I can edit\" → use a local skill.\n- \"Make a HeyGen project I can share / render in the cloud / open in the app\"\n  → use this MCP, even from a CLI agent.\n\n## What it does\n\nHyperFrames is HeyGen's framework for creating, editing, and rendering programmable\nHTML-based video projects. Projects are authored interactively (Magic Edit\nchat-driven flow) and rendered to MP4, WebM, or MOV at any framerate. Tools exposed\nhere cover composing, listing, fetching, and rendering projects… [truncated]"],"removedNames":[]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"6f04aa83-e39a-4615-aff0-9b928ff1be6a","timestamp":"2026-09-13T07:32:13.354Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/global-memory","sessionId":"c922703c-e012-455a-8f07-d26099b07dc4","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"6f04aa83-e39a-4615-aff0-9b928ff1be6a","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"skill_listing","content":"- anti-sleep: Keep the user's MacBook awake reliably with macOS caffeinate for a set duration or while a process runs. Use for \"don't let my Mac sleep\", \"keep the screen on\", \"anti-sleep\", \"caffeinate\", overnight work, or long builds. Unlike a normal background job, it survives temporary agent-shell cleanup.\n- ArXiv: Search and retrieve arXiv academic papers by topic, category, or paper ID — with AlphaXiv-enriched AI-generated overviews. Uses arXiv Atom API across cs.AI/cs.LG/cs.CL/cs.CR/cs.MA/cs.SE/cs.IR. Three workflows: Latest, Search, Paper. USE WHEN arxiv, papers, latest papers, research papers, recent ML papers, paper lookup, summarize paper, latest LLM papers, AI safety papers, cs.AI latest. NOT FOR general research (Research), URL parsing (_PARSER), or annual reports (_ANNUALREPORTS).\n- backup: Run the KeystoneOS daily backup on demand (git-push the memory repos, rsync state to Google Drive and the CRASHBOX SSD, reindex memsearch), then report what was saved and what was skipped. Use when the user says \"/backup\", \"run the backup\", \"back up now\", \"back my stuff up\", \"did the backup run\", \"the backup failed\", \"I left the laptop off — catch up the backup\", or when a session-start HOOK FAILURE names daily-backup. The scheduled launchd job only fires if the Mac is awake at 09:00, so this is the manual catch-up path. NOT FOR whole-stack health checks (use claude-stack-doctor).\n- brain-audit: Score the second brain as a system — the four C's (context, connections, capabilities, cadence) — write a dated report to ~/SecondBrain/audits/, append the score to a trend table, and end by proposing exactly two next builds. Use when the user says \"/brain-audit\", \"audit my second brain\", \"score my brain\", \"is my second brain working\", \"how healthy is my system\", \"level up my brain\", \"what should I build next for my brain\", or when a monthly ritual is due. NOT FOR automation plumbing — launchd jobs, venvs, backup freshness (use claude-stack-doctor). NOT FOR note-level rot — stale seeds, duplicates, conflicting claims (use improve-system).\n- capture-insight: Capture a story, win, or lesson the user just shared as a permanent atomic note in the SecondBrain vault. Use when the user tells a story or lesson and says \"capture this\", \"save this win/lesson\", \"that's worth a note\", or \"/capture-insight\". Do not use for end-of-day structured capture (use interview-me), whole-project distillation (use harvest), or plain facts (use memory-write).\n- claude-stack-doctor: Health-check Eric's automation/memory stack in one pass and verify the Mac-to-Windows handoff works: launchd agents, Python venvs, memsearch, the memory-compiler flush pipeline, backup freshness, and context budget. Use when the user says \"/claude-stack-doctor\", \"stack doctor\", \"is my stack healthy\", \"did my scheduled jobs run\", \"check the memory pipeline\", \"why didn't the backup run\", or when closeout/morning sessions should verify automation health. Do not use to run the backup itself (use backup) or to audit context-engineering practice (use doctor-plus).\n- claudex-loop: Harden a plan with independent Claude/Codex review, then optionally build and cross-inspect it. Start in either Claude Code or Codex: the host plans and the other provider reviews. Use for claudex this plan, claudex-loop, or the legacy crucible trigger; not for trivial edits.\n- claudex-route: Recommend a model and a scoped handoff for a task in Claude Code or Codex. Use when choosing who should handle a task, seeking a second opinion, getting unstuck, or delegating focused work; execute one handoff when requested.\n- cli-hub-meta-skill: Discover agent-native CLIs for professional software. Access the live catalog to find tools for creative workflows, productivity, AI, and more.\n- closeout: End-of-session sign-off ritual. Sweeps loose ends, commits and pushes what needs it, verifies memory capture, writes a cross-harness handoff, and reports \"clear to sign off\" or what's still open. Use when the user says \"/closeout\", \"any loose ends?\", \"am I good to sign off?\", \"close out for the night\", \"closing out\", \"goodnight\", \"wrap up for the day\", or asks for a system health check before leaving. Do not use at the start of a session (use startup) or for a standalone automation health check (use claude-stack-doctor).\n- code-review: Review the changes since a fixed point (commit, branch, tag, or merge-base) along two axes — Standards (does the code follow this repo's documented coding standards?) and Spec (does the code match what the originating issue/PRD asked for?). Runs both reviews in parallel sub-agents and reports them side by side. Use when the user wants to review a branch, a PR, work-in-progress changes, or asks to \"review since X\".\n- codex-build: Have Codex implement a concrete work order and have Claude independently inspect the final changes. Preserves the explicit Codex builder choice; use claudex-loop for host-based planning and automatic reviewer selection.\n- codex-review: Have Codex independently review an existing implementation plan while Claude coordinates a bounded revision loop. For automatic reviewer selection from either host, use claudex-loop with mode=review.\n- CreateSkill: Invoke BEFORE any skill work — creating, modifying, adding a workflow/tool to, renaming, validating, or canonicalizing any skill. Required by skills/CLAUDE.md; the single home for skill authoring in this workspace. Owns the skill lifecycle: scaffold (public TitleCase vs private _ALLCAPS, flat 2-level, Gotchas + Examples, BPE check), validate, canonicalize, test, improve, optimize descriptions. USE WHEN create skill, new skill, make a skill, build a skill, set up a skill, private skill, make a X skill, add a workflow, add a tool, edit/change/update/rename a skill, skill frontmatter, validate skill, check skill, canonicalize, scaffold skill, test skill, improve skill, optimize description, skill not triggering, overtriggering. NOT FOR TypeScript CLI generation (use CreateCLI).\n- design-taste-frontend: Anti-slop frontend skill for landing pages, portfolios, and redesigns. The agent reads the brief, infers the right design direction, and ships interfaces that do not look templated. Real design systems when applicable, audit-first on redesigns, strict pre-flight check.\n- dev-sync: Bring this machine into line with GitHub and load back the context that came with it: locate DEV automatically, pull config and skills, inventory every project, commit and push local work, pull the other machine's work, clone what is missing, report conflicts, then read the repo's STATUS and latest handoff and report where the last session left off. Use when Eric switches machines, sits down to start work, says 'sync DEV', asks whether everything is pushed, or asks where he left off. Do not use when Eric says '/startup' or 'good morning' (use startup, which runs this sync) or to scaffold a new repo (use new-project).\n- doctor-plus: Runs Claude Code's built-in /doctor health check, then audits the workspace against the 6 then-and-now context-engineering shifts Anthropic shipped with the Claude 5 models (rules to judgement, examples to interfaces, upfront to progressive disclosure, repeats to tool descriptions, CLAUDE.md memory to auto-memory, simple specs to rich references). Reports findings first, fixes only on approval. Use when the user says \"/doctor-plus\", \"doctor plus\", \"doctor-plus\", \"extended doctor\", \"then and now audit\", \"context checkup\". Do not use to check launchd jobs, the memory pipeline, or backups (use claude-stack-doctor).\n- fireflies-intake: One-command Fireflies pipeline — sync new meeting transcripts into the SecondBrain vault, rename generically-titled meetings, then report exactly what was ingested. Use when the user says \"/fireflies-intake\", \"ingest my recent fireflies meetings\", \"sync meetings that aren't in the second brain yet\", \"pull in the new meetings\", \"meeting ingestion\", or asks whether all recent meetings have been ingested. Do not use to search what was said in transcripts (use pp-fireflies).\n- five-slides-generator: Generate a '5 Slides in 5 Minutes' PowerPoint deck for Baylor Athletics Applied Performance — research, narrative, pptxgenjs, .pptx build, QA. Use when the user mentions '5 slides,' '5 slides in 5 minutes,' or gives a topic they want a Performance School deck on. Also trigger for a cross-functional staff presentation, a quick sport science deck, or any short educational deck in the established series format (CMJ, GPS, VBT are prior examples). Do not use for Power BI report pages (use pbir-report-builder) or to turn an existing HTML deck into a PDF (use html-to-pdf).\n- forecast-capture: Capture and freeze the CFB Victory Model's pre-game forecasts before kickoff, then report what moved since last week. Run every Wednesday during the season. Use when the user says \"/forecast-capture\", \"capture the forecast\", \"freeze this week's predictions\", \"run the CFB capture\", \"did we capture this week\", or asks whether the weekly model snapshot has been taken. Do not use for live scores, standings, or schedules (use pp-espn).\n- game-film-timestamps: Fix wrong startTime/endTime values in football game film JSON exported from Hudl, DVSport, or similar. Fetches correct wall-clock timestamps from the ESPN API and writes corrected copies. Use when the user mentions fixing or correcting play-by-play timestamps in game film JSON, or has files with \"OFF\", \"DEF\", or \"KICKS\" in the filename and mentions timestamps or time issues. Do not use for live scores or schedules (use pp-espn) or for GPS session data (use pp-catapult-openfield-connect).\n- graphify: Use for any question about a codebase, its architecture, file relationships, or project content — especially when graphify-out/ exists, where the question should be treated as a graphify query first. Turns any input (code, docs, papers, images, videos) into a persistent knowledge graph with god nodes, community detection, and query/path/explain tools. Do not use for a quick lookup of one known file or symbol, or for sport-science literature questions (use sports-science-research-grounding).\n- harvest: Distill a finished project into evergreen Knowledge/ articles in the SecondBrain vault and log the shipped artifact in the output ledger. Use when the user says \"/harvest <project>\", \"harvest this project\", \"distill what we learned from X\", declares a project finished/shipped/done, or when a session ends with a clearly shipped deliverable worth recording. Do not use for a single story or lesson (use capture-insight) or for the end-of-session sign-off (use closeout).\n- html-to-pdf: Render any local HTML report, deck, or dashboard to a print-ready PDF via headless Chrome — replaces the manual Ctrl+P → Save as PDF ritual. Use when the user says \"export this to PDF\", \"print version\", \"PDF for the coaches\", \"make this printable\", or a coach-facing HTML deliverable needs a shareable/printable copy. Do not use to build the report or deck itself (use status-report or five-slides-generator).\n- impeccable: Use when the user wants to design, redesign, shape, critique, audit, polish, clarify, distill, harden, optimize, adapt, animate, colorize, extract, or otherwise improve a frontend interface. Covers websites, landing pages, dashboards, product UI, app shells, components, forms, settings, onboarding, and empty states. Handles UX review, visual hierarchy, information architecture, cognitive load, accessibility, performance, responsive behavior, theming, anti-patterns, typography, fonts, spacing, layout, alignment, color, motion, micro-interactions, UX copy, error states, edge cases, i18n, and reusable design systems or tokens. Also use for bland designs that need to become bolder or more delightful, loud designs that should become quieter, live browser iteration on UI elements, or ambitious visual effects that should feel technically extraordinary. Not for backend-only or non-UI tasks.\n- improve-system: Audit the SecondBrain vault for rot — stale seed notes, conflicting claims, and duplicate notes — and propose merge/archive/update actions. Use when the user says \"/improve-system\", \"audit my notes\", \"audit the vault\", \"find stale/duplicate notes\", or \"clean up my second brain\". Do not use for mining sessions for missed learnings (use session-miner), capturing a win or lesson (use capture-insight), improving a skill (use write-a-skill), or scoring the second brain as a system (use brain-audit).\n- ingest-book: Ingest a book (PDF/EPUB full-text, Kindle highlights, or framework-only from a physical book) into the SecondBrain library as atomized, citable advisor entries. Use when the user wants to \"ingest a book\", \"add a book to my library/advisors\", \"/ingest-book\", or points at a book file for the advisor system. Do not use for articles, transcripts, or general resources (use ingest-resource).\n- ingest-resource: Ingest any article, YouTube link, transcript, PDF, or note into the SecondBrain vault — routes it to Knowledge/ (stable info) or Projects/ (active work), adds a summary block, and cross-links related notes with [[wikilinks]]. Use when the user shares a URL, file, or pasted content and says \"ingest\", \"save this\", \"add to my vault/second brain\", \"/ingest-resource\", or drops a resource with intent to keep it. Do not use for a whole book (use ingest-book) or for a plain fact to remember (use memory-write).\n- interview-me: End-of-workday structured interview in two parts — a 90-second supervisor log of operational facts (equipment, people, blockers) that feeds the biweekly status report, then four reflection questions that distill the day into atomic permanent notes for the SecondBrain Obsidian vault. NOT a journaling app — produces a fact log and curated synthesis notes, not stream-of-consciousness. Use when the user types `/interview-me`, says \"interview me\", \"let's debrief\", \"end of day interview\", \"process my day\", \"wrap up today\", \"log my day\", or otherwise asks to capture their workday for their second brain or for their supervisor update. NOT FOR building the biweekly report itself (use status-report).\n- ISA: Owns the Ideal State Artifact — universal primitive holding articulated ideal state of any project, task, application, library, or work session as a hard-to-vary explanation. Six workflows: Scaffold (fresh ISA at effort tier), Interview (adaptive Q&A), CheckCompleteness (score against tier gate), Reconcile (merge ephemeral feature-file excerpt back to master, keyed on stable ISC IDs), Seed (bootstrap from repo README/code/commits), Append (record Decisions/Changelog/Verification entries). Locked twelve-section order: Problem, Vision, Out of Scope, Principles, Constraints, Goal, Criteria, Test Strategy, Features, Decisions, Changelog, Verification. Auto-invoked by Algorithm at OBSERVE. USE WHEN ISA, ISC, ideal state, ideal state criteria, project specification, hill-climb, articulating done. NOT FOR creating new skills (use CreateSkill).\n- library-advisor: Consult a grounded domain advisor built from the curated book library; answers only from ingested entries and cites every claim. Use when the user says \"ask my <domain> advisor\", \"/library-advisor <domain> <question>\", \"what would my library say about…\", \"consult my library advisor\", or consults a standing advisor. Domains live in SecondBrain/Knowledge/library/advisors/. NOT FOR the built-in /advisor reviewer command. Do not use to review a domain for gaps or missing coverage, as in \"review my library\" (use library-review).\n- library-review\n- meeting-prep: Pre-meeting readiness brief for a project — what changed, what's open, every deliverable path. Use when the user says \"/meeting-prep <project>\", \"I have a meeting with <person> — are we in a good spot on <project>?\", \"verify we're up to speed before the meeting\", \"get me ready for this afternoon's meeting\", or names an upcoming coach/staff meeting. Do not use for the biweekly supervisor report (use status-report) or to sync meeting transcripts (use meeting-sync).\n- meeting-sync: Sync new Fireflies transcripts into the SecondBrain vault; audit vault filenames for drift against Fireflies titles. Idempotent. Use when the user says \"sync meetings\", \"pull latest meetings\", \"/meeting-sync\", \"update meeting notes\", \"any new meetings?\", or when invoked by weekly-review. Also for filename drift — \"are any meeting files misnamed?\", \"did a meeting get renamed after export?\", \"why is this meeting titled differently in Fireflies than in the vault?\", \"audit the meeting filenames\". Do not use to rename generic titles inside Fireflies (use name-meetings) or to search what was said in transcripts (use pp-fireflies).\n- memory-write: Writes, updates, removes, and recalls facts in the two-tier memory system (~/.claude/global-memory/ and ~/.claude/projects/<slug>/memory/). Use for write requests such as \"remember this\", \"note that\", \"update memory\", \"save this\", and \"forget about\", and for recall requests such as \"did we already…?\", \"what did we decide about X?\", and \"do you remember…?\". Picks the tier, dedups, and enforces caps; recall checks the injected snapshot, then memsearch. Do not use for a story or lesson worth a vault note (use capture-insight) or an article to keep (use ingest-resource).\n- name-meetings: Detects Fireflies meetings with generic titles (empty, \"Zoom Meeting\", \"Recording\", date-only, etc.), proposes structured names based on AI summary + participants, and applies approved renames via the Fireflies API. Use when user says \"/name-meetings\", \"name my meetings\", \"rename untitled meetings\", \"fix meeting titles\", \"my meetings have no names\", or when invoked by meeting-sync. Do not use to sync transcripts or audit vault filenames (use meeting-sync).\n- new-project\n- next-decision\n- pbir-report-builder\n- persistent-localhost\n- pp-catapult-openfield-connect\n- pp-espn\n- pp-fireflies\n- pp-zotero-research-library: Your entire Zotero research library, searchable offline — version-based incremental sync into SQLite with full-text search no other Zotero tool has. Use when the user says `search my Zotero library`, `what does my library say about`, `find papers about`, `sync my Zotero library`, `what did I add to Zotero this week`, `get the citation for`, `use zotero-research-library`, `run zotero cli`. Do not use for a substantive sport-science question that needs a grounded, cited answer (use sports-science-research-grounding).\n- project: Manage the portfolio project registry (ideas, updates, ranking, promotion, dev stages) and render/publish the dashboard. Use when the user says \"/project\", \"new idea:\", \"log progress on\", \"project update\", \"add to my project inbox\", \"show my projects\", \"re-rank my projects\", \"set the stage on\", \"mark <project> as spec'd/planned/shipped\". Do not use for the DEV drift dashboard (use project-triage) or to scaffold a new repo (use new-project).\n- project-triage: Generate the DEV/ project triage HTML dashboard for today or a backfill date, surfacing drift between curated and current state. Use when the user says \"project triage\", \"/project-triage\", \"run my project review\", \"dev dashboard\", \"what's open in DEV\", \"morning review\", or asks to see the open-projects HTML. Do not use to log ideas or progress in the portfolio registry (use project) or for one project's meeting brief (use meeting-prep).\n- Science\n- scroll-craft: Build a premium, scroll-driven interactive landing page for any business: a service company, a physical product, a food brand, a drink brand. Scroll becomes the timeline. Video scrubs frame by frame under the wheel, sections pin and advance, rails pan sideways, headlines assemble line by line, the page ground shifts colour as you travel, and the pointer moves things that are not scrolling. Interviews the human first (their vibe, their journey, one unbroken world or distinct scenes, and what assets they already own), then picks a page grammar and a signature move so no two builds share a skeleton, generates photoreal assets through kie.ai or builds from the user's own footage and photos, writes real semantic HTML on a design-system floor, and verifies the result by screenshotting its own scroll. Use for \"scrollytelling\", \"scroll animation site\", \"a site where scrolling plays a video\", \"Apple-style landing page\", \"3D scroll world\", \"interactive landing page\", \"make my brand a scroll experience\", \"make it feel different\", \"this looks like a template\", \"a unique scroll site\", or any request for a site that should feel like an experience rather than a document.\n- session-miner\n- sports-science-research-grounding: Use when the user asks a substantive question about sport science, return to play or sport, rehabilitation, strength and conditioning, workload, recovery, testing, athlete health, injury, performance, or wants a research-backed answer, and for Zotero full-library batch processing. Searches the user's Zotero library first, clearly labels external peer-reviewed fallback sources, and applies tier-aware model routing during bounded batch work. Do not use for sports scores or schedules (use pp-espn) or for a simple Zotero search or sync (use pp-zotero-research-library).\n- startup: Start of day, or sitting down at either machine: sync this machine against GitHub, then report where the last session left off and wait. Use when Eric says '/startup', 'good morning', 'starting up', 'where did I leave off', 'what was I working on', 'picking this back up', or opens a session without naming a task. Do not use for the end-of-session sign-off (use closeout).\n- status-report: Build the every-two-weeks Applied Performance status report for Kenny Boyd and Chris Ruf — paste-ready email, one-page PDF, internal record. Use when the user says \"/status-report\", \"build my status report\", \"time for the Chris report\", \"biweekly update\", \"status update for Chris and Kenny\", \"what do I send Chris this week\", or when a scheduled report date arrives. Do not use for a single meeting brief (use meeting-prep) or the end-of-day supervisor log interview (use interview-me).\n- weekly-review\n- codex:rescue: Delegate investigation, an explicit fix request, or follow-up rescue work to the Codex rescue subagent\n- codex:setup: Check whether the local Codex CLI is ready and optionally toggle the stop-time review gate\n- superpowers:brainstorming: You MUST use this before any creative work - creating features, building components, adding functionality, or modifying behavior. Explores user intent, requirements and design before implementation.\n- superpowers:dispatching-parallel-agents\n- superpowers:executing-plans: Use when you have a written implementation plan to execute in a separate session with review checkpoints\n- superpowers:finishing-a-development-branch: Use when implementation is complete, all tests pass, and you need to decide how to integrate the work\n- superpowers:receiving-code-review\n- superpowers:requesting-code-review\n- superpowers:subagent-driven-development: Use when executing implementation plans with independent tasks in the current session\n- superpowers:systematic-debugging\n- superpowers:test-driven-development: Use when implementing any feature or bugfix, before writing implementation code\n- superpowers:using-git-worktrees: Use when starting feature work that needs isolation from current workspace or before executing implementation plans - ensures an isolated workspace exists via native tools or git worktree fallback\n- superpowers:using-superpowers\n- superpowers:verification-before-completion: Use when about to claim work is complete, fixed, or passing, before committing or creating PRs - requires running verification commands and confirming output before making any success claims; evidence before assertions always\n- superpowers:writing-plans: Use when you have a spec or requirements for a multi-step task, before touching code\n- superpowers:writing-skills\n- last30days:last30days\n- context-mode:context-mode\n- context-mode:ctx-doctor\n- context-mode:ctx-index\n- context-mode:ctx-insight\n- context-mode:ctx-purge\n- context-mode:ctx-search\n- context-mode:ctx-stats\n- context-mode:ctx-upgrade\n- codex:codex-cli-runtime: Internal helper contract for calling the codex-companion runtime from Claude Code\n- codex:codex-result-handling\n- codex:gpt-5-4-prompting\n- mattpocock-skills:diagnosing-bugs\n- mattpocock-skills:tdd\n- mattpocock-skills:prototype: Build a throwaway prototype to answer a design question. Use when the user wants to sanity-check whether a state model or logic feels right, or explore what a UI should look like.\n- mattpocock-skills:research\n- mattpocock-skills:domain-modeling\n- mattpocock-skills:codebase-design\n- mattpocock-skills:code-review\n- mattpocock-skills:resolving-merge-conflicts\n- mattpocock-skills:wizard\n- mattpocock-skills:grilling: Grill the user relentlessly about a plan, decision, or idea. Use when the user wants to stress-test their thinking, or uses any 'grill' trigger phrases.\n- mattpocock-skills:writing-for-agents\n- watch:watch: Watch a video (URL or local path). Downloads with yt-dlp, extracts auto-scaled frames with ffmpeg, pulls the transcript from captions (or Whisper API fallback), and hands the result to Claude so it can answer questions about what's in the video.\n- dataviz: Use this skill whenever you are about to create ANY chart, graph, plot, dashboard, or data visualization, in ANY output medium — an HTML or React artifact, inline SVG, plotting code in any library (matplotlib, plotly, d3, Recharts, …), an image/PNG you will render and upload, or a chart shared into Slack. Read it BEFORE writing the first line of chart code, choosing chart colors, building a stat tile / meter / KPI row, or laying out a dashboard. Produces visualizations that read as one system — elegant, accessible, consistent in light and dark — using a brand-neutral placeholder palette you swap for your own. Teaches a design-system-agnostic method: a form heuristic, a color formula with a runnable validator, mark specs, and interaction rules. A validated default palette is documented in `references/palette.md` — swap that file's values for your brand's. Triggers on: \"chart\", \"graph\", \"plot\", \"data viz\", \"visualization\", \"dashboard\", \"analytics\", \"visualize data\", \"categorical colors\", \"sequential / diverging palette\", \"stat tile\", \"sparkline\", \"heatmap\", \"legend\", \"axis\", \"tooltip\", \"chart colors\", \"color by series\".\n- update-config: Use this skill to configure the Claude Code harness via settings.json. Automated behaviors (\"from now on when X\", \"each time X\", \"whenever X\", \"before/after X\") require hooks configured in settings.json - the harness executes these, not Claude, so memory/preferences cannot fulfill them. Also use for: permissions (\"allow X\", \"add permission\", \"move permission to\"), env vars (\"set X=Y\"), hook troubleshooting, or any changes to settings.json/settings.local.json files. Examples: \"allow npm commands\", \"add bq permission to global settings\", \"move permission to user settings\", \"set DEBUG=true\", \"when claude stops show X\". For simple settings like theme/model, suggest the /config command.\n- keybindings-help: Use when the user wants to customize keyboard shortcuts, rebind keys, add chord bindings, or modify ~/.claude/keybindings.json. Examples: \"rebind ctrl+s\", \"add a chord shortcut\", \"change the submit key\", \"customize keybindings\".\n- simplify: Review the changed code for reuse, simplification, efficiency, and altitude cleanups, then apply the fixes. Quality only — it does not hunt for bugs; use /code-review for that.\n- fewer-permission-prompts: Scan your transcripts for common read-only Bash and MCP tool calls, then add a prioritized allowlist to project .claude/settings.json to reduce permission prompts.\n- loop: Run a prompt or slash command on a recurring interval (e.g. /loop 5m /foo). Omit the interval to let the model self-pace. - When the user wants to set up a recurring task, poll for status, or run something repeatedly on an interval (e.g. \"check the deploy every 5 minutes\", \"keep running /babysit-prs\"). Do NOT invoke for one-off tasks.\n- schedule: Create, update, list, or run scheduled cloud agents (routines) that execute on a cron schedule. - When the user wants to schedule a recurring cloud agent, set up automated tasks, create a cron job for Claude Code, or manage their scheduled agents/routines. Also use when the user wants a one-time scheduled run (\"run this once at 3pm\", \"remind me to check X tomorrow\").\n- claude-api: Reference for the Claude API / Anthropic SDK — model ids, pricing, params, streaming, tool use, MCP, agents, caching, token counting, model migration.\nTRIGGER — read BEFORE opening the target file; don't skip because it \"looks like a one-liner\" — whenever: the prompt names Claude/Anthropic in any form (Claude, Anthropic, Fable, Opus, Sonnet, Haiku, `anthropic`, `@anthropic-ai`, `claude-*`, `us.anthropic.*`, `[1m]`); the user asks about an LLM (pricing/model choice/limits/caching) — never answer from memory; OR the task is LLM-shaped with provider unstated (agent/MCP/tool-definition/multi-agent/RAG/LLM-judge/computer-use; generate/summarize/extract/classify/rewrite/converse over NL; debugging refusals/cutoffs/streaming/tool-calls/tokens).\nSKIP only when another provider is being worked on (overrides all triggers): OpenAI/GPT/Gemini/Llama/Mistral/Cohere/Ollama named in the query; OR `grep -rE 'openai|langchain_openai|google.generativeai|genai|mistralai|cohere|ollama'` over the project hits (run this grep FIRST if no provider named — don't Read the file).\n- run: Launch and drive this project's app to see a change working. Use when asked to run, start, or screenshot the app, or to confirm a change works in the real app (not just tests). First looks for a project skill that already covers launching the app; otherwise falls back to built-in patterns per project type (CLI, server, TUI, Electron, browser-driven, library).\n- init: Initialize a new CLAUDE.md file with codebase documentation\n- security-review","skillCount":104,"isInitial":true,"names":["anti-sleep","ArXiv","backup","brain-audit","capture-insight","claude-stack-doctor","claudex-loop","claudex-route","cli-hub-meta-skill","closeout","code-review","codex-build","codex-review","CreateSkill","design-taste-frontend","dev-sync","doctor-plus","fireflies-intake","five-slides-generator","forecast-capture","game-film-timestamps","graphify","harvest","html-to-pdf","impeccable","improve-system","ingest-book","ingest-resource","interview-me","ISA","library-advisor","library-review","meeting-prep","meeting-sync","memory-write","name-meetings","new-project","next-decision","pbir-report-builder","persistent-localhost","pp-catapult-openfield-connect","pp-espn","pp-fireflies","pp-zotero-research-library","project","project-triage","Science","scroll-craft","session-miner","sports-science-research-grounding","startup","status-report","weekly-review","codex:rescue","codex:setup","superpowers:brainstorming","superpowers:dispatching-parallel-agents","superpowers:executing-plans","superpowers:finishing-a-development-branch","superpowers:receiving-code-review","superpowers:requesting-code-review","superpowers:subagent-driven-development","superpowers:systematic-debugging","superpowers:test-driven-development","superpowers:using-git-worktrees","superpowers:using-superpowers","superpowers:verification-before-completion","superpowers:writing-plans","superpowers:writing-skills","last30days:last30days","context-mode:context-mode","context-mode:ctx-doctor","context-mode:ctx-index","context-mode:ctx-insight","context-mode:ctx-purge","context-mode:ctx-search","context-mode:ctx-stats","context-mode:ctx-upgrade","codex:codex-cli-runtime","codex:codex-result-handling","codex:gpt-5-4-prompting","mattpocock-skills:diagnosing-bugs","mattpocock-skills:tdd","mattpocock-skills:prototype","mattpocock-skills:research","mattpocock-skills:domain-modeling","mattpocock-skills:codebase-design","mattpocock-skills:code-review","mattpocock-skills:resolving-merge-conflicts","mattpocock-skills:wizard","mattpocock-skills:grilling","mattpocock-skills:writing-for-agents","watch:watch","dataviz","update-config","keybindings-help","simplify","fewer-permission-prompts","loop","schedule","claude-api","run","init","security-review"]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"8cb3e65c-826a-4136-9cc1-7a41d8b63f13","timestamp":"2026-09-13T07:32:13.354Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/global-memory","sessionId":"c922703c-e012-455a-8f07-d26099b07dc4","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"main"}
